      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>DRINK</head>
        <p>DRINK</p>
        <p>Sir,—Can a petition, be drawn up and sent to Mr. Harold Smith, kI.P., asking Parliament to pass a law forbidding women to leave young children outside public-houses? I have .seen little :children blue with cold sitting in carriages Outside public-houses, It is a shame. We are talking of saving infants but are goiug the right way to kill them when they are left outside public-houses in the bitter cold. These women who drink .ought not to make their children suffer, for - their " pleasure." We have very foolish laws on the drink ques- tion.. Let us try to save the children by „pre- . venting them from coffering because of drunken mothers as in the past. A LOVER .OF LITTLE CIIILDREN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>DRUNKENNESS AND DRINKING</head>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS AND DRINKING</p>
        <p>FACILITIES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l can quite understand your corre- spondent, Mr. B. T. Parkes, being astonished at my attack on the teetotal superstition that "reduced facilities &amp;icreoye drurk " I know this theory appears very plausible at first sight, but it does' not square with the facts. What happens when drinking facilities are reduced is that the moderate drinker is inconvenienced needlessly, while the immo- derate drinker is nowise improved, as he quickly finds a way to circumvent the restric- tion. Also these measures .promote home , drinking, and natitralli favour the consump- tion of spirits in place of ale and stout. When the policy of restriction is pushed to its logical conclusion, and we have complete pro- hibition,. do we find drunkenness has vanished? No; .we, find secret drinking in its lowest and most bestial form flourishing everywhere.. We ". also find a tendency for the accursed drug habit to develop, and this 'Vice, Mr. Parkes will agree, is even more insidious and more deadly than the vice of drunkenness: The Prohibition -States, of America are a striking warning to this country to beware of pursuing the teetotal restriction policy too far. J. E. SIA.IPSON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WHAT OUR HEADERS WANTED.</head>
        <p>WHAT OUR HEADERS WANTED.</p>
        <p>What our readere wanted was proof that cures were lasting. And this is exactly what is given here, in the word of one of our neigh- bours in Warrington. On January 6th, 1913, Mr. J. Lakin, of 16, Iland-street, Warrington, said :—"I work in a hot place, and coming out into the cold air was the -cause of my kidney trouble. I was at- tacked by acute pains in the region of the kidneys, which seemed to drag me down. As my work is a strain on the back these pains made it difficult for me. I " 10.zy and in the mornings felt weary and tired. "The' water WM in an unnatural state, 9,nd I kipew from this my tried Doan's Backache Kidney Pills for this complaint, and proved them entirely satis- factory. After taking the pills the water be- came clear again and my back was free from pain. Since then I have usually kept a box in the house and use them if I feel any signs of the old trouble. (Signed) J. Lakin." On March 15th, 1915—over two years later— Mr. Lakin said :—" I have kept well since I used Doan's Pills. They cured me splendidly." If !ion have any snch clear signs of kidney and bladder' disorders ,as backache, urinary troubles, gravel, dropsy, lumbago or 'rheuma- tism, persevere with Doan's Backache Kidney Pills until every trace of the ,dread kidney disease is gone. Doan's Pills assist the urinary „system like a laxative assists the bowels. Price 2.19 a box, of all dealers, or from Foster-McClellan Co.. 8. W:11s-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for back- ache or kidney pills—ask dietinctly for Doan'.% Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mr. Lakin had:.</p>
      </div>
