      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>How The Guild Of Help Directs The Flow Of Charity.</head>
        <p>How The Guild Of Help Directs The Flow Of Charity.</p>
        <p>CARE OF MOTHERS AND BABIES.</p>
        <p>The large attendance and sustained interest 'shown at the annual meeting of the Civic Guild 'l3f held at the Town Hall on Wednesday, "gur well for the future of this excellent 'tnsganisation, which has and is doing so much 4ireet benevolence to the people who really 'Aeselved it to the .exclusion of the professional "dger.</p>
        <p>L'lcellent addresses were given by Prebendary ')arsons, principal of Wells Theological College, And Mrs. Lawton, of Stoke-on-Trent, who dealt With two., aspects of the question of the applica- lion of charity through the-individo.l or by an organisation. His Worship the Mayor pre- sided, but having another engagement, he was -compelled to leave early, and' the subsequent proceedings were presided over by Mr.. Charles E. Parker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>MAYOR APPfOVES GOOD WORK.</head>
        <p>MAYOR APPfOVES GOOD WORK.</p>
        <p>The Mayor pointed out that whilst there was 'net the same distress in the town. as in past Years, there were cases whieh ,required the con- Attuat care and attention :of so worthy an organi- sation. The Town Council and other institu- tions were doing a good work, but there were 'numerous• cases which a Guild of -that kind Alone could deal with *through its voluntary effort and kindly asist,anee given by its -mem- l'ers.-v,ln regard'- to the treatinent of cages of consumption the 'Guild had rendered very ..valu- le help ,in the loan of beds- and bedding for Patients .who required, to be isolated in their homes. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>tXPLOITING PUBLIC SYMPATHY</head>
        <p>tXPLOITING PUBLIC SYMPATHY</p>
        <p>In the annual report the General Secretary (Mr. W• J. Rowland) -drew attention to the exploita- tion of public gratitude to our soldiers. One man in the prime of life known to the Guild be- fore the War, as a tramp taking wife and chit- dren from town to town, got into the Army, but was discharged in less than a month. Though -well. able to work, he was •successfully begging. The Guild not .only refused .help, but was able to forewarn in .certain- directions where .he was likely to sponge' - The. following is an analysis of new cases re- ported during 1915:—Helped.with food, clothes„„ Work; medical comforts, 4c„ 89; helped ly advice and friendly visitation,. 70; referred to local authorities or. other societies, 23; .no .help needed, , 8; false addressewor gone away, 3; 'help refused to unsatisfactory cases, 9. -grhe Guild was often spoken of as a clearing house, which meant that eases were investigated and brought into touch 'witluthe right source of Every. year public officials were brought into closer contact with poor homes, but their Powers were strictly limited. When such officials discovered a need which they were not able to meet, it w satisfaction tq have at hand a society like the Guild of Help, which was being increasingly used.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Insurance Act Administration.</head>
        <p>Insurance Act Administration.</p>
        <p>The 'complicated Insurance Acts haul worked With remarkable -smoothriesA 'on the whole, but there had been-some cases of unjustifiable delay in the payment of .benefit. The Guild had in- euced the societies .concerned to make advances vending a settlement of the dispute. The secre- t!-ry of the Guild also brought this difficulty to the notice of the Insurance Committee. The Insurance -Commissioners had since issued a eiretilar requesting —approved societies under iiitailar circumstances to make advances. Other remedial work had also been done. 1:114ler Present conditions only a certain number Of tuberculous patients could be treated in a "anatorium. For. those who remained at home it was very necessary that further infection should be checked. The provision of a separate bed for the patient was often highly necessary. The small experiment made by' the Guild in 1914: to lend single beds was se greatly .lippreeiated by the Health Authority that the executive determined to extend it. A special fund was therefore raised for the purchase of five additional , beds complete with bedding. Eight beds are now in constant use, the last five of which were first put intb use in May. Six- teen patients benefited from thiS scheme in 1915</p>
        <p>Direct Help. Although the following table is not complete, it Sets: forth some of the material help given directly by the Guild. The 'figures. of course, do not include the large amount of help pro- cured through the instrumentality of the Guild from co-operating charities, including parochial and other funds. Clothing and bedding, 42; neces4Bl7 food, 31; milk, 19; pourishthent for invalids, 13; con= -valescent homes' or special hospitals, 3; railway fares, 4; financial help in loans, debts, rent, &amp;c., or coals, 17; surgical or medical aid, 4; work found, IR.</p>
        <p>Metnek' and Babies' Welcomes. •-. The WelcoMes situated in Cairo-street and 'Catherine-street have had a very satisfactory Year. The number of attendances 4)f ntathers and children at both places totalling. 2,753. Babies had been weighed and health talks given, and a further advantage was the -medical inspec- tion by Dr. G. W. N. Joseph (the medical officer of health). It was gratifying to those respon- sible for carrying on the Welcomes to have re- -I.Tived from,the Health Committee {,f the Coun- cil public testimony as to the value of their Work.</p>
        <p>In a report of the Local Government Board it was shown that during recent years, out of the seventeen County Boroughs in Lancashire only one (SoutltTiort) had made greater strides than Warrington in reducing the rate of infant Innrtality.</p>
        <p>Even to havn a small share in helping. our ilealth Authority to save child life i. sufficient ?eeompense for devoting leisure to these Wel- comes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>FINANCES.</head>
        <p>THE FINANCES.</p>
        <p>ThO Tian. 'Treasurer (Mr: J. If. Riln,;othe), in submitting his annual financial statement. stated that the donations, which amounted to. £O2 3s. Bd.. showed .a slight decrease. but on the Other hand he was able tc report a slight in- erease. in the amount of snhscriptions which totalled for the year £220 3s: 6d. Their totql Teeeints amounted to (-'272 Ss. 4d., and their ex nenditure £317. 13s. 6d., leaving an..overdraft•at •the bank of £45 ss. •od. Realising the difficulty Of -raising- their usual donations, the secretaty very wisely had cut down the expendilltre. and their expenses on the. year were aboi.t i 1 I leEs tt an the previous tar,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>STATE AND THE INDIVIDUAL IN</head>
        <p>THE STATE AND THE INDIVIDUAL IN</p>
        <p>CHARITY.</p>
        <p>Prebendary Parsons, in moving- the adoption the reports, Spoke on the reasons why he as ready to support such. an organisation as a citizen, and a clergyman. As a citizen in.- terested in the progres6 of social legislation he Said they were passing through a period of very</p>
        <p>• ; , • it: e THE BO'D</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>marked change. The effect of a number of im- portant Acts of Parliament during the last ten years had been all in one direction; that of lessening the field of direct private benevolence. They must see to it that charity did not dis- appear from the personal life .of the ordinary citizen. Legislation dealing with social sub- jects would go on, and the State would take under its care one thing after another which hitherto had been left to 'the charity of the in- dividual. There would -be a greater need as time went on of bringing into play organisations which would give the individual his or her chance of directing charitable work. It was just that need which an institution like the Quild of Help was going to supply, to enable them personally to take their part in doing the State's work of 'charity. Signs Of the times showed that there was more - and more a need for organising personal service. Charity to-day often meant giving sixpence to. some person who might, or might not deserve it, and prob- ably very little better off after receiving it. The 'charity of the 'future must be organised charity, and must always have clearly in front of it an ideal which was not merely the giving out of doles and sops, but doing what was really best for one's neighbour. The Church, like the . State.: was . passing through a time of great change and revolution. By. the Church he did not mean only the Church of • England, but the Church in the widest sense of the word. One real difficulty of the Church's work, not only in the Church of England, was that the, parson was employed an work -which he ought not to be expected. to do. The air would be cleared .if something could be done to separate entirely a clergyman's work in his parish from his personal manage- ment of the charities of that parish. There were great disadvantages connected with the present system. To a great many people who did not go to church 'a clergyman was •looked upon as a person out of whom they could get .something, • Don't Stifle The Guild Work.</p>
        <p>The inauguration of a society like the .Guild of Help was a practical step forward towards the unity of the forces of religion, which was the greatest need of our time. .</p>
        <p>"I feel," continued the speaker, "that what the Mayor has said about the need of carrying on filo work right through the war is very true indeed. You have only to read the reports to feel convinced that there is no reason what- ever why your efforts to support the Guild should be stifled at all during the war. The interim work it is doing is important, and it is even more important that experience should be gathered, and that a forecast should be made in reference .to the great social economy and moral readjustment which is inevitable as soon as the war is over." (Applause.) Ire was convinced the Guild of Help was one of the agencies which was going to lift up the social and religious life of the community to a level• which the needs of our time required. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>PERSONAL TOUCH.</head>
        <p>THE PERSONAL TOUCH.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Parker, referring to the question of individual charity and the scheme of organised charity, said that they who were associated with the Guild claimed to combine the two, inas- much as the Guild visitor, was given the oppor- tunity of bringing into the work that personal love and personal touch which an organisation alone sometimes tended to kill.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN THE GREAT NATIONAL ASSET.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Lawton seconded the adoption of the reports, and confined her address to the ques- tion of the wastage of infant life. She said that at the present time appeals were being made to the people to practise thrift and to abolish waste. There was no greater or more vital wastage going on to-day than the wastage of children. The cream of the men were fighting, and a good many would not come back. In a few years the cry, would be for men, and there would be none, because they were allowing chil- dren to be killed. At present there was a wastage of Child life of over 200,000 per year, more than had been killed in the British Army in a whole year of battle. With the declining birth-rate, this was a serious outlook for the country unless every effort was made to save the life of the young children. Children Who Have No Chance.</p>
        <p>There were many children who had no chance in life, , because their mothers had had none. They must save the children by dealing with the mothers first. Another great waste was the waste of suffering on the part of mothers who brought children into the world to lose them. • The three great causes which killed so many children were poverty, ignorance and inherited diseases, and of the three, ignorance was the greatest. The remedy was in education. If they conic only get hold of the women and persuade them to attend Welcomes and be educated they would be able to stamp out this dreadful infant mor- tality. It was very disheartening work to these connected with the movement. The women would not come along, and there was a tre. mendons amount of prejudice, to break down. She did not consider the Baby Welcome was work entirely for voluntary effort. .The time had arriv&amp;l when the, countty should undertake it with the help of the voluntary workers, but it must be put under the control of the town ; authorities. The town must employ and pay ; people for that special work. There would be ; plenty of work co-ordinating with it for the voluntary worker. Children were a national tiset, and it therefore ought to be a national effort (Applause.) IMPRACTICAL POLITICIANS. Mr. W. P. Rylands, in proposing a compre- hensive vote of thanks, remarked that the guild was not founded for the purpose of stimulating charity, but to direct it in the proper channels, Without such an organisation the most plausible 'cadger; possibly the least deserving, succeeded in extracting the greatest amount -from the charitably disposed. That was the very antithesis of what they desired. That organisa- tion did not reckon to be an agency for the distribution of money among the impecunious, but it was intended that the people receivink help should be deserving of it, anti to :lic,coorag. others who did not euctrve it. FTe believei :t to be a fact amongst the poorer classes that the most deserving people said the least. If the State could look after such a question with- out any suggestion of charity, but simply as a matter of practical value, the sooner they got to work the better. Legislators never concerned themselves with anything of practical utility. ; If anything was practical the last thing ex- pected of them was their interest in 'the matter. Perhaps in the days to come things'might change, and- that and many other real prac- tical subjects might receive attention. The Rev: Irvine Lister seconded, and emphasised the necessity Of individual responsi- bility for the welfare of the community, and ' trying to. help men and women to a better life and better economic conditions.</p>
        <p>A Splendid Father. Coster go Parish Visitor, who has been com- miserating with him on the loss of his father): "Yes, mum, 'e were a splendid father to us and no. mistake. Yer see, mum, there was eleven of us, and I never knowed 'im to raise 'is 'and to one of us—'eept as it might be in self-defence."—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>• 0r5t,..,,.L• WORLD . tt:l e 0</head>
        <p>• 0r5t,..,,.L• WORLD . tt:l e 0</p>
        <p>clerical staff of the Great Western Railwa3l/4 They- are taking the places of. men who have joined the Army, and are doing their work quite as well as the men. The Great Central Railway has nearly two thousand women in its employ, and one woman is a supervisor of the line. On the Great Eastern Railway the sister of an employe has learnt how to manage the levers at a level crossing, and will take her brother's place until the war is over. School Girls' War Work.</p>
        <p>The work for war funds, &amp;c., done by children during the last eighteen months has been astonishing. Schoolboys and girls of all classes 'have spontaneously made many sacrifices in the matter of pleasures, which before the war they regarded as their right, and have devoted their holidays and spare time to good works of many kinds. The record of the activities of the Girls' Public Day Schools in Liverpool would indeed be difficult to beat. The girls have collected and earned enough money by means of sales of work, entertainments, Itnd the profits on their gardens, to finance a Belgian hostel for refugees, which they manage themselves. They adopt prisoners of war, and send each welcome parcels at regular intervals. One school adopted the whole crew of a torpedo destroyer. The girls have received frequent requests for thousands of swabs, hospital bags, bandages', and other surgical appliances, and all have been made within forty-eight hours, The work of the girls is admirably organised, and there 'is absolutely no waste of time or energy. • The New Blouses.</p>
        <p>Blouses are as alluring as ever, and even more indispensable. In transparencies their name is legion, but there are also a goodly number in taffetas and soft satin. Some *few models in the former material are decorattd with gold, and oxidised thread worked by hand to form a kind of embroidery that somewhat resembles darn-</p>
        <p>ing. Lozenge shaped, and wheel-motifs form many of the designs for this novel handwork, but machinery is employed when a raised effect is desired. A novelty is a patterned crepe de chine, with designs of flowers, apples, pears and citfons on a white ground. Both flowers and fruit are small, and as they are artistically treated, the effect is pleasing. For indoors and theatre wear, the blouse is of the same colour as the skirt. An economical arrangement, whereby monotony is avoided, has been devised by a clever dressmaker, who advises three tops to one demi toilette skirt. The first is of the same material, the second of a transparency in contrasting colours, and the third a plain shirt of white washing satin, with satin buttons to match the skirt. In each blouse the colour of the skirt was relwated in the trimmings.</p>
        <p>'The Princess Petticoat. The neck of the blouse is a subject. that has given rise to much discussion of late, but tre question remains still unsettled. That the high neck is smart cannot be denied, but like the dec6llete it demands a firm, round throat, which every woman does not possess. The problem will doubtless be solved by the adoption of the collar that is high at the back and open in front, for curiously enough, a very thin or a very fat neck looks best uncovered. A very pretty finish to the neck of a blouse may be obtained by means of a soft kerchief folded a-, the back to form a tiny hood, the cuffs corre- sponding. Petticoats are of infinite variety in shape and trimming. The combined petticoat and camisole, which is really the old-fashioned Princess, is one of the latest ideas. It is shown in washing satin, of a pale pink flesh tint for evening wear under transparent (bodices, and in black satin, trimmed with black lace, as well as in less expensive materials. It is a most com- fortable garment, hygienic too, and economical in• material as well as in the time required to take it off and put it on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Lady House Agents. The woman house agent has long been an accepted fact in Canada, where she buys and sells land, whilst women lawyers do the con- veyancing. In this country the idea is some- what novel, although the work is peculiarly adapted to. a woman's capacity, and experience. An agency has lately been started in the West End of London by ladies who undertake con- siderably more for their clients than do the agents of the opposite sex. The ladies person- ally inspect all houses and apartments on their lists, thus saving house hunters many unneces- sary journeys to ,unsuitable places.</p>
        <p>Dispensing for Women. There is at present a great demand for women dispensers and pharmacists, in hospitals, sur- geries, &amp;c. The prejudice against feminine competition in pharmacy, as in many other things, has been very hard to break down, but now trained women are taking the place of chemists called to the Colours. They are not. however, in sufficient numbers to till the vacancies. Much useful work has ben done by many trained women. zis dispensers to yariciN Voluntary Aid Detachment units. Some c` these are paid positions, but only those holding. an assistant's diploma are eligible. The work is interesting, and would doubtless attract many educated women if the dispensing of inedicines were, as on the Continent, divorced from the sale of soaps and scents. Women on the Railways.</p>
        <p>, No fewer than six women graduates of the University of London are employed. on the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>lIONIE NOTES.</head>
        <p>lIONIE NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes And Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Pancakes.—Beat the yolks of three eggs, add one cupful of milk, half a teaspoonful of salt and a teaspoonful of sugar. Four one-third of this mixture on half a cupful of flour and stir until smooth; then add the remainder of the prepared milk gradually and beat well; then add half a tablespoonful of melted butter, stir- ring it in gently. Butter a small frying-pan and pour in just enough of the mixture to cover the bottom. As soon as the under-side is brown, turn and lightly brown the other side; butter the last side browned. Spread with raspberry jelly, roll PP the cake, jelly side in, and keep hot ill a covered dish while you prepare a few more in the same way. Sprinkle powdered sugar over them just before sending to the table.</p>
        <p>Pancakes in the Making.—To make the per- fect pancake the batter should be allowed to stand for two or three hours in. • a cool place before it is needed. The whites of eggs, when beaten to a froth, should be added just before 'cooking, , All pan Cakes, except the very thick ones—for instance,- those made from maize flour—should</p>
        <p>be tossed, not turned over, and it is good fun for each member of the family to toss his own pancake. If this is to be done, have ready a small spirit stove, a ,small omelet pan, batter, oiled butter, and a piece of kitchen paper. A hot dish should be waiting to receive the pan- Cakes, and sugar, cut lemon, or preserve. The stove and ingredients can be set on a small table near, the dining table. Each 'person can then make his own first pancake, and the para- phernalia be removed in, a few moments.</p>
        <p>A hint •to the unpractised cook will not be unwelcome. When the pancake is ready to toss —that is, it is set round the edges—slip it to the side of the pan farthest from the handle, almost to the edge. Give an outward toss and the pancake will safely return.</p>
        <p>Pancake Day.—ln view of the near approach of Shrove Tuesday, the fol- lowing recipe for pancakes should be found useful :--" Take half a pound of flour, one teaspoonful of Borwick's Baking Powder and a little salt, mix well in a dry state,' acid one egg and three-quarters of a pint of milk. Fry quickly over a brisk fire and serve immediately." Pancakes made according to these directions will turn out beautifully light and appetising. Borwick's Baking Powder is economical to use, one teaspoonful to every half-pound of flour being quite uffieient to ensure the most deli- cious pancakes.</p>
        <p>Artichokes a la creme (French).—Choose large Jerusalem Artichokes of an equal size, peel them, blanch them in cold water for twenty minutes, then boil them in milk for ten minutes, when they must be drained and cut into moder- ately thin slices. Put a pint of the slices into stewpan, with a seasoning to taste of salt and white pepper; add nearly half a pint of cream, cover close, and simmer very gently until tender, great care being taken that the slices do not get broken. Garnish with ssippets of toast.</p>
        <p>Salsify en friture (French).—Dissolve 2oz. of butter in half a pint of warm milk, then mix into it very smoothly 10oz. of flour, and just before the batter is to be used mix in also the well-whisked whites of two eggs. Cut some Plainly boiled and well-drained Salsify into 3in. lengths, which dip in the batter, and fry crisp in boiling fat or oil. Sprinkle with Krona pepper, and serve piled high upon tt folded nap- kin.</p>
        <p>Eggs parmesan (Indian).—Butter a pie-dish, or individual dishes, sprinkle with browned breadcrumbs (these can be made from crusts and odd pieces of bread baked and crushed, and kept ready, for use in a tin). Break three or four eggs carefully ' into the dish, sprinkle with salt, pepper, and grated parmesan cheese and bread- crumbs, add small pieces of butter; and bake Until a nice brown. This may be _varied by put- ting a layer of sliced tomatoes under and over the eggs, sprinkling each layer with cheese.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>++4.**•••• ********• • • 4- Children' s corner.:</head>
        <p>++4.**•••• ********• • • 4- Children' s corner.:</p>
        <p>• Conducted by UNCLE JACK. - • • 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4, 4. •4. 4. 4. 4. 4.</p>
        <p>Children, Join This Corner: Enter The Competitions! Write Your Best! Always Be Glad! Uncle. Jack, " Examiner," Warrington.</p>
        <p>rHE FLOWERS I WOULD LIKE TO GROW.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,—l thought I should be making a difficult task for myself when I asked you 'to write about • growing flowers, and so it has proved. If ever I was sorry I could not give away prizes wholesale it is this week. The letters sent in have been good and interesting. All kinds cif flowers have been mentioned, from orchids to snowdrops, and mentioned in such a way as to show that you have a great love of the beautiful. It has, as you may imagine, been hard to say which essay deserves the prize, but I have decided to award it to Niece Edith Pugh, 44, Glazebrook-street, who, besides saying which flowers she would grow and why, wrote a neat letter without any mistake whatever. She only failed to state her number and her school but, fortunately, I was able to get this information without very much trouble by searching for her name in my big book.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>I wish Uncle Ned was back from the wars Perhaps he would be able to seltct some new suitable subject for competition among his nephews and `nieces. I have found subject after subject, until I am afraid I have nearly ex- hausted myself. There is little doubt that 'you will have plenty of ideas for a competition. You know what questions interest children—and it is so long since I went to school. The prize next Week will be for the best</p>
        <p>Suggestion of a Subject For Next Week's Children's Corner Competition.</p>
        <p>Now isn't that a good idea? To let you name your own kind of competition and give you a prize for doing so?</p>
        <p>ECONOMY IN ENVELOPES.</p>
        <p>T want to impress upon all my little people the need there is for economy in the use of all kinds of articles. Paper, as you know, is. now double its former price, and envelopes, which are made of Paper, are not only very, dear, but very scarce. The envelope makers seem to have gone to the war in a body and taken their machines with them. It is one of the most diffi- cult things to get envelopes from the makers. May I say, therefore, that essays and letters sent to Uncle Jack may be handed in or put in the letter box at "The Examiner" Office yithout being enclosed in an envelope? You will thus :be saving money for your parents and paper for the country. Your loving. - UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WINNING ESSAY.</head>
        <p>THE WINNING ESSAY.</p>
        <p>By 720, Niece Edith Pugh, 44, Glazebrook- street. Niece Edith is only twelve years of age, and attends Arpley-street School.</p>
        <p>The Flowers I Would Like To Grow.</p>
        <p>"Dear Uncle Jack,—We have only a small g.arden at the back of the house, but still it will grow such things as flowers. ThtS flowers I would like to grow are hyacinths, because they are of different colours, and they send off into the atmosphere such a lovely fragrance. Also the narcissus, tulips and daffodils. The nar- cissus is a very nice flower; it is white. Daffodils and tulips are all flowering bulbous plants. "I think flowers are beautiful. They often cheer somebody when sick, and we often take people them.</p>
        <p>" I remain„ "Your, loving niece, "EDITH PUGH."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</head>
        <p>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</p>
        <p>I give selections from 'many letters I have received to show the excellence -of the choice and the interest taken in the subject Niece.. Mary Roche, 6. Bnstock-street, tells me not only that she would like to grow, the par-</p>
        <p>eissus, but how that flower got its Mime:— " A man named Narcissus went for a walk ; down to a pool of. fresh and clear water from • the fountain. He was stooping down for a drink, and at once he saw his own image. Narcissus-thoulkht it was one of the fairy queens.• He bent day ;liner' day and night after night. looking at this image. Narcissus had nothing .to eat'. He became thin and faint,., and le 'died. A little flower sprung from .the place where ,Narcissus died.. The friends and his parents said that a fairy had changed him into a flower, so they called it narcissus. It is called the same name -to this very, day." Niece Lucy Lees, 51, Forshaw-street (Beamont) says :—" I like wallflowers because they smell so sweet, Daffodils and Virginia stock show a garden up and make it look very pretty. We- used to have -a garden, .and we grew pansies, mignonette and tulips. If I had &amp;garden of niv own I would grow ever so many flowers, because I like them." . • Niece Edna Healy, 4, George-street (Wycliffe), "If I had the garden there, are a great-many flowers I would, like to grew. The daffodil is a very gay flower, but it has not" any sweet per- fume. It is ,sometimes -called the .14ent lily be- cause it Makes its, appearance at Easter. The inext flower is a rose; it is a sweet scented flower. The climbing rose is very pretty, but is not sweet smelling. The next flower I have chosen is the "chrysanthemum. This has a head of which the petals are very curly and look very pretty. It can either be grown in a hot- house or out of doors. If. they are looked after when young they will grow very tall." Eva M. Morrell, 47, Price-street.—" If I had a garden, stock, with its various colours, would form the border on three sides, whilst sweet peas trained up ,against the wall would form the fdurth side. Finally., a selection of cornflowers and carnations planted hero and there, and in the centre a rose tree growing on a little mound, surrounded by geraniums, would be my dream of an ideal garden." Niece Edith Buck, 11, Walker-street.—" should prefer primroses and 'wallflowers, for they dolook very pretty together, and especially when they are in full bloom; and you can look upon them and know that your own care and devotion has.. helped to make them look so sweet." Niece Erica Bolton, 22, Marson-street.—" would like to grow tulips, Ives, daffodils, wall- flowers, lilies. snowdrops, forget-me-nots, crocuses. Roses are stately and. prominent, and besides are the pride, and the emblem of our country, England." Niece Dorothy E. Bennett, 42, Warburton- street, Stockton Heath (Arpley-street School).— " Growing in' my garden I would see snowdrops,, tulips, daffodils, violets and, later on in the year, tea roses and sweet peas, which are my favourite flowers. I woold weed them and water them in dry weather. How beautiful it is to see the flowers growing and eXpanding!" Niece Margaret Gleave, 64, Priestley-street, says she would have roses in her garden. "The parents- of these are the wild roses that deck the waysides in summer. Another lovely flower , is the, iris of many shades. Some of them are I striped and blotched with colour. Last of all are the beds of lilies, bordered with pansies, which perfume the air with their breath. It is good to spend a little time among the flowers. Their graceful forms, their bright colours and their; sweet scent should all teach us- that -we .too, should be bright and 'cheerful and happy." Niece Nelly Mannion, 44, Norris-street (Bea- mont, thinks snowdrops, crocuses, daffodils and narcissus make the entrance of the house look so much brighter. Niece Edith Morland, 3,3, .Fitzherbert-street (Beamont).—" You have set me a difficult task this week, as I like every flower, but on con- sideration I would like to cultivate the growth of the 'rose, 'for I think a garden of rose trees in full bloom, and of different colours, would look beautiful." Niece Nellie Roberts, 43. Dudley-street (Bea- mont), favours the daffodil, which, she says, first ;of . all a •green • bud. But ,when it has been a bud for a day or two it gets tired, so it says to itself I think I will show my flower," and all at once, when it is sunny, it peeps out. Niece Dorothy Wright, 68, Winwick.;street (Wycliffe).—" If I had to choose a flower to grow I should choose the lily of the valley. Growing lilies look very pretty." she(NV y, says,eli f f) a prefersrea nddaffwodhiel m n. you nL daeaffoldititllse way from them they look very pretty swaying in . Niece Mabel Wright, 68, Winwick-gtreet the breeze. 'the double, h Niece Phyllis Lightfoot, 4, Dudley-street (Bea- mont).—" Lilies and sweet peas; they smell so sweet and look so nice." . . • Nephew Joseph Jeavons, 9, Elizabeth-street.— "I should like to grow the rose because of its beautiful scent and colour. Trl olden days there were wars between the Yorkists and Lan-</p>
        <p>castrians. The Yorkists wore the white rose and the Lancastrians the red rose. Both sides won one after the other, but in the end the Yorkists won."</p>
        <p>Nephew Royle Dakin, " Glaslin," Green-lane, Padgate.—" I should like to have a patch of the garden for my very own, in which I would set roses of all varieties. I would water them and would see that the ground in which they were planted was well manured, and weeded, and that they obtained plenty of sunshine." Niece Jeannie Wright, 10, Church-street.—" I would like to grow hyacinths, daffodils and the narcissus."</p>
        <p>Niece Florrie Forber' 61, Bewsey-street.---" If I had a garden I would like to grow some daffo- dils. Mother bought some the other day, and I thought they were very nice. She put them on the dressing table in my ,bedroom. Well, I could not help looking at them, they were so pretty. I looked at them until I fell asleep."</p>
        <p>- Niece Bertha Jackson, 56, Bewsey-street, would like to grow wallflowers " because they have such a- lovely scent. The 'shade also is very pretty;• and, again, it is a favourite with all people." Niece Fanny Smith, The Crescent, Oughtring- ton.—" I should like to set tulips, as I think they are very nice flowers, and they 'look so sweet in 'a nice little garden. They look very pretty in vases." Niece Amy Eaton, 22, Marson-street, would like to grow daffodils, hyacinths, narcissus, crocuses, wallflowers, the iris, jonquils, tulips. acacia, lilies, primroses, snowdrops, pinks, nasturtiums, marguerites, poppies, anemone, chrysanthemums and pansies. Niece Beryl Malkin, Royal Exchange, Golden- square, says her favourite flower is a hot-house flower called orchid. "Orchids are very dear, but I think they are a beautiful special act of Nature."</p>
        <p>Niece Florrie Houghton, 55, Brick-street.—" I should like to grow the rose and the lilac, my favourite flowers."</p>
        <p>Niece Florence Wycherley, 19, Leonard-street, would like to grow the tulip, pansy and nar- cissus, which look most beautiful when in full bloom.</p>
        <p>Niece Cicely Arnold, 7, Hamilton-street, likes best the rose, violet and chrysanthemum. Niece Mabel Langley, 178, Padgate-lane.— " Roses, sweet peas, wallflowers, ten-week stock, candytuft, violas, I would very much like to grow, as they have beautiful colours and they are sweet scented. I like the climatis and the hollyhock, too, for their beautiful colours. I only wish I had a great big garden that would grow all those flowers."</p>
        <p>Nephew Philip Wycherley, 49, Leonard-street, favours tulips, snowdrops and daffodils. Nephew Harold Critchley, 355, Wellfield-street. —" In the spring Lwould like to grow daffodils tulips, narcissus and hyacinth's; in the summer roses, pansies, forget-me-nets, daisies and sweet peas."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>FORGOTTEN NUMBERS.</head>
        <p>FORGOTTEN NUMBERS.</p>
        <p>I wish all my nephews and nieces would, when writing to me, give their name and address and number. There are, now nearly 800 of you. When you state your number I can find your entry in the Big Book in a moment; when you do not I have to go through all the names, which is a big job, isn't it F Uncle Ned's wisdom foresaw this difficulty, and an easy way out of it when he gave to each of you a distinguishing number. Forgotten numbers are asked for by the following:— Nephew Royle Dakin, Padgate, whose number is 411.</p>
        <p>Niece Dorothy E. Bennett, Stockton Heath. whose number is 435.</p>
        <p>Niece Mary Roche is placed on my list of new members. She has already won a prize, but for some reason had not been enrolled. Her num- ber is 751.</p>
        <p>Niece Edith Buck-539. Niece Edith Morland—number 567.</p>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>750 Edith Howard, 26, Grafton-street, 10 part of age; Evelyn-street Council School 751 Mary Roche, 6, Bestock-street, Bank Quay. 752 Madge Langley, 178, Padgate-lane; 10; Private School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NIECE DOROTHY'S THANKS.</head>
        <p>NIECE DOROTHY'S THANKS.</p>
        <p>"68, Winwiek-street, 'Warrington, February 27th, 1916.</p>
        <p>"Dear Uncle .Jack,-:—I thank you very much for the beautiful . prize which I receive:l on Saturday morning: It appears to me to be a tong and interesting 'story, which 1 approcistte very much: I can promise you I will takei care of-lt and keep it nice and clean, .so thct,if body wants to 'See it they will se .how I have taken care of it..</p>
        <p>"No. 739 DOROTHY WRIGHT"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1916.</p>
        <p>+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++ ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++ + +4. ++ +4. ++ +4. ++ +4. ++ +4. ++ 4.4. ++ +4. ++ • 4.</p>
        <p>Otburrli grills.</p>
        <p>A SAD LENT.</p>
        <p>Rector On Joint Responsibility Foi</p>
        <p>The War.</p>
        <p>The Rector of Warrington, in the "Pan Magazine" for March, says:— If ever there was a year when Lent should b observed it t‘coild seem to be th 9. presen4:. A least ten nations are engaged in war, and more than twenty millions of men are fighting. War, too, of a horrible kind on the part of our enemies -Germans, Turks, Bulgarians alike, not the war of civilised nations, but murder, massacre, mutilations, starvation, abominable atrocities too dreadful one would have thought even for imagination. Five millions of soldiers, either of the enemy 'or of the Allies, have been slain. Relations and friends in every country are mourning for the dead. It is or it should be a time of penitence. Who are the authors of this war? Without hesitation we must say the Germans—the Emperor, his ' military and naval advisers and the people. For years they have been preparing,' and in 1914 they thought the time had come when we were not ready and the country was divided into parties. Nor was it the Army and Navy only that wanted the war. It was the people also; this has been abundantly proved and bitter hatred' has filled the hearts of all. Are We Blameless?</p>
        <p>But are we blameless in this matter? Was it not our carelessness, our culpable defenceless- ness, our unpreparedness, our divisions and miserable party strife that provoked the war? Would Germany have dared to attack us had we been united, had we been prepared? No, we hai`e to a great extent brought it on ourselves. But let us look more closely and think more seriously. Who else has caused this war? Without doubt the Devil. He has stirred up all these bad passions of envy, jealousy and hatred. He it is who delights to make strife and bad feeling amongst nations. He without doubt was the prime originator of all this trouble. Yet once again let us consider care- fully who has allowed this war? Reverently we may answer, God. Nothing happens without Him. He has permitted it and for our good. What was our condition before the war? What was the state of the streets at night in London and other large cities of the Empire? Was the number of convictions for drunkenness on Satur- day nights creditable to us as a nation? An Inordinate Love of P)easure.</p>
        <p>And even though there has been a great im- provement in many respects do not the people seem given up to an inordinate love of pleasure? We are told that the picture palaces and places of amusement are packed every night. Is this reasonable, is it right? Can the Reople afford such expense? Ought they to be so spending money when economy is such a dire necessity? No, God intends to teach us by this war the seriousness of life. He wishes to prepare us for the enjoyment of a new and better life, and Lent is the call of His Church to her children to put away for a season even innocent pleasures and think for a time of the things which con- cern our souls of the life which we might be living even in this world for the good of others, and as a preparation'for the life to come.</p>
        <p>sh</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>REV. T. A. J. RIDPATII</head>
        <p>REV. T. A. J. RIDPATII</p>
        <p>Visits Old Parishioners At St. George's.</p>
        <p>All who attended St. George's annual congre- gational tea, held in the Mission Room on Wed- nesday evening, were delighted, particularly as they had the opportunity of meeting their late curate, the Rev. T. A. J. Ridpath. The reverend gentleman left Warrington about four and a half years ago to take up the rectorship of Whel-no-tham, Bury St. Edmonds. This week he has been spending a few days in Warrington, and, accordingly, on Wednesday evening, took the opportunity of meeting some of his old friends. There would be about 200 parishioners and friends present at this annual Igathering, and to these the reverend gentleman spoke a few words. He said he always likkd Lancashire people, and especially those at War- rington. He was very pleased to be amongst them again.</p>
        <p>An enjoyable evening was spent by all. After an excellent repast had been partaken of. dancing, games and card playing were indulged in, while songs, recitations and a sketch entitled "Four Lancashire Lassies," by four small girls, all gained unstinted applause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LADIES' DAY AT WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</head>
        <p>LADIES' DAY AT WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>An open meeting was held on Sunday, and presided- over by Mrs. T. Moore, who spoke appropriate words upon the work of the P.S.A. Miss Bogstrom, of the Railwaymen's Mission, delivered an address upon the words of God to the Prophet Elijah in the cave, "What doest thou here, Elijah?" She said that the prophet was very despondent, the people having grieved him with their sins and wayivardness, sci he be- took himself to a cave, which was not the place for a leading man of God. And God spoke to him. We were called to have faith in God at all times. Faith was a living reality, and despondency showed up our weakness. Elijah must cane out of the cave. He would not have peace there. His place was amongst the people, and not to hide away from every- body. Like Moses, God said to him, "Get thee down from the mount to the plain, the people have gone wrong." We in our sins must be chastened. We must not be downhearted be- cause of the.sin of men around us, but as Christ showed us our duty, so let us do it faithfully. God gave us power to overcome all difficulties. As Elijah heard the voice, so let us hear and be obedient What was the question to us? What were we doing? -Were we in the cave, or up' and doing? We must not be downhearted with the present events, but might they make us more trustful, patiently waiting upon God. • Miss Jones rendered "The Slumberland "with power and taste. Mr. Payton presided at the organ, and Miss Alice Beswick read the lesson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MILITARISM EXALTED.</head>
        <p>MILITARISM EXALTED.</p>
        <p>Friends' Lecturer Says Civilisation Is</p>
        <p>In Peril.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. E. Pollard, of York, in the Friends' Meeting House, Buttermarket-street, on Satur- day evening, said that the chief perils under which civilisation lay were the prolongation of the slaughter that was at present going on, and a danger of a wrong kind of settlement being arrived at on the conclusion of war. In war the idea of the standard to which conduct must conform or be judged was very nearly abolished. As a matter of fact: there was no standard ex- cept success in war, or the best road to victory. War, therefore, must tend to make the feelings ,of men more antagonistic every week it lasted. A statesman had described war as "the grave of all good," and that summed up the truth. The tendency of war was to exalt militarism, and therefore it must be opposed to human liberty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>STAY-AT-HOME SERMONS.</head>
        <p>STAY-AT-HOME SERMONS.</p>
        <p>There are many who from various causes are prevented from attending public worship, and yet they need the consolation of religion in these anxious and troublesome times. For such the new Stay-at-Home Sermons advertised in our columns will supply a felt need. A speci- men copy and full particulars of this new pro- posal will be sent for twopence. The contribu- tors, we understand, are well-known preachers.</p>
      </div>
