      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>OLD AND NEW WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OLD AND NEW WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Regrets About The Bridge Expressed At Academy.</p>
        <p>Formed eighteen years ago to aim at the pre- servation of ancient buildings and monuments, and to maintain the intellectual and artistic traditions of the town, the Warrington Society has now a membership of 103. Last year there was a loss of £23.</p>
        <p>The report submitted to the -annual meeting of the society in that famous building, the Old Academy, at Bridge Foot, noted with. satisfac- tion that the bridge had been completed during the year, but regretted that the Corporation could not see their way to erect commemorative tablets on the two main southern pylons con- taining a synopsis of the history of the previous bridges, which is vitally connected with the growth and evolution of the town. They were glad to record the completion of the Butter- market-street improvement, which, unfortun- ately, involved the demolition of the Old Fox, One of the most pictures'que and ancient local buildings. A fine Elizabethan mantelpiece was discovered in the course of its removal, and this and its interesting record on a panel at the front, "Potatoes and Apples Downwards, 1704," were now preserved at the Museum. They were , pleased to learn it is proposed to erect this ancient landmark as a shelter in Victoria Park. The committee sincerely hoped that, in all future public improvements, the few remaining local links with our distinguished and historic past might be preserved wherever possible, or duly recorded for the benefit of future genera- tions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Local Patriotism.</head>
        <p>Local Patriotism.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett, who moved the adoption of the report and accounts, said that owing to the war the society was in a state of suspended animation. But, to be perfectly frank, he did not think they had done all they might have done, or all the public expected of them. Their objects were so comprehensive that there was not one of them' who might not take SOlllO active part in carrying them out. Their mem- bership was many-sided, but there was room and scope for them all: All of them could do some- thing for the Academy, something for Warring- ton. Patriotism began at -home, and, if they wished to be worthy of the Empire for which so many of their friends, and some of their own members, ?wer,e shedding their • heart's blood, they ought. at least to do their very utmost for the place in which they lived to see that, when the war is over, this bit of England should at .any rate be made the most of; and that its great tradi- tions should inspire them to still greater deeds, remembering always Mr. Beamont's wisp re- , minder that " the past is a book filled with lessons to teach the present how to improve the future." Mr. J. Longland seconded. _ _</p>
        <p>The election of officers resulted as follows: President, Alderman Bennett; bon. treasurer, Mr. J. Foster Jackson; hon. secretaries, Messrs, James Richardson and H. Stuart Page; hon auditor, Mr. A. Brotherton.</p>
        <p>How To Treat A Wife. Extract from lecture by N.C.O.:— " Your rifle is your best friend, take care of it; treat it as you would your wife; rub it all over with an oily rag every day."—" Punch."</p>
        <p>The Best PANCAKES are made with BO RWICKS BAKING POWDER.</p>
