      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1916.</p>
        <p>EARLY CLOSING FOR SHOPS AND MARKET.</p>
        <p>Abolish The Fair.</p>
        <p>While we are straining after points upon which we can save it would be well if we took in hand at once those reforms which would be ready to hand. One of the most. striking sentences in the autobiography of BENJAMIN FRANKLIN is that in which he records his impression of English customs. Englishmen, he tells his readers, rise from bed and begin work after the sun has been in the heavens for hour—and then they complain about the price of candles. Of course, it is the price of the cubic feet of gas or the unit of electricity about which we com- plain in these days; but we still make use of the wrong end of the day, when, for nine months in the year at all events; we Could get all the light we need for nothing.</p>
        <p>The wasteful use of artificial light is much more pronounced now than in FRANKLIN'S time. The great shops with their elaborate windows and artistic dressings, the greater public-houses with their garish attractions, the huge glass-covered marka-place and its close relations the railway stations, were then unknown. Electric cars and street lighting, which cause a glare to ascend over a moderate- sized town that can be seen miles away, were uninvented. In our prosperity and imagined security we have endeavoured to make the night an improvement upon the day; though, as is generally the case when we clash with the decrees of Nature, we stand to be badly handled. War with its limiting conditions appears to have come to check us in our mad flight, and it has already brought us back some distance of the vl'ay that may lead to the employment of more common-sense customs.</p>
        <p>A fortnight's experience of the Lighting Order, which in reality is a Darkening Order, has made people think .that it may be pos- sible, after all, to adopt rational methods- to our trades and businesses. At a stroke of the pen the shop windows have been blotted out as allurements to tho passing potential ens- Naturally, the question at once arises, Could not the customer be induced to enter the shop in the hours that are light? The shopkeeper thinks the customer mig,ht condescend to do so—if all shops closed when the time came to light the lights which must not be seen. There is in town a partial agree- ment already among tradesmen that con- certed action could. be arranged in certain circumstances.</p>
        <p>tomer.</p>
        <p>Those circumstances have reference to the Market. At present Warrington 'Market may be open to the public every day except Sun- day, Christmas Day and Good Friday, or any day appointed for a public feast, or thanksgiv- ing, as follows: From Lady Day to Michael- mas, from 6 a.m. till 9 p.m., and on Saturdays till 11 p.m.; from Michaelmas to Lady Day, from 8 a.m. till 8 p.m., and on Saturdays till 11 p.m. There is an important proviso in the bylaws that such other hours may ho fixed' "as the Corporation shall, after one calendar month's public notice, direct.", The present seems to be a very opportune time so to vary the hours of opening in the Market as to make it possible for the tradesmen in town to close their establishments at a civilised time at night, especially Saturday night. An attempt has been made to paint the glass roof of the Market so that the light from the hundreds of stall gases cannot be seen from aloft. The result has been a double failure. On the one hand, the artificial ligu4 has not been ade- quately obscured, and the attempted darken- ing will be little or no protection against hos- tile sir craft; while, on the other hand, it has so subdued the natural light of the daytime</p>
        <p>that stallholders will have to transact busi- ness in semi-darkness or go to the expense of being perpetually lit up—a curious way of effecting war-time ecdnomies. As is well known, the Market goes along till nearly mid- night on Saturdays, and many shops are dragged along to the same unnatural hours, merely as an act of sea-defence.</p>
        <p>Why could we not, alter all this and put affairs on a sound businesslike footing? We now have such a chance as we never had, a chance that will not recur. Public-houses close at 9.30, and the old excuse that the late comers from these places must be pro- vided for no longec holds good. True, the tramcars still run till *midnight or there- abouts, and so offer . facilities for late shop- ping, which is a reflection on a Corporation that has, for economy's sake, stopped nearly all public work for which it is responsible. Why, we ask, should the cars remain in full operation when material is so dear and people are, or should be, at home? We understand that representations have been made to the Tramways Committee this week upon these lines, and we can only hope those representa- tions will bear fruit.</p>
        <p>So far we have dealt with the extravagant growths of modern times. But what of the survival of the extravagances of a bygone age, as represented by the orgy of noise and light that we get at the Fairground? Here present conditions give another opportunity not only to effect a reform, but to abolish a nuisance. For years the people living in the neighbour- ' hood of the Fair have longed for its abolition. They, being ordinary human beings who require sleep and occasionally have the sick and dying upon their hands, have sometimes asked for a removal of the nuisance from their doors, but their complaints and requests have fallen upon deaf ears. Last week four men who run such superfluous concerns as roundabouts, Aunt Sallies and hoop-las on this Fairground were fined for not complying with the Light- ing Order, and so being a centre of danger to an otherwise darkened town. Why not con- sign hoop-las and similar entertainments that require so much light even in defiance of Gov- ernment orders and of Zeppelins to that bourn from which they will never return—as a permanent institution at any rate? The CHIEF CONSTABLE has, within the last day or two, made suggestions to the Markets Com- mittee respecting the lights from the Fair- ground, and it has 'been resolved that the Fair, which cannot be screened, must "close down" when lighting-up time begins. Those who have rented the place will have an allow- ance for the time they thus lose.</p>
        <p>Late shopping, late marketing, late ears, fairgrounds, and the like were, even in peace. times, •woefully wasteful; in war time, when our national existence depends upon our ability to save and our local safety may any time depend upon our loyalty to the Lighting Order, they constitute a dangerous and a criminal extravagance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
