      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>110 INCREASE IN RATES.</head>
        <p>110 INCREASE IN RATES.</p>
        <p>There will be no increase in the rates which will be called for next Tuesday night, when Councillor Wilkinson makes his budget speech for the year 1916-17. Seeing that the rates were carried from Bs. in the £ to 9s. 4d. in the t last year, , and that they now stand at the lattek high figure, there is cause for thank- fulness that We have, at all events tempor- arily, reached a stationary figure. Perhaps next year we may get some relief—perhaps. The estimated expenditure this year. is £107,141, against £107;1705 last year, and £89,631 in 1914-15. The total has been kept near last year's• total, notwithstanding many changes on both sides of the ledger, such as the saving on one side of £BOO on police- men's wages, £3,000 on the lighting account; and the extraordinary expenditure on the other in connection with war charges—in- creased interest on loans and substantial in- surance against aircraft damage. The slight increase in the needs. of the town in the aggregate has been met out of the balances in hand, which- are now as low as they can be allowed, with safety, to go.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>SET AT NOUGHT.</head>
        <p>SET AT NOUGHT.</p>
        <p>Shopkeepers And Others Who Disobeyed Lighting Order.</p>
        <p>HEAVY FINES.</p>
        <p>The determination of the authorities that th:_t Lighting Order shall be strictly observed may be judged, from the fines imposed at the Police Court on Thursday.. Lawrence Silcock, roundabout proprietor, Latchford, was fined £3 for having two swing- ing lamps on the hobby-horses in Park-avenue, which threw a light into the sky like a search- light. For having lights visible from fourteen win- dows, three being skylights, in the laundry pro- mise 4 of the Warrington and Latchford Steam Laundry Company, in Stafford-road, the firm were fined £lO. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The light from the butcher's shop of. John Cross, Horsemarket-street, was shining on the footpath and half-way across the street, and for this offence he was fined £5. When spoken to by Acting-Sergeant Hayes, Cross pointed to some Corporation lamps and asked, "Why don't you put those out, too"?" The officer replied, "Those are for the guidance of pedestrians," where- upon Cross retorted, "Well, these lights are for my business, and I shall not drop my blinds any more for anybody." An unobscured fanlight at the hairdresser's shop of • William Titchard, 8, Barnard-street which threw a bright light on the roofs of the houses opposite, cost him 30s. The "first prosecution against a householder for having a bright light in the front room resulted in a penalty ef 40s. being imposed upon James Buckley, Sheplierd's-row, Orford-street. The light was shining at 12.30 a.m. George Cunliffe, the manager of the Argenta Meat Stores, was fined £3 for having his shop, in Bridge-street, brilliantly illuminated with electric lights, and the light shining across the street.</p>
        <p>A similar fine was imposed upon David Walsh, Pitt-street, for showing a light through his back kitchen window. The blind had not been drawn. When spoken to by a policeman, Walsh said, "You might let me off. I am just going to bed." It was 10.20 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>NO LIGHTS.</head>
        <p>NO LIGHTS.</p>
        <p>A cyclist named Albert Smith, 293, Lovely- lane, was fined ss. at the Borough Court on Wed- nesday for failing to have a red rear light on Sunday night, February 20th. When stopped by a police officer in Folly-lane, defendant said that as he was so near home he did not think it was worth while relighting the lamp. John Millman, 51, Birchall-street, who was in charge of a horse and lorry in Liverpool- road at 6.35 at night was fined ss. for failing to have his lamps.lighted. Peter Birchall, 31, Tanners-lane, was fined a similar amount for an offence in the same road.</p>
        <p>Ronald Syers, 2, John-street, was fined ss. for having no light on a handcart which he was pushing in Foundry-street near midnight on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
