      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1916. GOSSIP. I FATAL ECONOMY.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1916. GOSSIP. I FATAL ECONOMY.</p>
        <p>• Lying on his back in the Lord Derby Hospital, a soldier thought of a line for a Limerick com- petition, and on, Sunday received, word that he had won £3OO. A nice little stand-by when he has spent his bob a day!</p>
        <p>Procedure at the.Warrington District Recruit- ing Tribunal, which met in Bewsey Chambers on Thursday, was truly rural.</p>
        <p>The members sat round the table, and the public stood round them, and, after half an hour or so, when the ladies were probably get- ting weary, the Chairman (Mr. George MatlYer) said he was sorry he could not offer them any chairs or, cups of tea! .</p>
        <p>• Mr. Mather is an admirable gentleman for putting victims at ease. He knows nearly every- body in the district, and this was typical of his waiyi on anyone entering: "Good afternoon, Henry; you want your son exempted, don't you?" "I do that," emphasised the farmer.</p>
        <p>After questions, Mr. Mather remarked,,Almost apologetically, "Well, just sttnd outlide a minute, Henry," and on his rettirn,• "Sorry, Henry, but yolell have to try to get someone else to do the work. We can only give you a month." And such like.</p>
        <p>Dr. Murray, the member of the Tribunal who chanced to be sitting nearest the door, acted as usher. When a name was called he rose from his seat, opened the door, and vociferated ponderously, as if he were a constable in a police court corridor.</p>
        <p>Once he could not find the applicant, and he returned with the remark, "He has not yet made his appearance, your worships."</p>
        <p>I am pleased to state that Mr. Fairhurst, the Borough Treasurer, is rapidly recovering from his indisposition, and hopes to be at the office next week.</p>
        <p>Is the gentleman who last week suggested the dismantling of Arialey Cannons, afraid lest Warrington should be taken for a fortified town?</p>
        <p>The Warrington Recruitinerribnnal is com- posed of very impressionable and sensitive gentlemen—to cold.</p>
        <p>On Friday or last week and Monday of this they occupied seats at the end of the Council Chamber farthermost from the fireplace, and several times I heard murmurs of complaints of feeling cold; in fact, twice during intervals Alderman Tinnion sought comfort by sitting on the radiator. Dangling his feet ,he remarked, " Ah, that's better!"</p>
        <p>Yesterday, however, the court was turned round, and Alderman Tinnion, sitting within a yard or two of the fireplace, looked warm and comfortable, as an alderman.of the borough should do.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Chamber of Commerce has been been well represented at the annual Meet- ings of the Associated Chambers of Commerce at the Hotel Cecil, this week. They were: The Vice-president (Mr. J. J. Whitley), Mr. P. A. Trost; Mr. ' Nat. Greening, Mr. Holman Kingdon, and the Secretary (Alderman Bennett)</p>
        <p>• I hear very strong comment.; about the severity of the magistrates who tried the first eases brought against Warrington people for dis- obeying the lighting Order. For first offences the penalties were excessively high, especially in view of the practice of other Courts.</p>
        <p>The prevailing darkness, so I am informed,' resulted in a shopkeeper who usually " takes '' £4 on Sundays, taking only 2s. 4d. last Sunday.</p>
        <p>I am pleased to see that at least one Warring- ton organist has thought it desirable to substi. tute tt good English• tune for one of, •enemy origin. Last Sunday at St. 'James' Church, Mr. E. Davies. for the singing of 545 "A. and M.," replaced the tune " Austria,"•which is the tune of the Austrian National, Anthem, by the tune "iEverton." There is ceitainly a more homely and tasteful flavour about that word. ' VIGLTANT.</p>
        <p>-saving? Can they undertake the physical training of lads between the ages of elevenlrand fifteen? Are women teachers capable of: con- trolling the boys over fourteen who attend secondary or evening continuation schools? I think not; and if the conditions under which male teachers work are not made more attrac- tive the outlook is most depressing." There would always be a: dearth of ,xnale teachers until conditions were improved under the following heads :—Fair and systematic methods of appointment and promotion;, the variety of methods of qualification; the pecu- liar methods of judging work done in schools; and the smallness of emoluments compared with those of other professions With all its faults elementary education had produced humane, heroic and self-resourceful men, and women, capable of .coping. with any work .the nation may • need of them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>MASONIC INSTALLATION. Bro. J. S. Hill Master Of The " Lodge Of Charity."</head>
        <p>MASONIC INSTALLATION. Bro. J. S. Hill Master Of The " Lodge Of Charity."</p>
        <p>The "Lodge of Charity," No. 2651, held its annual installation on Thuisday. There were over ninety brethren present, this being a record attendance. The provincial brethren included: W.Bro. W. P.M,, P.P.G.5.8.; W.Bro. J. 11. Birrell, P.M., P.G.W., W.Bro. W. H. J. Jenkins, P.M.., P.P.R. ; W.Bro. John Hesketh, P.P.A.G.S.; W.Bro. Rev. H. V. Pigot, P.M., P.P.G.C.; W.Bro. R. H. ' Pearson, P.M., P.P.G.O:; W.Bro. T. Cooper, P.M., P.P.G.D.C., P.P.G.P. (Stiffs), D.C. ; W.Bro. D. Tinnion, P.P.G.5.8.; W.Bro. IL F. _Greenough, P.M., P.P.G.5.8.; W.Bro. Fred Howard, P.M., P.P.G.A.D.C. The local lodges were represented by: W.Bro. T. I. Fletcher, W.M., "Lodge. of Lights," 148; W.Bro. Lunt, P.M. "Gilbert Greenall" Lodge, 1250; W.Bro. J. Moore Murray, W.M., "Lodge of Friendship," 2963; W.Bro. T. S. Steel, W.M., "St. Elphin's " Lodge, 3287; W.Bro. Herbert Woods, W.M., "Lodge of Rectitude," 3597; "Lodge of Instruction" by W.Bro. H. E. Travis, P.M. The installing Master was W.Bro. Tickell, 1.P.M., and acting (during W.Bro. BraCecamp"s absence) as W.M. The Master-Elect, Bro. J. S. Hill, was pre- sented by W.Bros. J. R. Young, and W. H. Woodcock, and after being installed, invested his officers: S.W., Bro. F. Hollingsworth ; LW., Bro. T. Harding; Treasurer, W.Bro. H. M. Ash- ton, P.M.; Secretary, Bro. F. A. Harris; S.D., Bro. P. 11. Bennett; J.D., Bro. H. Lowe; D.C., W.Bro. W. 11. Stansfield, P.M.; Organist, Bro. E. Wilkes; 1.G., Bro. Sydney Bolton; Chaplain, W.Bro. W. Bracecamp, 1.P.M.; S.S., Bro. J. Allinson; J.S. Bro. J. Cann; A.S., Bros. J. Storey. and E. Lowe; Tyler, Bro. S. Rice; Almoner, W.Bro. Hainsworth, P.M.; C.R. W.Bro. D. Tinnion, P.M., P.P.G.S.P. The ceremony was conducted excellently, and reflected the greatest credit upon W.Bro. T. Cooper. The usual banquet was dispensed with owing to the war, and light refreshments were served. Violin solos were given by W.Bro. Pearson, songs by W.Bro. Tickell, and a 'quar- tet by " Charity " Quartet Party. Mr. W. IT. Rigby contributed two songs, which were much appreciated. W.Bro. Pearson and Bro. E. Wilkes supplied the accompaniments in an effi- cient manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MR. JOHN WILLETT.</head>
        <p>MR. JOHN WILLETT.</p>
        <p>Recognition of 50 Years' Service</p>
        <p>At Crosfield's.</p>
        <p>On Monday, in pursuance of the established policy of Messrs. John Crosfield and Sons', Ltd., a presentation of the sum of £25 was made bi- the Chairman of the Company (Mr. 1% W. Brock) in the presence of the Directors, to Mr. John Willett, who completed half a century of service a few days ago. Addressing Mr. Willett, Mr. Brock said: have very great pleasure in asking you to accept this cheque as a memento of the 50 years of ser- vice you have just completed. I also desire, -on behalf of the Board, to express to you the appreciation which we feel for the loyalty you have shown in your consideration of the firm's interests over the half century of your employ- ment here. While, no doubt, you will ap- preciate this gift for its material value,. I am sure you will attach much greater importance to the fact that you have gained and retained the confidence and respect of your employers. I sincerely hnpe tha t for many years to come, in the enjoyment of good health, we may count you amongst our workmen, and that in the continued loyal discharge of your duty you may help to inspire the younger men in the works to imitate your example. Mr. Willett, in reply, desired to thank the Chairman and Directors of the Company for the substantial present he had received. He was' always proud of his connection with the firm, and to-day he felt, extremely grateful that he was still spared to work for a company, so ably managed, and the success and prosperity of which was so well known.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Wild And Untimely Mania For " Saving" On Education.</head>
        <p>Wild And Untimely Mania For " Saving" On Education.</p>
        <p>TEACHERS SAY OUR WHOLE FUTURE IS AT STAKE.</p>
        <p>"This war will not last for 'ever. We must remember that at will, be followed by another and more persistent war—the war for commer- cial supremacy; and for that we must be pre- pared. The only possible preparation for, that is education. The children of to-day will be the citizens of to-morrow, and on them will the continued supremacy of the. British Empire depend. Is it prudent then to cut down to the lowest limit the provision for their education? The Board of Education is prepared to wink at overcrowding, and grouping of classes will, as time passes, become inevitable. .Health and efficiency will both suffer; still, I expect, the same standard of efficiency will be expected." In the above sentence is summarised the reason for the protest made by Mr. A. Knowles (Arpley-street School); the newly-elected Presi- dent of the Warrington and District Teachers' ,Association, against the attempts that are being made in the country at the present time to effect economy at the expense of the educa- tion of the rising generation .of the working classes.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting, at which the election took place, was held last night in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall, and was well attended.</p>
        <p>VITAL QUESTIONS IN THE SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles, in his presidential address, said the past sixteen months had been a time of all-round criticism, and few were -our insti- tutions that. had not been found: • wanting. Never hid the question of education aroused such interest as now, and it. was popular to commence articles by saying that. there was "something wrong with our. system of educa- tion." Of course, as human nature was not perfect, there Was something wrong about everything in the world—including education. He did not intend to attempt to answer .the critics, but to deal with one or two questions which they,. as a professi.on,- considered to be vital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Robbing the Future.</head>
        <p>Robbing the Future.</p>
        <p>The great cry was for economy. Some thought education was a. field for the- exercise of economy. On the other hand, there were those who hold that. economy in education impairs efficiency, and is therefore a saving of a costly character. "Economy in education," said. Arnold Bennett, "Will rob 'the future, of that which it will need most, and need with extreme acuteness, for no present advantage." It was said that teachers' would willingly, for patriotic reasons, shoulder, an increased burden of work. Teachers were as patriotic as any class and could be depended upon to do their "bit," whether :iri the trenches or class-room; but the question was--" Are the children of to-day to get' their. full chance, physically and mentally?!' If, not, ,future fit- ness is to be sacrificed' and pfudence is to give place to panic. ' , Appalling Shortsightedness.</p>
        <p>When the attacks on foot were examined it • was clear that the present national crisis waS being seized by those who had always been for "economy" in education so, far as the educa- tion of the children of the working classes was concerned. "The Schoolmaster"! commenting on the Contemplated closing down of evening schools in some towns, said :-- "Temporary saving to the Jecal atepa ye r s it may be, but at what a cost of future bene- fits The earning powers Of hundreds of future citizens, will be terribly curtailed.. At a time when money ,is being made with nnprecedented ease in industrial centres thi, wild and un- timely mania for economy in educational ex- penditure evidences appalling „Shortsightedness in our local administrators"' He hoped such a paragraph might never be written of our local authorities. While America spends .V 3 per pupll4early we only spend half as much; we are smattig more on one month of war than on Cine,manth of educa- tion. Every twelVe. days we, spend xnore, for war- than we spent last Year foi"the edneation' of our 7,000,000 boys and gifle:lA. 'Ctirtvict had spent upon him, ten times the amOnnt that was :spent upon a schoolboy. Sir Henry ilibbert; , warning the Lancashire Eclucatibn Cardinittee against the dangers of going 'to extrenie4 in this class of economy, said the rising genera- tion would have the responsibility e? the heri- tage of war laid upon them, and 'to stint their I.education now would be eqUivaltrit to'sendlng i ill-trained 6oldiers into the fight. Yet, in Many places the technical education'jof our young people was being violently interrupted, or stoPped altogether. The School Leaving Age.</p>
        <p>Elementary school: teachers were sometimes blamed for not turning out perfbctly educated; children from their schools. He would ask : How many of those children pass through the highest standards of these schools? When boy of 13 or 14 left school he had only just begun to appreciate education, and unless the good foundation is made good use of in the home and by the continuation of his 'studies, there was the danger that all• the good work done would be lost. Unfortunately many parents do not take the slightest interest in education, and he had seen it stated that many employers do not like an educated workman. On the other hand, there were employers who compelled their young people to continue their education. It was held by some that the only solution was the holding of day continuation classes, where one-half the attendances would be made out of the employer's time and the other half out of the employe's time. It was astounding that, in a country like ours, so little interest should be taken in the 1,500,008 children between the ages of 14 and 17, who are receiving no education at all and who at the most critical period of their lives are left without sufficient guidance and care. ks he read lately: " Oh ! that in the piping times of peace the State would invest its capital where the greatest possible interest would be obtained and that it would realise the only capital that has personal si,gnificancethe men and women of the nation—to whom frantic' appeals for assistance are made in times of national peril." Exemptions Too Easily Got.</p>
        <p>"How gladly," said the speaker, "would we all welcome the raising of the compulsory school age to 15. We know that'the times are abnormal. Yet we all. recognise that our own local authority grants far too easily and readily exemptions for children of 13. We know too well that the majority of parents— long -before the war was thought Of—only wait for a boy. to attain the age of 14, and if that day comes in the middle of a Week it makes no difference: he is at once removed from school, and, in ninety-nine eases out of a hundred, that boy's education is at an end." The attempts that were being made, and successfully in some districts,' to lower the age of exemption must have struck education- ists with dismay. It behoved all branches of the union to exert the greatest vigilance to prevent, the exploitation of child labour-t-one of the most serious problems to be faced when the war comes to an end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Will Women Displace Men Teachers?</head>
        <p>Will Women Displace Men Teachers?</p>
        <p>Another matter contemplated. , with grave misgivings was the future staffing of the schools with fully-qualified teachers. Anxiety on this point was great before the war; 'anxiety had been intensified by the war almost into alarm. There was a rapidly diminishing, number of new entrants and , the serious shortage was bound to develop into a return to the increased employment of uncertificated and: stipple'men- tary teachers. From 1911 to 1915 there. was a decrease of 25 per cent. in boy entrants to the profession; 11,400 teachers had 'enlisted; 9,000 had attested; 147 had joined the naval forces; and 236 women teachers were serving as nurses. A magnificent professional record included : V.C.'s, 5; •D.C.M.'s or Military Crosses, 25; mentioned in dispatches, .• 12; killed, 232; wounded, 118; missing, 9. Many of the teachers who returned, whether able or unable, might not continue their school work. ..The figures pointed to the Americanising of our schools in the sense that they will be taught *almost en- tirely by women. "I de not want lady teachers to misunder- staml me," he said. "But if schools are al- most entirely run by women it will, be bad for the school as, a whole and bad fql• tile. boys. Some women- are capable of teaching the. elder boys in elementary schools, but they are only available for the work inside the sChOol. What is to become-Of the edneation of these lads" out- side' the school wail?' Can' women ,teachers take the organised games?, cricket and-foot- ball?' Can they take swimming and life-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>BUSINESS OF THE MEETING.</head>
        <p>BUSINESS OF THE MEETING.</p>
        <p>• Mr. E. Morgan, the retiring president, occupied. the chair until he had installed his successor, Mr. KnowleS. SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>F. 11. Longshaw : (the hon. secretary) reported that the association had had another successful year's work. The great war, which had upset the working of all societies and associations, had given .the committee and officers increased work and anxiety. The membership at present was 319; notwithstand- ing' removals of several old members, an in- crease of one. Every available teacher was a member of the association. With reference to the many and varied ways in which members of the association had helped towards sharing the burden of carrying, on the war, he ven- tured to say that the teachers of Warrington had: responded nobly, to the call of help from all sources.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Patriotic Profession.</head>
        <p>A Patriotic Profession.</p>
        <p>First and foremost, continued Mr. Longehaw-, all .our men teachers of Military age have volunteered their',.services for King and coun- try. Their patriotism has been admired by all and your committee not long:ago thanked the Education Committee' for their special reselu- Con of appreciation of the manner in whici our members had given a lasting example of true patriotism to the children under their charge: " I also wish to place on record the sacrifices and work so freely given by the members et this assoeiation in the exacting work of National Registration, when 57 members offi- ciated a.s4enumerators , and 150 worked many hours after school time, often at personal in- convenience, at .the clerical _work. Credit and encouragement must also be given to the -lady members, who, of their own true womanly love, haye devoted many hours of their time both night- and 'day to the care of our sick and wounded; while we cannot but admire -thor work ,of ,those members who have helped in many recruiting campaigns, the making and sending of comfort s to our brave fighters, the purchase and dispatch of parcels to our prisoners-of-war, the attendance at classes in order to qualify for ambulance and Red Cross work, and the collection of money from our schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>TREASURER'S STATEMENT.</head>
        <p>TREASURER'S STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr J. Upon (hon. treasurer) presented a satisfactory general balance sheet, showing .tl9 OS. 9d. in hand. For the War and Relief Funds'the amount subseribed was .{1272 68. ad. (from March 10th, 1915, to. December, 1915). To N.U.T. War Air Fund, £195; balance (re- served for local needs as they arise), .07 Cs 3d This did not exhaust the association's efforts in war relief, substantial amounts having been sent to the Prince of Wales" Fund, West Lan- cashire Base Hospital, Belgian Relief Fund, eze.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The following officers were elected:—Presi- dent, Mr. A. Knowles; vice-president,. Mr. W. vf. Stansfield; ex-president, Mr. J. E. Morgan; hon. secretary, Mr. F. R. Longshaw ; 'hon. treasurer, Mr. J. Upson; Pmss and Par- liamentary correspondent. Mr. W. H..Minards.</p>
        <p>Committee: The Misses Brookes and Orton; Messrs. J. Armkrong I. Bowen. J. Cann, S. W. Jeffery, W. Parker, and G. Newburn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>SCHOOL CHILDREN'S NAAR FUND • EFFORT. '</head>
        <p>SCHOOL CHILDREN'S NAAR FUND • EFFORT. '</p>
        <p>A Splendid Result.'</p>
        <p>The dramatic account of the war given, by the .children attending St. James' School.(head- master Mr. ..Stansfield) and Fairfield School' (headmiStresa Mrs. Stansfield),, in Parr Hall last week, was as great a itiCces\s: financially as artistically, the proceeds ,reaching a , record figure . for a Warrington school children's entertainment.</p>
        <p>- The total takings were £6B Gs. 2d., including £64 19s. for the sale of .1,209 tickets;—,a is. Gd. taken at the dress rehearsal; and £1 ss. Bd. for the sale of programmes., The expenses amounted to £l5 16s. 2d., including £6 1.35. 5-Id. for costumes, £4 is. for printing and ad- vertising, and £.3 35. for the hire of the hall. There was thus £52 10s. left in hand, and this BUM was distributed as follows :—Donation to the Y.M.C.A._Huts Fund, .225; donation to the Church Army nuts' Fund, £25; St. Elphin's Company of the C.L.B. Uniform Fund, £2; Parish Church Branch of the C.B.M.S. for Church Army." Rest Huts, 10s. The accounts have been audited .by the Director of Educa- tion, Mr. J. M. Murray.</p>
        <p>Cheering anci Encouraging to Other Workers.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Palmer Howard, of the Y.M.C.A., ' writing to Mr. Stansfield, February 29th, says: "I thank you very. much for your letter of yesterday's date, together with the very generous, donation of £25 to our Y.M.C.A. work for the soldiers. We are exceedingly grateful for this -assistance and shall .be -.glad if , you will accept for yourself and convey to all con- cerned the warmest thanks of our Council for this, splendid result." Mr. W. Spencer, of the Church Army Head:, quarters, writes:— "In the name of Prebendary Carlile and the Executive Committee 'of. the Church' Army, I desire to 'pass on to you,andMrs..Stansfield; and all those who were associated with you in or- ganising the performance A. Drama- tie Account of the War,' an expression of our most sincere thanks. The result was indeed splendid:- and the amount which you have sent to the Church Army is very welcome indeed, as the demands upon us ,from., all: sonrces :aTe increasing daily, and funds becoine.' more and more difficult to obtain to carry on the work. We should be very, grateful, and esteem it -as a favour, if you will kindly take the grst opportunity of telling all those who were associated with you how cheering and encour- aging such tokens:of 'sympathy are to those of us who are engaged in this arduous/ and . diffi- cult work on behalf -of our gallant men, and. I am sure that whatever self-denial and hard work it caused you, will be more than compen- sated by the knowledge that this • money is lbeing definitely used to. bring a measure of comfort and alleviation to our gallant troops in the many hardships that they have to lendurb."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>E ICHER MARRIES SOLDIER.</head>
        <p>E ICHER MARRIES SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>Picturesque Ceremony At Our Lady's</p>
        <p>Church.</p>
        <p>A pretty and interesting wedding took place on Saturday at Our Lady's Church, St: Mary-street, when Private J. F. Wilson, of the 4th South Lancashire Regiment, was joined in marriage to M 1 A. Kitching, who, for the past eleven years. has been first assistant mistress at Our Lady's School, Latchford. • The ceremony was performed by the Rev. Father Tallon,' the bride, who had her sister, Miss Lydia Kitching aS a. bridesmaid, being given away by Mr. James Booth. The bride. groom was attended by his brother, Mr. Peter Wilson, of Carlisle. A number of friends wit- nessed the ceremony, which was followed by Nuptial Mass, the children's choir singing the Mass of Notre Dame, and Smith's Ave Maria. Suitable ?selections were played on the organ by Miss F. Flood, and the happy couple left the sanctuary to 1' he ,joyous strains of LOhengrin.'s Bridal March. A re'eeptiOn was held in Our Lady's Scheel, the bridal party, clergy and staff being entertained to breakfast. Father Tallen proposed the health of the bride, refAring to her associaticn with Our Lady's. . Among the many handsome and beautiful presents' was a massive oak-mounted inkstand presented to the bride' by the children of Our. Lady's Lady's School: The honeymoon is , being spent in London.. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>A.B.C. 'Time Table.</head>
        <p>A.B.C. 'Time Table.</p>
        <p>The A.B.C. Time Table for March was published on the Ist inst.', and is now on sale. All alterations in the train service have been carefully attended to, and the Guide is a marvel of accuracy.</p>
        <p>Working Men's Infirmary Committee. The annual meeting of the above committee will be held at the Town Hall next Saturday afternoon. His Worship the Plia:c,-tr will take the chair at three o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Raddon Court Pound Day.</head>
        <p>Raddon Court Pound Day.</p>
        <p>The usual Pound Day is to be held on Thus- day next, at the Raddon Court Hospital, when gifts, especially groceries and eggs, are desired. Tea will be served from 3.30 to 5.30 ata nominal charge. • • . , Advance in Price Of Bread.. . .</p>
        <p>, The Warrington 'Master Bakers'. Association and the Warrington Co-ope. ratiVes ,Stores, through our advertisement columns, announce that on and after lionday next the price of bread will be increased one polity 'on every 4Th. loaf.</p>
        <p>Warrhutton Teachers Reuresenled at</p>
        <p>Lancashire N.U.T.</p>
        <p>Representing Warrington teachers at the annual meeting Of the Representative Assembly 4 the N.U.T. (Lancashire County Association). held at.,LiverpOol University on Saturday, were Mr.. J. W. Parker (headmaster of Heathside Schools), Mr. J. Armstrong and •Mr. George Newbu rn. .</p>
        <p>Pneumonia Prevalent. Last week there were in Warrington 29 d.eaths, against 24 in the corresponding week last year. The. following were the causes of deatliv—Pneumonia, six; bronchitis, c.ancer and tuberculous disease ,other than phthisis, two each; plithisis, whooping cough, enteritis, heart disease and accident, one each; all other causes, twelve. The births numbered 32 against 45 last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Late George Cleave.</head>
        <p>The Late George Cleave.</p>
        <p>The directors of the firm of Messrs. Joseph Crasfied and Sons, Ltd., as a special mark of respect they had for their old, employe, George Gleare, appointed Councilor Henry Roberts and Sir. Holman Kingdon to represent them at the funeral last Thursday week. The names of these gentlemen were inadvertently omitted from the list of mourners we published last</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Warrington Market Prices.</head>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's Warrington,Market,' the price of wheat ruled. at. Bs.3d. per 70th., and 4s. 6d. to 4s. 841. per 451 b. was realised for oats. Flour realised 51s. to 565. per 2801 b., 2s. 21d. to . 4-Id. being the price of thirds. The price of bran and other offals. was Is. lid. to 2s. 2d. It is very difficult to get hold of straw owing to the Army authorities claiming it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Presentation to Journalist.</head>
        <p>Presentation to Journalist.</p>
        <p>The members of tke Warrington, Widnes and Runcorn Branch of the National Union of Journalists. at .their monthly meeting, held at Widnes on Saturday, presented Mr. Ernest Iddon, of Warrington, with a fountain pen, in recognition of his five years' service as honorary secretary .o the branch. Mr: Iddon, who is now deputy-chairman, recently relinquished the position, and has. been succeeded by Mr.. C. E. Hawthorn. • The presentation was made by Mr, B. Baker, chairman of the branch, who paid a tribute to the useful work Mr. Maori had accompli sliecl .on behalf of the branch. Several other, members also spoke in appreciative terms of his services. . .</p>
        <p>'Literary Guilders. The -literary evening in connection with tile Sankey .Bedges. Wesley., Guild was held on Monday, Mr. S. Blythe presiding. Mr. R. Schofield the secretary) read the various items, contributed by members to the Guild Maga- zine. In his opening remarks Mr. Schofield, as editor, complimented' his contributors on the excellence 'of their literary efforts. Essays, poems, an account of. :a voyage to the Par East,: and the inevitable love story, found th.eir way into,the Magazine. .Humorous items, interspersed between the more serious matter,. kept the audience merry, and some very .original. "answers to, correspondents" by the Sub-Editor- brought a very 'interesting and enjoyable evening to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Healthy Leather Trade.</head>
        <p>Healthy Leather Trade.</p>
        <p>. . The demand for all 'classes of leather con- tinues 'unabated, says the Warrington corre- spondent Of the "Leather Trades Review," and there is a.yery healthy, and gratifying com- petition amongst buyers for the limited quan- tity of. free leather which is on the market,, all of which tends to a rapid movement and a satisfactory. turnover.. :At the same time, 1 do not think trade has expanded very much dtirin.g the last week or two, and would rather ascribe the present Very strong position to the disparity 'which exists between the capacity cf the demand and the amount coming along to meet it. Internally, matters are just a shade easier with. us, supplies of tanning extracts, though still below normal; are coming along better than they were, and it is encouraging tanners to .venture upon some expansion in anticipat- ing the future needs of the leather raarl:et. Labour, although not plentiful, is at the mom- ent sufficient, and is moreover not .seriously threatened ,by the Recruiting Department, in which we compare favourably with most cther industries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>For Raddon Court Funds.</head>
        <p>For Raddon Court Funds.</p>
        <p>At a whist drive held in the Socialist Hall, Bridge Foot, on Tuesday, there was an attend- ance of over 200 •persons, including the Mayor. It was the first of two drives (the second took place last night and will bo reported in net Saturday's ." Examiner ") organised in - aid of the funds of the Raddon Court Red Cross Hos- pital. .At the intPrval Miss S. Broadbent thanked the donors of prizes and all those who had helped- to make the drive a success. She also distributed the prizes at the Close and thanked the Mayor for attending. The Mayor replied and said he was always. pleased to do anything he could for the hospitals, and also 'proposed a vote of thanks to Miss S. Broadbent for '.distributing the prizes. , Prize-winners :—Ladies: 1, Mrs. Ellis; Mrs. Houghton; 3, Miss L. E. Thum; 4, Mrs; Walton; 5, Miss S. Broadbent; 6, Mrs. Rigby; highest score half-time, Mrs. J. Helsby; lowest score at the finish, Mrs. Gregory. Gentlemen: 1, J. J. Pritchard; 2, J. Upton; 3, J; IL Gittens; 4, W. Waring; 5, A. Jewett; 6, W. F. Waring; highest score half-time, W. Dudley; lowest score at the finish, T. A. Dean. The hall was lent and the folrwing Pave prizes :—Mrs. Waring, Mrs. Smith, Mrs. Etiis, Councillor Chrimes, Messrs. S. Donoug,he, J. H. Gittens. E. •White, J. Coxon, W; Mather, J. Lodge, W. Fletcher,..Warritre;ton Picturedrome,,Miller. 4-, Co., and Carter &amp; Sons. Building Society Makes Good Profit.'</p>
        <p>With a net profit of £847 9s. 6d., the members of .the Warrington Permanent Benefit Building Society met in the Cairo-street Schoolroom on Thursday evening, and .declared a higher divi- dend than they have done for many years, i.e., five per cent. Mr. W. J. Shaw (chairman) said that from the commencement of the present directors taking over the society some years ago, when things were in a "terrible Mess," it had always been their ambition to bring about a dividend of five per cent. That had at last been done,. and everything was fair and square. It was a profit purely due to their sound business methods. Mr. W. H. Woodcock, an auditor, said the position of the society was now, as strong as it need be, and things were satisfactory from every point of view, but he could not under- stand why the society should continue to be so small. In his opinion, property at the present time was well worth baying. The directors who • are paid 2s. Cd. each per meeting, were granted an honorarium of £l5 10s. The retiring directors Messrs. T. Critchley, C. H. Hibbert and F. A. Milling were re-elected. The net profits ..on the -year's working amounted to £847 9s. 6d„. and -the amount brought forward from: last .year's actount was £175 10s. 5d.„ Making a total profit of .£1,022 19s: 11d, for distribution, out of _which a:dividend of five per cent, was paid (free of income .tax), and the balance Carried forward, after de&lt;luct- :bag the honorarium. The reserve funds of the .ociety now exceed ,t 2,000. .</p>
        <p>Legh-street Sale of Work. A sale ot work is to be held in the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church on the 15th and 16th inst., the opening being timed to commence at 3 o'clock. Your Patronage is invited:</p>
        <p>• Warrington Church Society. Lenten addresses are to be. delivered in the Parish C'hiirch on Friday by the Archdeacon of Chester and the Bishop of Lichfield, in the after- noon arid evening respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Off-Licence Transfer.</head>
