      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Fatal Accident to Cab briver.</head>
        <p>Fatal Accident to Cab briver.</p>
        <p>Whilst crossing the road near Warririgton• Bridge on Thursday night, :a cabdriver named. George West (46), 8, .Sparlingstreet, Warring- ton ~on his way home after finishing work at the Patten Arms hotel, was knoe.ked down by - a,taxi .cab and sustained such serion.s injuries, that he died later in the Warringtoninfirmary._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Vt) cliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Vt) cliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The twenty-third anniversary sermons of tho Wycliffe I'.S.A. are to be held to-morrow afternoon at 2.45, when a weleope is to be given to the Rev. T. T. James, who has just returned from the Front. The speaker is to be Alderman W. G. Wilkins, of Derby '(presi- dent of the National Federation) and the Salvation Army Band is to play Selections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Death of Miss Mary I. Biggin.</head>
        <p>Death of Miss Mary I. Biggin.</p>
        <p>News has this week been received in Warring- ton of the death of Miss Mary I. Biggin., former superintendent nurse to the Union In- firmary. For some considerable tittle" she has been residing in Bucks, where her death took place rather suddenly. The deceased lady was highly esteemed for her strict sense of duty whilst in Warrington, and her decease has created a painful impression amongst her many friends. It will be remembered that the late S:l(i.s.s Big,gin* was a fortunate',part-winner in the Calcuttasweepstiike of a'few years ago. . Au Old Parish Church Bell-Ringer. • After being *troubled for several years with a bronchial illness, Mr. Harry Tilling, of 45. Howley-lane, passed away on Tuesday evening in his fifty-first year. For 20 years he was a carpenter and joiner for 'Messrs. Jos. Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., where he was respected by all his workmates, whilst it is only about twelve Months ago since.: he gave up the position of ringer at the Parish Church, a -position he had held since he was fifteen years of age. He leaves tos.wsidow- and two.. daughters to mourn. their</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Warrington Musical Society's Concert.</head>
        <p>Warrington Musical Society's Concert.</p>
        <p>Lovers of 'music are, in-for an especial treat .in the concert to ho held in the Parr , 'WednesdaY, March 15th. rniScellaneOus pro- gramme has-been arranged and some notable artistes .are due to appear. Sir 'Charles V. Stanford's "Last •Po IT. j. .Taylor's The Eve. of. Waterloo," and Sir Edward Elgar's . " Carillert" are to-be executed, and-the chores rid Orel; estra 'are under the; caiddeterShiP 'or • Mr. 1:'. IT.. Crossley. ; • The plan .cipens on 'Mon- day next at Pearson's, Bridge-Street. As it is in aid of local, war funds your patronage is invited. • - ' ,•.' ••• Forthcoming 'Auction gales. Several auction sales are due to take place within the next week or so, -for M&amp;srs. A. 8.. Clive &amp; Co.' are to sell, on •Wednesday, • the: ' • 15th inst., at the Blue. Bell Inn, Ilersemarket- street, a varied lot: of effects, including free- hold property; Mr. 'Robert Lowe, on Saturday • next, at the Red,Lion inn, Penketh,:furnituro and other effects • removed from: an adjoining Belgian .home; and on • Wedne.sday,. the 15th inst., varied lots at Market Gate Sale Rooms; and 'Messrs. John- Mainwaring &amp; Co.; at the- -IBlue Bell Inn,, the effects of the .late Mrs. Daniel. Jones, of 51, • Wilson-Patten-street. Fur- • • ther particulars can be obtained . from crar advertisement .columns. • '•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Successful Whist Drive and Dance.</head>
        <p>Successful Whist Drive and Dance.</p>
        <p>A well-attended and sUcceSsful -whist drive and dance was held in the John •Crosfield Memorial Hall,. Bewsey,street, on Thursday .everiing, the forty-eight tables being occupied and some keen competition being displayed `fr. several handsome prizes. These successful *ere: -7-Ladies : 1, Mrs. Clayton; 2, Miss A. Messiter; 3, Miss L. Messiter. Gents: 1, Mrs: Critchley; -* Mr ,Gleave; 3; Mr. R. Jones.; • The whist drive and dance. Was in aid- of the Women. :Liberals" Sewing Fund; .and was organised by Mr. and • Mrs: J. Collins .and .Mr. and Mrs.- J. 'Per- cival. Mr. A. Jowett acted as M.C., and the prizes were distributed by Mrs. Tinnion. It may. be added that for ten successive Saturday evenings successful whist drives have been heft." in this hall, and a. substantial aniennt reelisel for . charity, for which great credit is due to the organisers. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>POLICE COURT MISSION.</head>
        <p>POLICE COURT MISSION.</p>
        <p>The Value Of Mission Work In Visiting Women.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Liverpool Diocesan Police Court and Prison Gate Mission was held yesterday at the Town Hall, Warrington, under the presidency of the Mayor. The Mayor com- inented on the valuable help and advice the magistrates received at Warrington, from the local missioner, Mr. W. R. Lairs. Despite the war he hoped the Mission would continue its sphere of usefulness, as the results had been so satisfactory.</p>
        <p>The Rev. P. W. Pheysey (organising secretary, Liverpool) in his annual report, calls attention to the fact that since the Probation of Offenders Act came into _operation all the Police Court missionaries on:the staff had been appointed pro-: bation' officers, and had consequently- received more official recognition than Was . preVionsly. accorded to .them.. There had been he Ireak: in. the continuity of the. iniportant-Work which Was. Carried dn in the Courts. - The -Work •of the Mission,' which was .Carried on with economy, ought to obtain as mud' -:financial stpportas - posSible, but at the end of 1915 there was a deficit of 5',147, which had to be. cleared off by a 'grant from the general fund, needed' for. other branches, of :the work. •</p>
        <p>Mr. Laws, in reviewing his work at Warring- ton, said it was in the visits to the homes of the people that the chief work was done,- where they could listen to both sides of the:question. Sir Wm. Scott Barnett pointed out that though the ' convictions for drunkenness were less,, partly accounted for by the fact that the popula- tion was less and the, drinking hours curtailed;) there was still a good deal of drinking going on which wanted curbing. It was no Use shutting our eyes to the fact that it was taking place amongst the women of the country in their homes. The police could not deal with such cases, but one way of helping wits by such societies having women viM.tOrs, who would go amongst them in their homes.</p>
        <p>Archdeacon Howson spoke of the value of the Mission. Such work gave men and women a chance of moral recovery. Those in trouble, need and di:stress were given a Aew start in life. There was something intmzely, inspiring in that thought of a new start in life, and a stimulus to try and do better. , - On the proposition of the Rev: Canon Steven- son, Seconded by Mr. C. E. Parker, a vote of thanks was passed to the Mayor and the speakers. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>USEFUL ENDEAVOUR.</head>
        <p>USEFUL ENDEAVOUR.</p>
        <p>"The Adventure Of Life in War</p>
        <p>And In Peace.</p>
        <p>LOOKING AHEAD.</p>
        <p>BY C. E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>War is a great uplifter. The monotony, the -aimlessness, and much of the frivolity of life have for the time being passed away. As Englishmen we feel for the moment that we are unified by a great purpose, imbued with a great ideal, anarching to a music. with which every hear,t is in tune, and everyone .keeps step. We have got something to think of. beyond the routine im- Posed upon us by the ordinary monotonous arnggle of life. Everything is full of interest; we are adventurers, feting, as never before, vital interests. English history, the world's history, is being made before our eyes, and each one can take his part in theimaking. But is not this present vivid life and its con- trast a proof, that our ordinary . national and individual life is to some extent a failure? The living interest which is ours in war cannot be with us in the normal condition of Our lives; and no one will contend that we must continue at war to maintain this. Yet our natures are restless, craving adventure, and the question is can we invest the normal times of peace with those conditions which will satisfy this natural °liging? Ever since the war began, some have "t*-11,, engaged •in the premature task of con- 6zoering and planning what sort of a peace we ure to impose upon Germany.</p>
        <p>Peace for Ourselves. Would we not be better employed in prepar- ing the peace we are going to make for our- selves? I mean, .we should thinkout the condi- tions under, which we are to •live in. our own homeland when peace is resumed, arrange what 'sort of peace is to be the lot of England. It 'should be a peace in which all, even the poorest, will have a chance of adventures of mind and spirit, of leading a life, of full and varied activity. It should be a peace in which all are bound by a common duty, .share in a common "opportunity, and in a 'common reward., We must be disciplined that we may work effec- tively. together. It must .not be a German dis- cipline. crashing individuality, but a discipline which leaves room and opportunity for-purpose- ful advancement, both for the nation and the individual. Peace has her 'adventures, her vie- lories, as well as war, victories in which there is no ghastly aftermath of the Wounded and the slain. Nor must the peace we look forward to be simply a ease of "as We were." It ought •to be *waive, not negative, constructive not 'destructive, conditioned so that it•will open to 'all the possibilifr of adventure for which the 'human spirit craves, and -which . war •in a 'ghastly way provides. If there is not this pos- 'sibility for healthy adventure for body and -mind, it will be sought in other. ways. Many 'have limitations imposed upon them by their 'circumstances, but more by their own sordid 'ideals of life, and such will be found among all 'lasses. These, having little other healthy out- ;let, seek it in times of peace by- excessive•eat- ing and drinking, liixurious living, empty show, ltild the like. •</p>
        <p>laeward of Those Who Dare. I:Dave we sufficiently considered that these ex- are often the ofitcome of a natural in- stinet gone wrong? Some, for example, drink to excess for excitement and .n .desire to explore -the possibilities of •life. - 'But are there not healthy outlets for this instinct in peace times, are there not opportunities for hazards, voices •calling far up the heights, "Excelsior"? Our world is full of interest, of 'mystery', of .gran- •deur, of difficulty to be surmounted, ealling.for 'high endeavour. Only to those ,who dare.-will 'the earth reveal her secrets and her, enchant- ments. Men and women with free energy, free thought, Can find an abundance of worthy out- let for their capacity, their longing for advert, lure, and have ne.need to stoop to that which is bestial and,ignoble. • How, can we refuse the thought of adventore 'and emotiow which, the. .universe.offers? -Why should we need the 6x- eeitement of war, op of bodilyk or mental. excess, when there is infinity before us to beexplored, its joys and wonders to be experienced, its risks to be taken, and all in our too brief span of `life?</p>
        <p>The Shame of the Drone. • The greatest need of democracy is not the acquisition of more power, .but the knowledge and ability to use the power it has. We have more freedom of action than- wisdom to use that freedom. If we realised our opportunity in times of. peace, if we appreciated our capa- 'city, if we realised the richness of the heritage to which the human race is heir, if all would resolve to take their part, we should not require a war to supply high adventure and useful endeavour. But everyone must feel the obliga- tion to be a helpful citizen, to attest in his class, should recognise tht, interests of all as well as those of _his own personality. Every man must do his bit. There will be no room for slackers after the war, no room for loafers, rich or poor. Every man who hereafter lives a fruitless life ought to be made to feel its shame. If the coming peace brings with it this spirit WO could show that England is worth fighting for and, if need be, dying for. We could trans- form, or rather re-create, another world of science, art, commerce, and social wellbeing, ahaned on larger, cleaner, and nobler lines. We •could remove the obstacles that stand in the way of national in-ogress: storm, conquer_ and transform the trenches of disorder and selfish- and develop •lives. in-so doing fuller and better Nobler Side of War. •</p>
        <p>- We have contrasted the possible adventures of Peace with those of war, but war. is something more than an opportunity for mere adventure. 'ln its ontward appearance, and in very deed, this red wrath of war is a ghastly evil. It is as if hell were turned loose upon earth, with Only the evil pas.sions dominant.. And yet, thank God, it has its alleviations, its splen- dours of sa.crifice, its effacement of self in the corapanionship,of de-ath and peril. Duke's son and cook's sou, high and lo*, master and ser- vant, rich and poor, saint and so-called sinner, stand side by side" as brothers, all exposed to like hazards, all 'competing as to who shall give most for home, for country, and for right. ,</p>
        <p>We few, we happy few; we baud of brothers: For he, to-day, that sheds his blood with me Shall be my brother—be he e'er so vile, This day shall gentle his condition; And gentlemen in England, now abed, Shall think themselves accursed they were not here, And hold their manhood cheap, while any speaks That fought with us upon Saint Crispin's day.</p>
        <p>There is something here. more than the love of adventure, something more than war fever. 'There are sacrifices and .sufferings such as have hardly been since the beginning of time. Men who have all that makes them. love life, and those who have nothing, fealessly face death for their country and their comrades. We see in Wonder and thankfulness, and it is only In the years of peace to come thattwe shall know i-t•rneans for the nation in its-character, its lijst°rY and its progress. :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>FUNDS FROM AFAR OFF.</head>
        <p>FUNDS FROM AFAR OFF.</p>
        <p>Warrington Petty Officer Raises £5O</p>
        <p>For -Soldiers.</p>
        <p>Desirous of helping to keep the South Lulea- thire Regiment supplied with tobacco, Mr. M. Macdonald, a Warrington man who is a Pett3 officer on board the Ss. Verdi, trading ,between New York and Buenos Ayres, recently alla.ngurated a concert at the latter port, by Ineans of which nearly .250 Was raised. Mr. Macdonald will be remembered particu- rlY us an ardent spirit in the Catholic Young "en's Society and United- liish League a few! Years ago, before entering the maritime ser- vice. As the financial result shows, the concert was erainentlY successful. It was delightful in every detail, even to the 'programme-7a pictiir- "quo affair like a Menu card :at. a" first-class ponbiLic dinner, the. flags* 4 the' in U.S.A. and' the n leo Kingdom being embodied n the pictorial. Scheme, with a golden eagle siirineunting both.: ,e34,Z; Aracdonald himself too* part in the con- '''. DY singin.g "Thora," and, his rendering is .benstinisti i t" 11 ,s Ica y described in a Buenos Ayres '-ewsPaPer.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J D. RENSHAW.. Killed in Action.</p>
        <p>Milling and Sons, and then of Messri. Pearson and Flowles. He was 20 yours of age and a well-known member of the St. Paul's Company of the Church Lads' Brigade; a Druid, and an old boy of St. -Ann's School. ' Mr. 'and Mrs. Renshaw have received many letters of .sympathy from the Officers and men of the 4th South Lancashire's. ' . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>QUICKER THAN LADIES.</head>
        <p>QUICKER THAN LADIES.</p>
        <p>Raddon Court Soldiers' Dexterity With The Needle.</p>
        <p>EXHIMTION OF HANDIWORK.</p>
        <p>• When the day ,of the suffragette, -dawns and the ladies 'turn out to do the work, of the world while their husbands stay-at home to mind the babies and. darn ,the stockjugs, • maybe some: of. the. industrious •• wounde&amp; soldiers, ,now -,in••the ,Raddon Court hospital,. Latchford. will estab- .liSli A school, whence knowledge and experience in the art. of knitting may be bestowed -upon ousted man. These.soldiers exhibited ;numerous. lots .of their work to visitors to the hospital. on.. Monday evening and, at the conclusion, there was 'hardly aPtive which went unsold. All sorts of sewing work, from th.e.making of a* double-heeled, sock for soldiers to the deli- cately-woven silk mat 'lay- before one on- the tables in close formation, for there was a deal of work there. - - •</p>
        <p>" The boys. like .doing this sort of thing," whispered the nurse guiding the Press repre- sentatives. " I don't mind confessing,. that after a little practice they are quicker .than ladies who have devoted all 'their leisure to it: They an pick up stitches almost 'before they have dropped. They lie for hours engaged 'So. And they don't talk nearly as much as we do! That's one of the secrets of their speed. • They set their minds on it and their nimble fingers race away. It's surprising!" It really was...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>In Honour of the Hospital.</head>
        <p>In Honour of the Hospital.</p>
        <p>One Tommy," -Private G. A. McLeod,' has what one may call an artistic conception of needlework. Into • canvas he -had inserted' stitch by stitch the flags Of the 'Allies and other loyal symbols in picturesque splendour, and in the centre inscribed the. words " Raddon Court." •</p>
        <p>Not all of the exhibition, however, was con- fined to needlework. Sergeant Bryan showed a water colour painting of • a panel in Christ Church, Latchford; and other: soldiers were proud- of their skill in fretwork, their choice of subjects being varied, and including Gurkas' knives and Nelson's Ship •Victory, with his famous message carved underneath. The handiwork was judged by• Mrs. Parr, Mrs. Henry Greenall, Mrs. Gibson Smith, and Mr. Leonard Davies. Lady Baripood Banner was prevented by indisposition from distributing the prizes, so Mrs. Parr took. her place,. remarking that the work of the soldiers was remarkably well done, and the variety was wonderful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Prize Winners.</head>
