      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>CRIMEAN VETERAN.</head>
        <p>CRIMEAN VETERAN.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Cummings Awarded Mililar</p>
        <p>Honours At Funeral.</p>
        <p>The Crimean veteran, Sergeant William Cum- mings, of 94, Wellfield-street, who died on Wed- nesday .of ,last week, was. buried with Military honours .at the Warrington Cemetery on Monday The band of the 2nd Battalion•Seuth Lanca- shire Regiment, under the conductorship:lL:4'W: R. E..Cahill,owas in attendance, and headed' the cortege to the cemetery, where, at the close of the service,, the buglers sounded the " Last Post." • •</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth, vicar of 'Bt. Barnabas', conducted the funeral service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1916.</p>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL HENRY EVANS.</p>
        <p>First Co-operative Society Emplo.\ cTo</p>
        <p>'Fall In The War.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Robert Evans; of 148, Lovely- lane, Warrington, have this week 'received offi- cial confirmation that their son, Corporal Henry Evans, has been killed in action: In- cluded in many messages of sympathy are letters from Second-Lieutenant -.Herbert T. Jones, of the same, regiment, and the Officer Commanding.. The former writes I wish to offer you my sincere sympathy in the great loss you have sustained by the death of your .son, _Corporal. H. .Evans. We shall feel his loss very much, and I can realise how sad it is for you and your. family. Yet there is one thing I' feel sure will help you in your sorrow, and that is the fact that hi, death was heroic and a sacrifice for his coun- try. Please accept my deepest sympathy."</p>
        <p>• 'the communication. from the deceased soldier's captain reads:-". '• "It is with very great regret that I have to</p>
        <p>CORPORAL HENRY-. EVANS. . Kink! in Action.</p>
        <p>inform you of the death of your son. Ho was killed on Sunda* night, February 20th, as he was returning over the parapet from some good work, and it may be a great relief to you to know that on the Sunday morning he attended Mass and Holy Communion, so you may. be Consoled, by the fact tkat he died fortified by the rites of the Holy ChurCh: He is a. great loss to me, as he was my best ,corporal?' The first sad tidings were conveyed to Mrs. Evans by Lance-Corporal Smart, of the same regiment, who on Thursday, Of last Week re- turned for a brief furlough from the firing line. A Warrington lad, he immediately went to inform Corporal Evans's mother of her great loss. , He told her that he helped to 'bury kis comrade' in a cemetery at Neuve -Chapelle., and that the grave is marked with a cross. • _</p>
        <p>. . Corporal Evans; who'. Wak only; 21 years of age, was an ardent ' worShipper at the St. Alban' Church, and as a• 'youth was educated at the -Ileathside,School. He was a shoemaker at the .Watrington''CO-operative 'SocietY, but .PriOr to enlisting on ' the 2nd. Of September, 1914; was a. salesman in the bObt and shoe de- partment. He was. araf tech to • the. Front on Sub' 18th of last year, and is.the.first employe to I* killed On action.-..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Mother Was In Betioged Lucknow.</head>
        <p>Mother Was In Betioged Lucknow.</p>
        <p>Mr,. Evans informed our representative that she is the daughter of an . ~i.nditn Mutiny Veteran. Born in India during those stirfing times, she was in the relief of_ IlticknOW.: At that time:she•was only three years of; age, but during her early childhood. her-father often used to recall the terrible scenes in the be- sieged city: • Mrs. Evans's father was attkhed to the 7th 'Royal Fusiliers, and was buried some years ago at—MostOn Cemetery, near 'Manchester, with full military honours.. • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL C. 11. NEWEOLD.</p>
        <p>Hockey And Tennis Player Wounded</p>
        <p>In The Head.</p>
        <p>Corporal Charles H. Newbold, of the King's Royal Ries, an ,employe of Messrs. Gartons, Ltd., and one of the soldier-sons of- Mr. New- bold, of Leahurst, Great Sankey, of the London and North-Western Railway, has been wounded</p>
        <p>CORPORAL C. IT. NEWBOLD.</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>in the head. The report from the base hospi- tal was that the Corporal was suffering front a scalp wound, which was not thought to be severe, and this opinion is borne out by the fact that he was moved almost immediately to England, and *as able to telegraph home his arrival at Dover safely, and that he was "going on fine." His many friends will hope he will continue to do so. _ _</p>
        <p>Corporal Newbold was educated at the War- rington Grammar School and joined the Forces along with his brother, who is now Sergeant F. C. Newbold, in the second month of the war. Both went to France last July and have been on active service ever since., Corporal New- bold, who is 20 years of age, belongs ,to the Latchford Hockey Club and tlfe -Penketh Tennis Club. He was recently home on a seven days' furlough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>THE WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Red Cross Work Costs £25,000</p>
        <p>Per Week.</p>
        <p>NEW WARRINGTON DETACHMENT.</p>
        <p>. Miss Sylvia Broadbent (vice-president of the Daresbury Division of the :British Red. 'Cross Society) on Saturday afternoon, 'in a' business- , , . like manner, submitted at the Town Hall to a large- au,clience, some very-interesting-facts con- cerning the, great and noble. work performed bS, ihe society which she represented. Miss Broad- bent's self-sacrificing effort ri for the British Red Cross Society, especially in connection with the Haddon Court Hospital, are well-known and highly appreciated: Saturday's gathering was the- occasion for the distribution; by Mrs.. Willis, of nursing Certifi- cates- to successful candidates at • the recent examination. held. under .the - auspices of this Order. A large number of ladies haVe become highly proficient in first-aid as well as nursing. that in consequence a Warrington Voluntary Aid Detachment of the Society has- already been formed, and at the present time only awaits the registration of the War Office to become an -absolute fact. To an audience composed mainly of those interested in Red Cross work,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>What The Society Has Done.</head>
        <p>What The Society Has Done.</p>
        <p>Miss Broadbent " announced some startling facts concerning the workings and upkeep for those of our wounded warriom attached .to the Society. She said that 'abroad and at home already there were'— . -</p>
        <p>- • , 1,000- motor amlmilances, ear, lorries, cycles, repair wagons, and motor soup-kitchens at work, costing •</p>
        <p>. , . . • E 200,000 for their Uplc_Cep for 12 months. - . • Four hospital trains .:running in France, each carrying 450 wounded. _. .</p>
        <p>12 vessels Sent to Dardanelles and Mesopo- tamia.</p>
        <p>25 hospitals in France, alt a And Egypt, con- taining 3,900 beds.</p>
        <p>~ . . 08.000 spent on St. Yohn. Brigade Hospital, Etaples, FranCe (920 be(ls).• . 6,500 surge Ons, riurseq. stroteher-bearers, los- pital orderlies, motor drivers, tte.., sent abroad. 30,400 items of information about missing and wounded men collected and passed on to rela- tives and friends. •</p>
        <p>Bed Cross work costs £25.000.per week. - One elaim set forth by Miss Broadbent with especial emphais was that the administration is very economical. •" the work." she said, "is costing over 5.',2 per niinute, whilst the cost of management at 'home. and abroad (excluding hospitals) represents 4.37 per cent. of the in- come, or less than lfgd. in the pound. Audited acCounts could always be obtained by the public at a nominal charge from headquarters."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>V.A.D.</head>
        <p>The V.A.D.</p>
        <p>. . With respect 'to 'the new Voluntary. Aid Detaehment for Warrington; Miss Broadbent said that in all probability it would be known as No. 150 Cheshire.- Officers who had already accepted the posts, were:-ConimaTidant, Miss joynSon; Medical Officer, Dr. Faulkner; Lady Superintendent, Nurse . : Phillips; Quarter:. Master, Mrs. 'Johnson; Pharmacist; Miss Hill. The object of the -Society was not' to provid? those who devoted their.-energies to a course of lectures for' the Piihiose of 'entertainment, but to be ;valuable asseWin alleviating the sufferers who had given all for their country's sake, .It vas anticipated that those who received the training would.gis-e:yieir seri-4.es' to the detach-. mentlin which- thOk'were; enrolled. Member were rightly expected to follow the orders- of their officers. '</p>
        <p>." It i omething-faither new to us women,". added Miss Broad Wilt, with, a 'smile, " to have to submit to' 51it I think_ we all .eel better for it: 'feel it iinportance. Officer had Oi&amp;C I 1 3', to f.,Tive orders ..,which 'they. ifeniiid; -ra,thait.iTying, and the execution sotnetinies entOled.4-Jiar4ship. We must leirkri that regulations aregnade to be. kept. For•in- sta'nce, no Member. of.,a detachnient is allowed to undertake any•-wqrk•without• the 'sanction of the commandant." -• Miss.-Broadbent remarked that she hoped pro • long 'further courses •Of lectures on first-aid. nursing. hygiene,. sanita- Hon .find a course, ot.:Red Cross cookery would be 'passible. , . • . ••• • , • • The Certificates. '.</p>
        <p>Mrs'. Willis. then distributed the eeitificat.ese the IT‘cipients Preliminary : MMa mes Birtle.4*, Bromley, Barmen. Fairelough,,Johnson, Mason, Robert- son and Taylor, .Misses Banahan; Brm*h, Diltton, B. J. .Pairelough, Holland, Harter, Hatton, 'llosker, Johnston, Kelsall, Knott. pownall; Pearson,. Sileock,. Stirrup and Wit:skill. • 7 . •• • ' •</p>
        <p>Advanced: Misses, Bennett, Corney, Collin, Mason, Orton, Witts,, Wright and Woodcock. An interesting feature of the proceedings was the presentation, on behalf of the members of the class, of a beautiful travelling bag to Nurse Davy, as a recognition of her sterling work as lecturer. , •</p>
        <p>Mrs. Fox (township loader for Warrington\ proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Mrs. Willis for the charming manner In which she had dis- tributed the certifietites, the vote being warmly accorded. • • • °</p>
        <p>The Rector suitably replied, and expressed his great admiration for the nurses who undertook such self-sacrificing Work. On the propositi6n of the Rev. L. J. T. Darwall, seconded'by i Mrs. Wheeler, a vote of thanks' was passed to the lecturer (Nurse Davy). the Examiner (Dr. Gilder) and the Clash Secretary (Mrs. Merry).</p>
        <p>, Letters of regret for non-attendance were re- ceived from: The Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock). Colonel Maggridge. Major- Fitzgerald, Greenall. Mrs. Fletcher, Mrs. Dakin, Mrs. Bowers, Mr. Arthur 136`nnett and Major Adams</p>
        <p>PRIVATE F. N. DIXON.</p>
        <p>Wounds In Ann And Side, Rut Progressing Favourably. Private Frederick N. Dixon, whose wi</p>
        <p>„ . resides at root, has been :Wounded in the right arrival-Id_ i(le by an explosive bullet, whilq seiving i*Franee. • • . , Froin the Jlopit&amp;il it I t ipes 'Private',.Pixon hns written t•iffying In is progreSsing favourably. Private Dixon, Who is 22 years of age,. was empl63 ed'bY the Manchester Ship Canal Co He enlisted soon after the outbreak .of war, ,and has been at' the 'Front slne iiiy. As a boy, he attended; the National S'dhool, Church-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>CURRENT TOPICS</head>
        <p>CURRENT TOPICS</p>
        <p>FROM</p>
        <p>STAGELAND.</p>
        <p>By " FOYER."</p>
        <p>Royal Court. The ever-popular "Dick Whittington "has again been delighting patrons of the Royal Court Theatre, and for jollity, and merriment has rarely been beaten for a provincial touring company;': •</p>
        <p>•As a dashing principal boy, Miss Edna Latonne is par excellence,- her impersonation of a British "Tommy "bring especially fine, and the " Dreadnought " scene is both effective and novel. - •</p>
        <p>wish Miss -Latonne "bon voyage" in her forthcoming trip to America and South Africa. She should create a favourable impression on the other side:</p>
        <p>Harry Russell's pantomime Cinderella" is booked for next week, which shows that this class of entertainment is by no means spent in spite of the season.</p>
        <p>I hear a good 'account. of It, especially from a vocal point of view. Harry Russell, by the 'way, at the conclusion of next week's perform- ance, goes back to London to perform under a big syndicate, his popular act with a con- ductor. Harry is always a favourite in the Metropolis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Hippodrome.</head>
        <p>Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>• The Grace Bros., who have this-week been creating thrills, at our, variety theatre; are natives of Barcelona,. and have been, on the music halls for many years, having the honour of appearing before the King of Spain, in Madrid, in 1908. .</p>
        <p>It took me some time to understand their broken English when I interviewed them on Monday evening, but eventually learned that they had toured in India, North and South America, .South Africa, France, New Zealand, 'Australia and Germany.</p>
        <p>The artiste who performs . sensational evolu- tions upside down on top of a lorig pole, has had ,some miraculous escapes from death; for at one of ' the principal • Parisian halls the pole brae, precipitating him amongst the orchestra, with rather serious results to several people. .</p>
        <p>The elder Of the two brothers has partici- pated in three bull fights in his native country, episodes which, by the way, have not always terminated successfully for himself, as. his niany healed up wounds testify.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. A. Jackson, the genial manager, has had many improvements made at the Hippo- drome since he took charge in Warrington, and he tells me the new entrance to the stalls is to he opened on Monday next. This is a:decided especially for the elder patrons, who pre- viouslY had to mount many steps before being' comfortably settled. , .</p>
        <p>Premier place on the bill next week is allotted to the Sutcliffe Family, Scotland's popular entertainers. They have been touring under the Macnaghten management for some little time, and from preliminary notices of Pie provincial Press their turn is both original and .clever.</p>
        <p>A farcical sketch entitled "Bedfellows" is also included in. the week's attractions, and 1 hear it is:a scream. I dO.-not. mean to miss this sketch,. foraccording to reports it is a splendid pick-w, up in these time of gloom and darkened streets</p>
        <p>Star kinema. • • - • `.`..After the Ball," a British film, and "The Girl of Yesterday'," 'have Proiedl'great attrac- lions. this 'week at the .Star 'Kinema house, the latter film featuring 'Miss Mary Pickford— trdly `,•the " ',.;siveetheart,"7 •and her rendering Of a delightful part leaves we with happy recollections, and adds still another triumph to 'her Already long list of notable successes.</p>
        <p>_"Sei-en Sisters," featuring Marguerite Clark, a•favaous Players principal and, a lady renowned in th 4 cinema World, is being screened the first •three 'days -next week. • .•</p>
        <p>- A. Comedy film, described". itti :a captivating .vision of romantic youth, is based on a COnti- nental marriage custom. There are many diverting incidents, and the scenery Is extremely picturesque. You are in Arcadia whilst this picture is being shown. . .</p>
        <p>Comedy, then tragedy; 'and you have plenty' of the - latter in " 'Laza," the premier movie booked fcir the remaining three days: Paulina Frederick plays the title part, and we who saw her portrayal in "The Eternal City" may be assured that " Zaza" will be worth witnessing.</p>
        <p>Miss Frederick is both an actress and voe,alist. Born_ in Boston, U.S.A., she made her first appearance on the stage when eighteen years of age. Prior to .resigning from the legitimate stage to become a permanent famous player, she was leading lady in "'Joseph and .11is Brethren," a play which some time ago had such a triumphant tour in the States and Canada.,</p>
        <p>I am told the greatest picture of all time is due to appear shortly at this hall. to wit, "Carmen." The sum of 250,000 dollars was offered to the Lasky Co. by a big syndicate for the exclusive rights—and refused. I shall have more. to say on " Carmen " next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Olympia, Liverpool.</head>
        <p>Olympia, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>Since the first night's performance, " Joy- land " has been run exactly to time, a feature muck appreciated by out-of-town patrons who desire to catch trains home.</p>
        <p>The show runs for another fortnight, and those who desire to see a performance never be- fore staged in the provinces will do well to book seats.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>CONCERT AT THE WHITECROSS MILITARY HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT THE WHITECROSS MILITARY HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>The spacious dining hall at the WhitecrOss Military Hospital was • filled to its utmost capacity on Monday •evening, the occasion being a concert promote.d by Staff-Sergeant P. Wink- ley, of the R..A.M.C., for thb benefit of the in- mates of the hospital. The programine opened with a song by Mr J. B. Poole, entitled "Drake Goes West." It delighted the audience, and Mr.. Poole had to respond to an encore by. singing "The Yeoman's Wedding Song." During the sevening he sang two other songs, viz., "Up from Somerset" and "The Devout Lover." Mr. T. Hudson was heard thud ;I..r.tage in his rendering of the songs WI en -Yoa •me Home." "Somewhere a Voice is Calling,"' "Thorn," ..and "The Sun- shine.•of Your Smile." A duet, The. Moon Rath.' Raised Her Lump Above" was also enjoyed. Miss Elsie Hewitt gained unstinted applause' for her rendering of "Dear Heart and "The Rosary," while she also accompanied Mr. Poole in a duet ."Excelsior." Master Davies, of the Parish 'Church Choir, who I)os.4esees an excellent 'voice, Sang " Swal- lows," "When the Birds go North Again," and "Roses." Mr. Anderton proved himself -a fine banjo player, and gave several selections, while. Mr: 11. Simpson had the house in uproars with his humorous selections. _</p>
        <p>At the close of the concert Mr. W. Loxham, master of the Workhouse, proposed a vote of iiiankS to the promoter of the concert, and to the artistes, -several of whom had journeyed f tom Manchester at considerahle.ineonvenieneC to themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>NEW SHERIFFS.</head>
        <p>NEW SHERIFFS.</p>
        <p>The following 'Sheriffs were appointed by the King for the year 101 G</p>
        <p>.Laucashire.—,Mr. ' -Percy ,Tolin • of Ila.mpsfieldi'Grange-over•-Sandg: Hibbert is a Ineniber of the. Lancaster County 'Connell. for •Cartmel; in which division Grange is situated. Cheshire.—Mr. Frederick William Wignall, of The Rookery, Tattenhall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Occupations for Maimed Soldiers and</head>
        <p>Occupations for Maimed Soldiers and</p>
        <p>Sailors.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Graham Wood, High Sheriff of Lancashire, 4, Cambridge-road, Southport, who is interested in a scheme for finding employ- ment for disabled soldiers aml sailors has just published a list of occupations which might be filled by those who have suffered in the jar. Ft is surprising to find how many occupations, even in what may be called dangerous trades, could- be filled efficiently by injured men. For instance,* they could be watchinen. and Ma* other things- in a colliery. Similarly there are -jobs for them in the building; shipping, or any trade where one cares to look with proper intentions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>THAT ODD DAY.</head>
        <p>THAT ODD DAY.</p>
        <p>Meaning Of Leap Year And Curiosities Of Ca!endar.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY'S DISTINCTION.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR MOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>Everybody knows the . irregularity of the calendar, and the odd arrangement which gives February an extra day once ereky four years. It; was instilled in the days of .our youth in the form of a precious bit of .poetry, which it is to be presumed that none of us have for- gotten. 'thirty days have September, April, June and November;., All the rest harp thirty-one, February twenty-eight. alone, ;Except in Leap Year I opine, When that ,month's days are twenty-nine.</p>
        <p>Of what. lies behind the vagaries of the cal- endar a long hiAory might be written. In the present brief article We will set -forth few of the simple, and interesting facts. The first thing to learn is that Nature has al year of her own. If the earth travelled round the sun in $65 days we could. make a calendar, of 365 days. and the seasons would always fit, in accurately. But Nature does things in her own *ay and without regard to our- conveni- ences. Her year is a period -of 365. days, Or thereabouts, and we have to :do our -best :to arrange an almanac that will meet the case.</p>
        <p>If we plan that our year shall have 365 days, it will be a..quarter of a. day too short, a:nd if of 366 days, it will be three-quarters of a day too long. In one case we should be losing at the rate'of three day ls in four years, and in the other we should he gaining upon Nature .at the rate of one day. It would never do to get wrong With Nature's years, and so we fall upon the plan ,of having three years of .365 days and one year of 366 .days; which makes 1;461 days in four years. This comes pretty close to Nature, but does irt exactly fit the case, as we shall have to show further. On.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>HISTORY OF THE CALENDAR.</head>
        <p>THE HISTORY OF THE CALENDAR.</p>
        <p>The ancient : Egyptians laid the foundations of the calendar we Use. They arranged for twelve months of .30 days, and added five sup-. plkmentary days. This was splendidly accurate, but they made no allowance for the fraction of a day we "have mentioned; and in the course of ,centuries they found themselves in winter at a Period which the calendar assured them was mid-summer.</p>
        <p>Some interesting things might be written upon the Greek calendar, but as the Roman plan became the basis of all modern ones, we will confine our attention to that.</p>
        <p>The Roman calendar, which tradition ascribes to Romulus, consisted of. ten- months, which began with March and ended with December. We can still trace the ancient Roman names in Our September, October, November and Decem- •ber, which mean seventh, eighth,- ninth and tenth. This calendar, however, only allowed for 304 days, and:we do not know how the remain- ing days were arranged. Under..Numa two JnOnths were added, January at the beginning and February at the end. The months had 2; and '3O days alternately, which gave a year of 354 days, but as odd numbers were regarded as lucky, and even ones unlucky; a day .was added and made a total of 355. This left the year ten days too short, and to remedy the defect an, additional Month was inserted every two years. This extra month had alternately 22 and 23 days. It was not, howeVee, firmly arranged, and as the Popes had the handling Of the calen- dar, this extra month gave them opportunities to he kind or unkind. They shortened the year ,pite their enemies and lengthened it to suit their friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>JULIUS CIESAR'S HAPPY EFFORT.</head>
        <p>JULIUS CIESAR'S HAPPY EFFORT.</p>
        <p>. . Things get into.-suCh a bad and confused state that Julius Ctesifr decided to take the matter in hand. .Raving conquered Wail and Britain. and got .a number of other small matters off his hands, he turned his attention to astronomy and calendar-making. The task was intricate for a ~,olaier,'ltnd he culled to hissheltiOn.Alexandr,ian astronomer named Sosigenes. This able and amiable. gentleman asserted that they were run- ning 'ninety days behind time, a discrepancy which caused the almanac to tell, them it was illitl,Winter When the spring flowers were telling quite another story. For an up-to-date institu- tion. like the Roman "Empire to be running three Inotiths slow was intolerable to: the doughty Julius, and in his most imperious manner he proceeded to put things. right by proclaiming that the current year (45 u.c.) would Consist of 445 days.</p>
        <p>This record year over, Father Time and the Roman calendar began to work evenly, and they have kept pretty close ever since. Julius fixed the length of the year at 3651 days, and planned the leap year of 366 days as we have it •at present. - The months were to have 30 and 31 days alter- nately, except February, which was to have 29 and 30 in the leap year.' This was just about as good as anything could be, and it .was only fit- ting that such a good calendar maker as Julius should have a month called after him. We celebrate Julius Cirsar and his work every time we mention July. A ROYAL MEDDLER.</p>
        <p>But there have always been jealous and med- dling people in the world, and one of them appeared when Augustus came to the throne. This important gentleman was troubled that Julius shduld have an honour which he had not. Ile therefore decreed that the eighth month of the year should be known in future as August. Alas! for the little-mindedness of the great! *July had 31 days and August only 30. But this could be got over; and so by royal decree it was enacted that August ' should have 31 days. February, already the shortest month, was de- prived of a day in order that August might have 31. This made matters eevn, but it left three consecutive months (July, August and 'September) each with 31 days. A little more meddling and the last four months emerged with their present number of days. . The Augustan' calendar has been in use ever since, .and is therefore just .as old as the Chris- tian . era. Only one defect showed itself, and this was a defect which belonged to the ar- rangements of .Julius Caesar. Nature's year, as we have intimated, is an odd quantity. The calculation of 3651 days proved to be 11 minutes and*l4 seconds too long. Though a very small miscalculation, it is a'discrepancy.which begins to tell in the course of a century, and when astronomers looked into the matter in the 16th century it was found that the almanac was at least 10 days ahead of Nature. Astronomers had called attention to the point for genera- tions, but it was not until Pope Gregory XIII. intere,sted himself that anything was done. By the issue of a papal bull he announced, that October sth, 1582, should be called October 15th, so as to enable the. calendar to catch up with the sun. In order that the divergence should not recur it was arranged that the centurial Years should not be leap years. except such as were divisible by 400. In prac- Hee this meant that 1600 Was a leap year, 1700, 1800 and 1900 were common years, and 2000 will be a leap year. This is ingenious, and Comes as near to per- fection as we can hope ti get. lint sbnie Statis- tical fiend has produced, figures to show that in 3,000 years we shall have accumulated an error of a whole day. .We will try not to worry about it. Possibly there will be wisdom enough in the world to deal with an odd day even when ,we are_ gone.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>SPARE GAMES.</head>
        <p>SPARE GAMES.</p>
        <p>Have You Any You Can Send To Our</p>
        <p>Boys ?</p>
        <p>Dear Editor,—l want to -put just a few lines in your valuable paper to ask the townspeople of Warrington if they have any spare games they could send out to us, such as dominoes, "draughts, or'a Rugby fobtball. It is miserabh when you have nothing to pass away the few moments we are allowed. I•am pleased to say that I receive “The Examiner" each week, and the:Warrington lads who are with me look, for- tard• to .its arrival. . tours truly, . . . • Driver,lo.SEPH HOPWOOD, • • . B Battery. „ .130th Brigade, 28th Division R.P.A., Mediterranean Force. • •</p>
        <p>EN-o doubt many of ogr readers have just what these men at the Front need. If so, send them at once!—Ed.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Allotments.</p>
        <p>The Lymin Council advertisement respecting land at Sow Brook for this-purpose recalled a pleasant experience which I had in a district of whose weekly paper, I had 'charge for some time. The local 'authorities added horticultural , classes to their syllabus of technical education, ,and placed a'plot ()liana at-the disposal of the students for the,time being; The experiment proved .an immediate and a .permanent success,. 'Classes and gardens increasing and multiplying. continually.. Moreover; the .whole neighbour- hoed was infected, and arrangements had.to be .made for allotments for older Men who did not wish .to go through the course .of instruction in theory. Thus did 'inanY an unsightly "wilder- ness and ,solitary place become "glad for them," and •deserts. were made t 0.." rejoice and blossom as the rose."</p>
        <p>'Sunday Fuiterals.• • The fnneral-of• FM old Forester here on Sunday afternoon reminded meof considerable areasboth in Lancashire and Yorkshire where such burial on ,that day would have been impossible. Time waS, of course, when it was otherwise. • The movement- against the practice began with the local clergy; ministers of all denominations agreed that they would condUct no ',more funerals on Sundays, and, although this 'pro- voked resentment'and hoStility in some quarters, they persisted in their determination until the public generally were brought to see how reasonable it was from their point Of view, and hoW slight the inconvenience was after , all. Eventually, everybody wondered why they had ever had Sunday funeral iat all. .</p>
        <p>A "Light " Charge. • The penalty imposed on Fair folk for dis- obedience of the Order as to restricted lighting brought to my mind the favourite joke of an old colleague, who repeatedly referred to his gas 'bill as the "Charge of the Light Brigade?' He expected us to laugh every time; and I sup- pose it must have been considered pretty good, for it Ultimately got into "Punch."</p>
        <p>I" Take Heed To Thy Feet." This, the order of the day, reminded me of a misllap to two keen officials which afforded an onlooker malicious satisfaction. The proprietor of a paper on whicht was the time went dawn early one morning to attend to some urgent I business. Tie was in the front 'shop, the only I comniunication With which from the works .was by A private doorway, along the lower part of I which passed the heating apparatus pipes. He was naturally startled when two men burst in. lat the front and rushed through the shop. Recognising the factory inspector and his assist- ant, he shouted to warn them of the obstruc- tion, but that only served to confirm their sus- picions that young people were being illegally employed in the works. They therefore hastened forward, and both fell headlong over, the pipes. wounding shins as well as dignify. they were very angry,. of course, and insisted that the offending pipes would have to be removed; but it was quietly and effectively retorted, not only that they .had disregarded ,timely caution, but that the Way they had gone was strictly private, and never used by the workers. The crestfallen, companions in misfortune were laelpleSs for once. " Bullock's Three-mile Swim."</p>
        <p>The incident •recorded under this sufficiently- explanatory headline in last week's "Farm. and Garden," set me thinking once more of one of ply old districts, through which ran a canal,, intersected by locks in a somewhat unusual _</p>
        <p>degree. The frequenk frestarting of heavily- laden boats 'necessibited by thek) conditions was hard .on the horses :employed, and it was not unusual for one of these animals to fall into, the thiter under the undue strain. Occasionally one livould become entangled and .drowned, but generally the distressed horse would be 'speedily released, and would swim along to a convenient point, where it could regain the towpath, whence it would be led back to the boat to patiently resume the journey.</p>
        <p>Romany Rye'. • The story of the nomads who were sUrprised whil living the simple life at the 'expense of the Bewsey Tennis glub, turned my thoughts. upon the gipsy. tribe at large. What are these, ; and *hence Came they? • I have read several authorities on the Subject, and the consensus of modern opinion as to their.origin seems to be that they "came from beyant Hindostaa," as "The Tipperary Christrening " song phrases it— that, :to be precise; they are of Sudar descent.' Grellan, the linguist, considers that quite 48 per cent. of. the words of the, gipsy lingo are either purely Hindostanee, or are closely related to that language. In his remarks on Popular antiquities, Branduvows the conviction that the first gipsies fled from Asia in 1408 and 1409; driven out.by the cruel.-Tamur Beg, when, ia his attempt to establish Mohammedanism, he slew half a million natives. The remnant mak- ing for Europe, and especially this country, by way tf Egypt, received the name of gipsies. Presuming this pretty general belief to be in- deed well-founded, it is somewhat singular how. for 500 years, these wanderers have kept themselves a. peculiar people, the Jews being , the only dispersed race comparable with them in this regard. "Golf Link Linguists."</p>
        <p>The foregoing allusion to an eminent linguist sends me in turn to this phrase in " Vigilant's " concluding paragraph in last week's "Gossip." Our old friend Dougal', the Scottish Shepherd, whom I have quoted once before, is unable to appreciate golf. Says he: "Ye pit a wee ba' doon on a wee heap o', sand, an' then let bash at it wi' a club. An' suppOsin' as ye do manage tae catch it a decent skein, ye've just got tee pick up yer bag o sticks an' gang stravagin' aff tae where ye pit the ba,' an' as likely as no' ye never find it. That's no' just terrible funny tae my way o' thinkin'. An' the language. Man, it's terrible! I'll no' be for denyin' that now and then when somethin' catches me unawares I mightna' in the heat o' the moment let slip a wee curse just to ease my mind, but, as sure as I'm standin' here wastin' my time, if I was tan Start an' be a gowfer I'd give myself up for lost." • It's the " language " we're dealing with par- ticularly for the moment. To be sure, few but</p>
        <p>the hopeless duffers allow themselves habitually to indulge in it. One reads, for example, 'that a military man, who could swear better than he could play golf, was flattered one day to find quite a crowd of well-dressed strangers follow- ing him around. "I hope," he said, gallantly. "that I play well enough to reward you?" "Oh, it isn't that, niajor," replied one; "we came out to listen." The Clown of Yore.</p>
        <p>I suppose the superior person of a later generation will.deprecate. it as hopelessly out-of- date, but I freely confess that I share "Foyer's" longing for a revival of the old-fashioned clowft of the pantomimes of our youth. One of the best exponents of the part I personally remem- ber was George Barrett, brother of the more famous Wilson Barrett. Moreover, the same order of clown invariably accompanied the Jack-in-the-Green itinerants entertainers, who ,delighted the youngsters. of London until com- paratively recent times. As a kiddie there, enjoyed these performances quite 57 years. agb..</p>
        <p>Spoonerisms I have an acquaintance who is given to amus- ing slips of the peculiar kind, identified by the name of the clergyman who became famous by publicly makiiig the startling confession, "I have a half-warmed fish in my bosom." My friend confided to me the other day that he was "collecting soldiers for wounded cigarettes," though, sooth to say, he is capable of more genuine spoonerisms than that.</p>
        <p>Nothing Like "Cheek." "The cheek of them!" was the exclamation of the tennis players when they came upon the tramping couple enjoying themselves in the club' pavilion. Well, one cannot help admiring that sort of thing nowl and again; I mean there are times when one envies a more self-confident fellow his possession of that quality. I hare just read in the columns of a contemporary a review of the "Life Jottings of an Old Edin- burgh Citizen," by Sir J. 11. A. Macdonald, Lord Justice Clerk, just published, and I found therein a most entertaining story of how ha himself got wherever •he liked : during the Diainond! Jubilee procession. , Be had on tho plumed roeked • lika and epaulettes .of the adjutant-general of the Scottisli•Royal Archer. Upon this he traded; cordons welted •liker.snow before him; and such, were the alien splendOurs, a his array that lie never even had to use the formula he had prepared to meet a verbal challenge. " Plees I do vish to go to ! de Forin Offeece."</p>
      </div>
