      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1916.</p>
        <p>- I ; l'' .r: ''..., ''. _„... „............0. I sTanw. 171...'"' -.....- • -- _ _ _. -. . TTRICT Kr -.....-t ',' 3 i':',.....----. .._ _. 7....,....., .:._............, ~.. .•.</p>
        <p>LYMM</p>
        <p>Y.M.C.A. Concert—The concert organised by members of the Lynim Branch of the Y.M.C.A. on Monday evening 'realised the sum of £l3 12s. • Of this amount,, £l2 has been allotted to the Y.M.C.A. and £1 12s. to the C.B.M.S. Recreation Ruts.</p>
        <p>Sergeant J. H. fkA osto n .—Sergeant J. H. Moston, of the local " Terriers," whom we reported, last week, suffering from nervous breakdown in th'o Canadian htilitary Hospital,"has this week been transferred to the Military Hospital, Belfast. He is progressing very satisfactorily, and ex- pects soon to rejoin his regiment.</p>
        <p>Musical Successes.—At the annual local dis tribgtion of certificates and prizes of Trinity College of Music, held at the small concert room of St. George's Hall, Liverpool, on Wed- nesday, the following awards were made to pianoforte pupils of Mr. William Cooke, organist of Lymm Parish Church :—Local Ex- hibition value 19 98., Senior Pianoforte Honours: Miss,,Marie Hawley, of Warrington Silver Medal, Junior Pianoforte Honours: Miss llilda Gandy, of Runcorn. Book Prize, Inter- mediate Division: Miss Constance Wood, of St. Helens, These awards are. made for highest marks of the year in their respective divisions. The Lady Mayoress distributed the prizes.</p>
        <p>Confirmed Under Fire.—The Rector, in th Month's Parish Magazine, referring to the ' approaching confirmation service, says.:— - " Perhaps it would not be out of place ' to mention as an encouragement to older people who have for some reason or other. not been confirmed what has been taking place among our men in Prance and Belgium duripg the past few months with regard to this rite, confirma- tions have been taking place regularly in barns and empty buildings, and even in the open fields. A description I had of one of these a few weeks ago was very impressive; men leaving the trenches to receive at the Bishop's hands this blessing and then returning straight to the firing line again. It does not require much imagination to appreciate what this has meant , to many of our brave soldiers, and not only to those who have been confirmed, under, such unusual circumstances themselves, but even still more to those who already confirmed have been privileged to witness the same. Surely, then, we at home, in comparative safety, should value these blessings when they are brought to I our very doors."</p>
        <p>son's words would be taken to heart. He (the, speaker) was glad to. be amongst them, and he hoped that many more pleasant evenings would be spent. As a Member of the Heatley Platoon; he was pleased with the noble work carried on .by Captain Williamson and his staff.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion • of the entertainment CaPtain. Williamson proposed a vote of thanks to the artistes, who had so generously come forward with 'their services.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rogers, on behalf of the artistes, replied that they were glad to help in any shape or form.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Moston then proposed a vote of thanks to Captain Williamson for the splendid way he had looked after the'platoon's interests. This was seconded by the audience, urho.sang "For ho's a jolly good fellow." - The proceedings closed with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>LIGHTING REGULATIONS.</head>
        <p>LIGHTING REGULATIONS.</p>
        <p>Accidents Through Colliding With Lamp And Railings.</p>
        <p>Stygian darkness lies over the village at nights, and the new lighting regulations have already been the cause of minor injuries to the inhabitants. In consequence of the greetings by lamp-posts, iron ralilings, &amp;c., local oilmen and sellers of bicycle lamps have been reaping a rich harvest, for in the evening on traversing the streets one often meets a passer-by with shining lantern. One wit has been going about acknowledging the pedestrians as follows:</p>
        <p>"Dark night, constabfe." Mrs. T. W. Field, wife of Sergeant T. W. Field, D.C.M., of Newfields, sustained on Mon- day •night a rather nasty cut over the eye through colliding with a lamp-post. She is confined to her home suffering from shock. Miss- Lucy Blease, of Newfield, also came in contact with some -iron railings near her home, sustaining rather severe cuts on her face. Several other inhabitants have come to grief also, including our worthy Clerk to the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Y.M.C.A. CONCERT.</head>
        <p>Y.M.C.A. CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The Lymm Hut Fund Now Over £2OO.</p>
        <p>Visions of a •Lymm Y.M.C.A. hut seem to be growing nearer realisation each day, for the proceeds of a successful concert held on Monday evening will help materially to provide the necessary funds. The Congregational Sunday School was totally inadequate to accommodate the patrons, and those fortunate enough to obtain admission voiced the opinion that seldom has an entertainmentbeen sothoroughly enjoyed as that set forth. One cannot express too high an appreciation to those artistes who came for- ward without thought of monetary reward, for thris noble object, and thanks are also due to the organisers for the efforts put forth to make the concert so successful. Individual solo efforts, recitations and humorous items were rendered in capital style by the Misses M. Adams, F. Little, G. Dixon and B. Wright, Mrs. C. Lewis. and Messrs. A. Schofield, T. Hill, j. H. Brazen- dale and J. Hewitt, all of •which called. for hearty applause. The recitation entitled "The looker-on" by Master E. Wibberley, was also of especial notice. The school children of Statham School, under the direction of several ladies of the Y.M.C.A. committee, rendered in masterly style part songs, their sweet singing and grace- ful action being highly commendable. Special mention is given to those children who had saved their humble coppers to purchase choco- late, which was sold during the interval as a means to swell the funds. Nearing the Goal.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the evening's entertain- ment, Councillor Gotobed (chairman of the local Y.M.C.A. movements) announced that he was pleased to .say that the funds had now reached the 'handsome figure of over £2OO, and he held high hopes that within a short time the neces- sary .5250 would have been reached. "It was little short of marvellous,"eontinued the chair- man, "considering the short time the movement had been Non foot that such a sum should have been realised, especially considering the many ails that had been made on behalf of other objects." To those who had so generously con- tributed he offered his best thanks. He also wished to express his sincere appreciation to 4-hose artistes who had helped to make the enter- tainment such a success, and he hoped their efforts had been as pleasing to themselves, as • they had to the listeners.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H.Brazendale, on behalf of the artistes, Quitably responded, and said that they were pleased to be able to do anything on behalf so noble a cause. Amongst the large audience were several wounded soldiers ,from the Brook field Military Hosital. The proceedings closed with the singing of the National Anthem..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>VOLUNTEER SUPPER.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER SUPPER.</p>
        <p>Platoon Commandant Deprecates Members' Irregularity At Drill.</p>
        <p>About forty members and friends of the Lymm 'Detachment, Volunteer Regiment, sat down on Thursday evening, at the Railway Hotel;-*Reatley,,...to enjoy_an excellent repast._ provided by Mr. T. Heaven, and to aPpreciate a concert of high-class vocal merit. Several local artistes of note contributed items, calling forth unstinted applause from the apprecia- tive gathering. The songs and recitations were rendered in effective and praiseworthy manner by the following. artistes: Corporal R. Hillier, and Messrs. Rogers, Waterworth, Owen, Gib- bon, Heaven and G. Parker. Prior to the concert .Captain G. B. William- son said that he was pleased to see such a good assembly. It gave him great pleasure to notice that the Church Parade of Sunday last was so well attended. This was as it should be. He was glad to announce that very shortly a shooting competition was to take place for the members and that handsome prizes for successful competitors had been forthcoming. It had been just a little source of regret to him to notice that for some time past the usual drills had not been so well attended as in the past. At a time like the • present, continued Captain Williamson, it was desirable that all should do their utmost for their King and country, and he hoped that his ,remarks would be taken to heart. They were recognised as part of His Majesty's Forces, and it was their bounden duty in the present crisis to be iirepared to the hilt against emer- gencies. Couneillor Gould, who also addres-;ed the _members, said that he hoped Captain William-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>NOT ZEPPS.</head>
        <p>NOT ZEPPS.</p>
        <p>Failure Of Gas Deludes The Inhabitants Of Statham.</p>
        <p>A NAPHTHALINE STOPPAGE.</p>
        <p>The good people of Statham felt, on Monday night of last week, that Zepps were in the offing, for :their gas-lights gradually dimin- ished.until not even a speck of yellow adorned the -burners.</p>
        <p>But the Zepps failed to torment them, these dragons of the night not having ventured out. Councillor R. W. Parker, therefore, when he attended the monthly meeting of the Lyn_im Council on Monday, wanted to know the why and the wherefore of this "false alarm." He recalled that the Council had passed a resolution instructing the Gas Manager that, if there were any intimation of the approach of aircraft, he should reduce the pressure of the gas. On Monday of last week the gas went out entirely at Statham, and he should like to know the reason why, seeing that there were no Zeppelins about. Councillor S. Critchley said that when the lights went out he took it that there was going to be an air raid. The Gas Manager (Mr. T. H. West) explained that the failure of the gas supply had nothing to do with Zeppelins. .It was caused by a naphthaline stoppage in the main, and this was entirely different to the reduction of the pressure when aircraft was approaching. The lowering of the lights on these occasions would be -effected throughout the district in three minutes, but the naphthaline stoppage took longer. Generally speaking, they were troubled very little with these interferences. In some places as many as a hundred com- plaints had been received, and, derefore, they in that district were very fortunate. If any further complaints were made in Statham, however, they would be attended to immedi- ately. Councillor C. E. Cowper: The explanation is perfectly satisfactory. It was stated in the Roads Committee's minutes that 1,200 circulars would be sent out to the district, warning householders to turn off the gas at the meters as soon as they were warned of the approach of hostile aircraft.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Firemen To Stand By.</head>
        <p>Firemen To Stand By.</p>
        <p>Councillor Parker intimated that - members of the Fire Brigade would be called out and stand by ready to act in case of emergency. The Chairman (Councillor R. H. Gotobed) said he was sure members of the Fire Brigade would be as patriotic as anyone, and would place their services willingly at the disposal of the community. '• (Hear, hear.) Councillor Cowper Walks in the Narrow Way</p>
        <p>Councillor Cowper mentioned that at the last meeting of the Council he referred to the ques- tion of accounts: Seeing that he had been contradicted as to a statement he made, he should like to ask whether a certain sum had been placed to the gas account. - The Chairman interposed with the remark that he did not know what good purpose Coun- cillor Cowper expected to achieve by bringing this question before the Council so often. He knew very well how the finances stood. Councillor Cowper said Councillor Groves had not been telling him the truth. Councillor W. G. Groves replied that th, Clerk (Mr. W. Mullard) had told them exactly how they stood. Councillor Cowper: I play the straight game, but a lot of others don't. Councillor J. H. Holt thougV it was very unwise for the Council to discus these matters unless they had. facts and figures before them It had gone forth that they as a Council, were very hard up for money, but it seemed to'be forgotten that in estimating their financial position that certain accounts had to be paid to them. Whereas at one time there might be a deficit at the bank there was always a lot of</p>
        <p>outstanding money. The Chairman deprecated open discussions on these matters, as it created wrong impressions in the minds of the ratepayers. The councillors present were large ratepayers, and in serving the general body of ratepayers they were serving their own interests. They only, wanted to '.clo what was right. In bringing pp this discussion Councillor Cowper seemed to be playing to the gallery.- .The matter had been discussed in the Finance Committee, and he knew perfectly well how matters stood.</p>
        <p>Councillor Cowper said he was as large a ratepayer 'as anyone present, and spent more money in the district. He denied that he was speaking to the gallery, and asserted that they should have taken His advice three years agG and put 3d. in the £ on the rates, instead of which they would have to pay more in future. Councillor F. Ingham having stated that he thought Councillor Cowper had had a thorough explanation given him in the Finance Com- mittee meeting, discussion on this subject ended.</p>
        <p>Overseers Reappointed.</p>
        <p>Councillor W. G. Groves and Mr. Sam Gould were reappointed overseers. Diphtheria And Scarlet Fever.</p>
        <p>Dr. Burrows, acting medical officer, statel that during the month there had been 37 cases of diphtheria and 21 of scarlet fever. The last case of scarlet fever was notified on Tuesday last.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : Let us hope it will be the last</p>
        <p>Those Present.,</p>
        <p>The members and officials present were Messrs. R. IL Gotobed, J. IL Holt, W. G. Groves, R. W. Parker, C. E. Cowper, F. Lockyer, F. Ingham. A, Mclntyre and S. Critchley, W Mallard (clerk), C. Mort (assistant clerk), Dr. Burrows (acting medical officer), Mr. T. IL ,West (gas manager) and Mr. C. I. Barnes (water ,mitriager)• •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>LATE LIEUT. I. A-lIIDGWAY.</head>
        <p>THE LATE LIEUT. I. A-lIIDGWAY.</p>
        <p>Memorial SerVice And Window Dedi- cation At Oughtrington Church. A very impressive service was held in the Oughtrington Parish Church last Sunday after- noon, in the presence of a large number of dis- tinguished,worshippers. It was the occasionl for dedicating a beautiful stained glass window and brass tablet to the memory of one of Lymm"s most beloved officers who has fallen for King and country, viz., the late Lieutenant Isaac Althorp Ridgway. Although admission to the church was limited to ticket holders until the hour of 3.15, fully an hour before the opening a large crowd had assembled from Lymm and the surrounding districts to pay tribute to the deceased soldier's memory. The church was filled to overflowing, and many members of the congregation had to be content with chairs placed down the aisles. Others were unable to obtain, admission. The officiating clergy were the Rev. Tudor Evans, rector of Oughtrington, and the Rev. G. B. "Thurston, of The Lymm Parish Church. The former, in a brief address, spoke of the great respect the deceased gentle- man had earned by his endearing nature and kindly disposition. One could not speak too highly of his most noble sacrifice on behalf of King and country. Although not knowing him, personally, added the Rev. Tudor Evans, he knew the feeling of the inhabitants for the fallen hero. The loss was a great one to the village, but dedicating the beautiful glass win- dow to his memory was a reminder that although his earthly remains lay in another clime, yet here was an everlasting remembrance. The Window. The window was a gift of the Ridgway family, whose efforts on behalf of charity and other works have been so spontaneous in every way. The design is of no than artistic value, for it takes the form of a soldier, fully armed, and ready for the fray, receiving a blessing in the true Christian way, before going into active service. The inscription underneath, which is in Latin, is one which the late Lieutenant often used to remark :—ml'hough the ocean divides us, Holy Communion unites us." "The brass tablet," continued the reverend gentleman, "had been sent by Lieutenant Ridgway's friends from the Commonwealth of Australia, and is similar to several erected in that country." Situated in the west end of the church, it was unveiled in solemn silence by Mrs. T. J. Ridg- way, the mother of the popular officer. Sir Arthur Haworth, Bart. (commanding the Altrincham Battalion Cheshire Volunteer Regi- ment) read the lesson, and a special prayer. was 'offerid for all the then who had fallen, in the war, and who formerly resided in the parish. The Cheshire Volunteer Regiment were represented by the Lymm Platoon, under th 9 command of Captain G. R. Williamson and Sergeant S. D. Moston. The Oughtrington Platoon were ,under the command of Captain Hawkins and Sergeant T. Peters, the Bowdon Platoon were also, present. Lymm find Heatlev Troops of Boy Scolds were also to be seen, under Scoutmasters J. L. Farrar and T. Nash. Hymns "The Mints of God, their conflict past" and For all the Saints who from their labours rest" were sung with pathos by the congrega- tion -and choir, and an anthem was rendered with depth of feeling entitledi "Blessed are the departed." The organist was Mr, 0. G. Smith, who near the elese gave an impressive render- ing of Chopin's Funeral March. . At the conclusion of the s4rvice the buglers of the South Lancashire Regiment stationed at Warrington sounded the "Last Post."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</p>
        <p>LVMM Platoon.</p>
        <p>Sunday, March sth.-31axueuvres with the 6th Manchester Battalion, Volunteer Regiment, at Dunham Massey. Fall-in Drill Hall, 2.45 p.m. Tuesday, March 7th.-7.15 p.m., Shooting; 8, Semaphore Signalling and Platoon Drill. Thursday, March •9th.-7.15 p.m., Shooting; 8, Bayonet Exercise and Platoon Drill.</p>
        <p>G. R. ,WILLIAMSON, TliAtoon Commander</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>For Prince of Wales' Relief Fund.—As a re- sult of the concert given by members of the St. Thomas' Sunday School on Monday, January 31st, the sum of £5 9s. 9d. has been handed over to the Prince of Wales' local relief 'fund.</p>
        <p>Ye New Alarm.—On Thursday at 8.15 the Spade Works buzzer sounded the Zeppelin alarm, which in future will serve as a signal for the assembling of special constables, ambu- lance men and the Voluntary Training Corps. Hill Cliffe Baptist Girls' Guild.—On Thursday last week an excellent concert was given in the schoolroom in aid of the Red Cross movement. The room was tastefully decorated, and was u-41 filled by an appreciative audience. Rev. Curtis presided. A brief but encouraging report was given by Mrs. Roughsedge (secretary), who is to be greatly conplimented, together with Mrs. Ault, for training the girls in the music': part of the entertainment. The prograinme consisted of chorus, "Three Flags"; prologue, Phillis Moss; songs, "Boys in Khaki, Boys in Blue," by Elsie Pickering, E. Brocklehurst, N. Frazer; "Red Cross Nurses," by girls; "Twelve Little Marks," by Junior Guilders; "Roses," Bertha Langley; "Singing Time," by Guilders; "Factory Lasses," by Guilders dressed in clogs and shawls, created a great deal of amusement. These sings being in character, were greatly enjoye,d and received hearty applause. Recita- tions were given by M. Williams; "Somewhere in the Trenches," E. Williams; "Lord Dulan's Daughter," May Richardson. The dialogue "Playing at Parliament, was quite unique and comical. The members were: L. Byfield, E. Brocklehurst. E. Frazer, E. Williams, H. Booth L. Gerrard, N. Frazer, P. Dooley, Bertha Lang- ley (speaker); dialogue "Our At Home Day,' Miss Hendlev, L. Byfield, B. Langley, caused a great deal of merriment: pianoforte solos were given' by E. Frazer and E. Brocklehurst. Miss Challoner was the accompanist. The drill dis- play was heartily applauded, and Miss Dodd (drill instructor) is to be congratulated on the girls' efficiency and accuracy of movement. The officers of the Guild are confident that this splendid entertainment will be success financially.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>2187 VISITS.</head>
        <p>2187 VISITS.</p>
        <p>Activity Of The Nursing Association In</p>
        <p>The Village.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Stockton Heath Nursing Association on Monday, it was stated that the. year began with a balance in hand of t 55 3s. 2d., and ()ID:lad with a balance of £4l 7s. 6d., of which 224 18s. 2d. was on the general account and £l6 9s. 4d. on the special or Corona- tion account. It would be seen that the balance in hand was diminishing, and, therefore, before long it would be necessary to make efforts to increase the income. It was hoped-that th present-time subscribers would not overlook the -necessity of continuing their contributions to this helpful local work fox. this ministry to</p>
        <p>the sick and suffering of the district must under any circumstances be maintained. The report on the work of the nurse showed that the total number of cases nursed during 1915 was 127-20 less than last year. There were 1,154 visits to surgical cases and 1,033 to medical cases—a total of 2,187, as against 2,474, a reduc- tion of 287 in 1914. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The following officers were elected: President, Mr. Veto B. Davies; hon. secretary and treasurer, Mr. W. H. Woodcock; auditor, Mr. E. Braashaw.</p>
        <p>Mr. Vere B. Davies presided.</p>
      </div>
