      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 6th March : Monday.—Drill at Headquarters, 7.45 p.m. Wednesday.—A Shooting Competition (on handicap) will take place at the Drill Hall, commencing at 6.30 p.m. The Section Match for the S.M.R.C. -trophy will commence at S p.m. Conditions will be posted on the notice board and at the Drill Hall. Thursday.—Non-Coms.' Instruction Class Hall Cafe, 7.30 p.m. Saturday.—lnter-manceuvres. toria-square 2.45 sharp.</p>
        <p>Fall in Vic-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>At a Parish Council meeting held in the Larg•-: Hall, Grappenhall, on Thursday night, there were present Messrs. E. Allen, J. Bradford, F. pox, A. Lister Kay, W. A. Wooding, and' C. IL Hoult (clerk). The Council accounts were read, and showed a balance in hand of £1 Is. A letter was read from Mr. F. A. Frost, who regretted his inability to be attend owing to urgent Chamber of Commerce business detaining him in London. Mr. A. Lister Kay therefore occupied the chair. The overseers' accounts for the half-year ending March 31st, 1915, were read and were considered satisfactory. A discussion arose as to the bad state of the footpath !between Knutsford-road and Chester- road, and it was decided to have it re- cindered. Mr. W. A. Wooding and the Clerk were- instructed to have the work put in hand. The 'annual Parish Meeting then took place, and in the absence of the Chairman, Mr. E. Allen presided. A number of letters were read from the County Council and the District Coun- cil relating to the main roads and sewerage of the .parish. Mr. Tlankey, raised the question of the foul state of ditches from Knutsford-road to the railway, and it was decided that the Runcorn Rural District Council be requested to clean the ditches out pending the commence- ment of piping, which was suggested as a per- manent remedy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>UNHEEDED WARNING</head>
        <p>UNHEEDED WARNING</p>
        <p>Precedes Drowning Of Promising Boy.</p>
        <p>Told not to walk along the canal side at Grappenhall owing to the intense darkness on Saturday night, an eight-year-old boy named Arthur Allcock took no hoed of the warning, with the result that an inquest was held on his body in the Large Room on Tuesday afternoon. The evidence showed that the boy's father and housekeeper being out of the• house, young -Allcock went to stay with a neighbour for an hour or two. While there he manifested a de- sire to fetch some sweets, and, persisting, he was allowed to go by the neighbour, Mrs. Ash- hurst. Warning him not to walk aldng the towing path as it was too dark, she placed a cape around his head to protect him from the wind, at the same time giving him a basket in which to bring her some provisions. But the boy went to the shop via the taring path, and the keeper, Mrs. Palin, advised him not to re- turn that way, but he only smiled. A minute later a man came running to say that someone was in the water, so she took a lamp and tried to locate him, but there was not even a bubble on the surface. P.C. Kelford was sent for, and he and Mr. Buckley rescued the body from the canal by using a -boathook. Artificial respira- tion was unfruitful.</p>
        <p>Fatal Precocity.</p>
        <p>The Coroner (Mr. H. C. Yates) remarked that, unfortunately, children nowadays were becoming too precocious and would not take the advice of adults. He very much regretted the death of the boy, because he looked so well and intelli- gent. It was a great pity. The boy met his death as soon as he left the steps leading from the shop to the towing path and, to his (the Coroner's) mind, the fact that he was "wearing a cape over his head might have been a factor in his death, for, as he walked along, it might have been blown by the wind across his eyes and continuing his steps, have thus caused him to walk blindly into the canal. Another idea was that the sudden contrast between the lights of the shop and the darkness outside niight have intensified the darkness to the boy. A juryman observed that the canal steps were very high and much worn, and the ,boy might easily have slipped. Verdict: "Accidental death," the jury sug- gesting that.a barrier should be placed between the bottom of the steps and the canal, so as to minimise the chance of people walking into the water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Gra-ppenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing sth March:— Monday, 6th March.—Musketry. Meet at the Range at 7.30 p.m. sharp. Range Officer: Q.M.S. and Sec.-Commander C. F. Read. Tuesday, 7th March.—Company and Squad Drill. Fall in at Headquarters at 7.45 p.m. sharp. _</p>
        <p>-- Friday, 10th March.—Musketry. Meet at the Range at 7.30 p.m. sharp. Range Officer: Sec.. Corporal W. Tranton. Saturday, 11th March.—Parade (Uniform) 'and Company Drill. Weather permitting this Com- pany "Drill will take place at Thelwall in con- junction with the Lymm, Stockton Heath, War- yington and possibly other detachments, and the O.C. hopes for a strong muster. Fall in at Headquarters at 3 p.m. sharp. Orderlies.—Sec.-Corporal P. Tipping, Private W. Johnson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>AIR RAIDS.</head>
        <p>AIR RAIDS.</p>
        <p>In the event of a raid by enemy, air-craft an alarm will be given by the ringing of the treble bell in the Church Tower, on hearing which, members of the Detachment are required to repair immediately to the Headquarters and fall in outside the room, the Senior Private taking charge should there be no N.C.O. present. They will then :await instructions from the police. Brassards must be worn, but not uniforms. By Order, F. A. FROST, 0.C., Platoon Commander. • Headquarters, ,4th March, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>• Mrs. Lawrance's Bereavernent.we regret to announce the death of the infant son of Mx* -Lawrance, matron of the Cottage Homes, Pad gate. The child has been suffering for some' time, but as his condition did not improve, he was taken last week to the Children's Hospital at Pendlebury, where an operation was per- formed, but death occurred on Friday morning of last week. The interment took place at Christ Church, Padgate, on Tuesday afternoon, the Rev. J. Hiist 'being the officiating clergy- man. The sad occurrence has caused wide- spread sympathy in the village, as it is only a little over 12 months ago since Mrs. Lawrance lost her husband.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—The usual weekly meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on M?nday evening, when a paper on." Church Praise and its Devel- opment" was given by Mr. W. J. Bate, of Pad- gate. He dealt with the power, of association between hymns and tunes, adding that a great responsibility rested . upon those who were re- sponsible for the singing in our churches, and urgsd the importance of seeing that associations were formed round the right kind of tune to banish as far as possible the mawkish and sen- timental, and so gradually raise the standard of music we offer to Almighty God. He also gave a brief history of church praise from the time of Moses, through the Hebrew and Christian. eras, until the publication of the Methodist Hymn Book in 1904. Illustrations were given showing the early developments of notation and</p>
        <p>the progress of hymnody from its initial stages until the arrival of the perfected hymn tune, was also explained. Amongst the musical illus- trations which were given by the choir was an 'old Gregorian chant which was sung in Latin, several German chorales dating from the Refofmation, and further selections were given slowing the style of tune used at different periods of history, introducing the old fugue tunes a the 18th century. Votes Of thanks were moved by Mr. W. E. Plinston and Mr. Pearce. The Rev. A. J. Morton presided, and Mr. W. H. Ward wa.s the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
