      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Wesley-- Guild.—On Tuesday evening, in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Mr. C. Wilcockson gave a most interesting lantern lecture, entitled "Devonshire Cream," being an account of a tour of 650 miles without a railway ticket, or the questionable pleasure of'a free ride under the Carriage seat, as one personal friend sug- gested. Mr.' Wilcockson's slides were perfect, and the Lecturer, who combines a love of all things -beautiful with a decidedly keen sense of humour, succeeded in making the evening one to be remembered, and the Wesley Guild are very fortunate in being able to sectre The lantern manipulator was Mr. J. Wood- ward.</p>
        <p>Gardener Who Made Waste Land Smile.—ln. this week's "The Gardener" there appears a large picture of Mr. James Ray's prize-winning garden, Heath-road, Penketh, accompanied by a letter written by Mr. Ray, from which we extract the following: "I had the plot let to me in September of 1914. It had not been dug over for fifteen years, and carried a splendid crop of plaintain and dock. I set to work to fork over every inch of the plot. I spread all the weeds out and got them dry, then heaped them up and burnt them. I saved the ashes, and in spring top-dressed the ground with them Flowers generally and roses particularly pro- vide my chief hobby. I had won first prize for a small garden for five successive years, so I determined to go one better, and got more land. I managed to get the larger garden into good order, for in the judges' report I came first with 38 points. The second and third were 22 and 21 points respectively. Then I entered some ex- hibits at the show—in all eleven entries—and gained eight firsts and two seconds, with the other unplaced. I am a keen follower of the following rules: to grow your own, show your own, and win with your own."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>The " Last Post."—On Sunday evening there was held in St. Mary's Church, Sankey, a memorial service to the local men who have fallen in the war. Outside the church the "Last Post" was sounded by buglers attached to the St. Paul's Company of the Church Lads' Brigade, the Vicar. church officers and a con- siderable number of people being present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</head>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</p>
        <p>Headquarters, Enville-street.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending Friday, March 10th : Parades.—Sunday (to-morrow).—The Corps will parade as strong as possible at Head- quarters at 945 a.m., and march to Christ Church, Latchford, to attend Divine Service. Uniform must be worn. Tuesday and Friday: Fall in at Bridge-street Rooms at 7.30 prompt Dress—marching order. Band Practice on Friday at Headquarters; 8 p.m. to 9 p.m. Thursday.—Class Firing. Parade at 7.311 prompt. Orderlies for the week.: Corporal Nelson and Lance-Corporal Cartwright. By Order, J. B. STARKE-sr, C.O.</p>
