      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>GHOSTS ON THE SCREEN.</head>
        <p>GHOSTS ON THE SCREEN.</p>
        <p>The Stony Faces Of The Dead' Shown At Warrington.</p>
        <p>SAD OF COUNTENANCE.</p>
        <p>A still lantern with the ghostly figures of the dead taking up plenty of room on the screen at the Druids' Hall on Tuesday, evening, proved as interesting as any cinematograph, though it would have been more exciting if they had walked.</p>
        <p>The lecturer, Mr. W. Hope, of Crewe, ex- plained that it had taken him nearly 12 months to get all the slides together. Certainly, there were enough of them. There were scores, and, as time went on, surprise developed into familiarity. Most of the pictures had been taken at seances at Crewe, though several Were the result of sittings elsewhere, including private houses. The method adopted, said the lecturer, was usually to place any kind of photographic plate the sitter himself brought to the seances, and this was inserted in the official camera. If however, the sitters wished, their own camera could be used, and results of this work were thrown upon the screen. • Frequently the camera itself had been dispensed with, and many scep- tical-minded individuals had been privileged to purchase a box of photographic plates from any- where, and, having brought them to the seances, were allowed to nominate any of the plates in- -side the unopened box, and, after a time, on being opened a message written by a departed spirit, was found inscribed thereon. In •other cases the plate itself was taken out of the Packet and -held in front of the. sitter. After exposure, the sitter went home and de- veloped the negative, and usually—though not always, for success did not follow consecutively. there was either a physical, floral, or caligraphic apparition on the plate, as well as the sitter" s own figure.</p>
        <p>Dead Languages by a Living Spirit.</p>
        <p>Examples of this work were shown, and Mr. Hope gave all the data connected with them— names, addresses, and circumstances under which they had been taken. Gentlemen—even ministers of religion—who had been opposed to the possibility of spirit photography, had a sit- ting in the seance, and were surprised on de- veloping the negatives to find that either a physical figure was- resting on their shoulders, or concentrating his eyes on them, or that a message had been written to them. In the case of one London clergyman, round the photo- graph appeared messages in Greek, Latin, Eng- lish and German. The lecturer said he could not tell the audience what all the messages were,. as he was not a German, but the English words, in effect,' were a message of good cheer. The clergyman presumably harked back to his college days to understand the other languages. Especially interesting to the audience, who were mainly members of the Warrington Spiritualist party. • were the photographs of several ladies and gentlemen who had paid visits to their meetings. In one,instance the face of a child was seen illuminated, near that of its mother, and a member of the audience said he had been much touched with this photo- graph, as he had helped to bury the body of the child. All except one photograph had been taken at seances. In this exception, however, there was a picture of a motor cycle, riderless, but stand- ing just clove by. and with a background of ivy leaves, could be faintly distinguished the figure of the owner, who had been killed while riding it. It was very faint, but there it was. Generally speaking, besides the faces which were shown, their garments seemed to be noth- ing but a trail of chiffon. This was even so with the men, though frequently these were wearing the' clothing usual to all pedestrians. In showing one figure, the lecturer mentioned _ _</p>
        <p>_ that the gentleman who had passed over 'and was now standing by the sitter was her grand- father, who was killed while working down a coal mine many years ago. One could not but observe, however, that he appeared to be wear- ing a frock coat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>ow:A</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
