      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>MUNITIONS COURT.</head>
        <p>MUNITIONS COURT.</p>
        <p>Blacksmith Who Got More Money When Single.</p>
        <p>In applying at the Warrington Munitions Court on Monday for a discharge certificate from a local works, John Millington, married, 17, Albert-terrace, Warrington, who is employed as a blacksmith's striker, complained that the Wages paid were not sufficient to kecp.his three motherless children, and that the conditions of his employment were unhealthy. Applicant stated that he had been engaged at the works since January. His wages were 235. 6d. for a fifty-three hour week. He was rated as a striker, but he did not get a striker's money. When a single man in 1889 he was get- ting more money than he was to-day. He would ' not have started at the works only that the fore- nian promised that he would be put on piece work and overtime. The smell of the spelter fumes made him have violent fits of coughing, and it was the first time he had been ill since he .erne out of a sanatorium. , A representative of the firffi said if the man was granted a certificate they would experience great difficulty in securing a striker to fill his Place. On one occasion applicant was offered overtime to remove ashes, which was part of the Work of a striker. He refused to do it, stating that such work was for a labourer. The Application was refused. The Wages Attraction.</p>
        <p>A similar application was made by an employe of a Government controlled Helsby firni, who desired •to change his employment for more wages with a firm at Frodsham. The applica- tion was made on his behalf by a trade union official, who stated that the applicant had to travel four miles in order to get to his work at Helsby. It was pointed out by a representative of the Helsby firm that in December the wages of their employes were submitted to arbitration, but no advance was ordered by the arbitrators As a result men had attempted to get away from the works, and this was brought as a test case. Applicant was formerly a reservist,• and the com- pany had paid to his wife a sum a nearly £2O whilst he was away fighting. The application was refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>Novel Attempt To Get An increase.</head>
        <p>Novel Attempt To Get An increase.</p>
        <p>Two apprentices from Wigan, James Baldwin. 12, Ellis-street, and Herbert Coates, 32, Douglas- street, were each fined, the money to be taken from their wages at the rate of Is. 3d. per week, for failing to apply themselves to their work between January Ist and February Ist. The allegation against the defendants was that they lighted a fire outside the shop where they were working. A foreman extinguished the fire, but three hours later it Was found to have been re- kindled. It was also alleged that the two de- fendants infringed the rules by not attending to 'Weir work. Their conduct was very unsatis- factory. One of the defendants said they had put i- an application for an advance in wag, but it had been refused, and that was the only way they could take it out of the firm. The Chairman (Mr. Ridgway): That is not the right way to get an advance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>Absentee Labourers.</head>
        <p>Absentee Labourers.</p>
        <p>Two labourers employed at a Widnes iron and steel works named Thomas Doherty and James llealeY, were summoned for absenting them- selves without leave. They left work on Satur- (l, aY, February 12th, and did not return until Ills following Thursday.—A fine of 2s. 6d. each Was imPOsed. An unavailing " Better Offer."</p>
        <p>A e° man complained that his eertificate, had h -1---Ilennlttnureasonably withheld. .11.40 'stated .he had volunteered for munition work, and that his present wages were insufficient. He had a better offer from a similar works in the same town.—The application was refused. Mr. T. J. Ridgway presided over the Court, and the assessors were Mr. J. Mercer, Hough Green (employers' panel), and Mr. H. F. Brown, Crewe (workmen's panel).</p>
        <p>The County Motor Co. Stockton Heath Garage, Stockton Heath, Warrington.</p>
        <p>41!1117111111.71. in \ Telephone ‘-11117-7 e tp, 63 Stockton Heath</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>A £2O FINE.</p>
        <p>Failed To Question Man Who Fought In 1870.</p>
        <p>An alien having stayed a night at the British Workman's Restaurant in Horsemarket-street, without haying been questioned by the manageress as to his nationality, &amp;e., this lady, Mrs. Hampson, was fined ..£2O (or, in default, a month's imprisonment) at the War- rington Police Court on Thursdal. The alien himself, John Henry gitk-elt.r, was I sent to gaol for three months, for having 1ai12,1 to fill in the registration form n quired. The prisoner, an elderly man, decl.inid that he was an American, but the Chief Constable said 'there was a grave doubt as to his nation- ality, because when he worked at Salford the manager and foreman at the works there said they always understood him to be a German; in fact, he had told them the very town in which he was born in Germany. At times he hitcl also related his experi.nces when he fought in the Franco-German War in 1870-1. It should be added that he had a son, Frank, in the R.A.M.C. in the British Army. At Sal- ford he had committed an offence by stating that he was a British subject. On :Steeler being sentenced, Mrs. Hampson was called; and Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, solicitor, who defended her, said that when Steeler came into the house Mrs. Hampson was busily engaged. He had not been there long before he went to bed, and left early the next morning. Although she had neglected her duty to obtain particulars of himself from Steeler, she had very little opportunity to do so. She very much regretted the lapse. To Detect Spies.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that the Registra- tion Order was widely published, and, in fact, a. bill containing its regulations was posted up in the restaurantt But, apparently, ever since the let January, Mrs. Hamßson had absolutely failed to take the particillars from anybody who had been staying at the house. It seemed to be a case of gross neglect. The State relied absolutely upon thu,so w f;ept hotels, lodging houses and refreshment rooms to detect any spies who might be travelling about the coun- try, and unless information were obtained there was less likelihood of spies being caught. Mr. A. J. juicy, with whom sat Dr. Nauem, said they regarded Mrs. Hampson's offence as being more serious than that of Steeler's. 'Fine as above.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>GENEROUS HOUSE!</head>
        <p>A GENEROUS HOUSE!</p>
        <p>Patrick Smith, a stranger, was sent to gaol for one month by the magistrates on Monday, for begging in Froghall-lane on Saturday night. Prisoner: I had only been to three houses, and had got nothing. The Chief Constable: I hope he has come to a house where he will get something! (Laughter.) There is absolutely no need to beg in these days. when men are so urgently wanted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>GRAVE SOURCE OF DANGER.</head>
        <p>GRAVE SOURCE OF DANGER.</p>
        <p>For failing to have a rear light on his cart and for having been drunk in charge of a horse and trap, John Leaity, Enfield Villas, Fearn- head, was fined 445„ or, in default, twenty-eight days' imprisonment About half past sevorron Saturday night P.C. Dawson saw a horse and cart standing on Pinner's Brow. There was no rear light on the cart. He found prisoner in a fruiterer's shop in a drunken condition. The officer put him in the trap and drove ,hina to the Police Station, where he 'Was detained.</p>
        <p>Defendant said he was giF;en to understand that a red light shining from the front light was Rufficient.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said the Act laid it dowa that a red rear light was necessary. Now that the streets were so dark it was a grave source of danger for a man to be in such a condition when in charge of a horse and cart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>GIRL SENT TO A HOME.</head>
        <p>GIRL SENT TO A HOME.</p>
        <p>Dorothy Rowell, 11, Kean-street, a young domestic servant, who was remanded at the Police Court last week in connection With a charge of stealing two ten shilling Treasury notes, the property of Mrs. Winifred Jennings wife of the licensee of the White Bull, 10rse- market-street, has promised to go into a honr3 for twelve months, and the case was adjourned sine die.</p>
        <p>_The Chief C,onstahle said the police were satis- fied from inquiries that the girl had no know- ledge of the theft of three g,..1 rings, which had been recently stolen from the house. Mr. A. Browne, who appeared for the girl, said it would be to her advantage if, the magis- trates would agree to sending her to a home for twelve months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>COUNTRY YOUTH WHO "lIELPED" BY CRUELTY.</head>
        <p>COUNTRY YOUTH WHO "lIELPED" BY CRUELTY.</p>
        <p>Ernest Miller, a youth, of Stretton, was fined 10s. at the Police Court on Wednesday for cruelty to a cow by beating it with a stout stick across the head as it was walking oot of the ring at a cattle sale. Defendant said he only turned the cow back again in the ring. He thought he was helping the owner by doing so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>WAITED FOR THE REWARD.</head>
        <p>WAITED FOR THE REWARD.</p>
        <p>But Had To Pay A Fine For His Delay. _</p>
        <p>For failing to notify the police on finding a dog in the street, George Barlow, 30, Fair- dough's-avenue, was fined ss. by the Warrington magistrates on Wednesday. It was stated that on February 18th defendant called at the Police Station and notified - Detective Anderson he had found a retriever puppy a few days pre- viously. It was his duty to report his find with- in forty-eight hours. In 'consequence of his delay the owner and police were put to a good deal of unnecessary expense and trouble in advertising the loss. The Chief Constable added that the defendant evidently had waited until the reward was offered'before notifying the police. Defendant said he could not have come earlier as he was working over on munitions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>LOST HIS TF3IPEIt.</head>
        <p>LOST HIS TF3IPEIt.</p>
        <p>Carter Who Struck A Boy With His Whip.</p>
        <p>Arthur Hartley, carter, 13, Kimberley-drive, Stockton Heath, was summoned at the Warring-,. ton Borough Court on Wednesday for disorderly behaviour in Scotland-road on Saturday, February 19th. Defendant was seen by Special Constable Mason to strike a boy in Charge of a handcart with a whip on the legs without pro- vocation. The boy in wheeling the handcart had accidentally got wedged between the foot- path and the lorry the defendant was driving. Defendant lost his temper and struck the boy with the whip. He was bound over, and ordered to pay tho costs, 7s. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>ALL IN A ROW.</head>
        <p>ALL IN A ROW.</p>
        <p>I (mills' Frightlulness In Darkened</p>
        <p>Street.</p>
        <p>Since the darkening, of the- town, Sankey- reet, always.a parade grounk ham become the joyland. of young people prone to rowdyism. That the police intend protecting the peace. loving section of the public may be judged by the fact that five youths, prosecuted for dis- orderly behaviour, were bound over to keep the peace for six months in a sum of £5, and ordered to pay the costs (ss. 6d. each) at the Poliee Court on Thursday morning. ' • The Chief Con Stable said that since the Light-- ing Order came into force and Sankey-street had been darkened, crowds of youths and maidens went along, shouting and singing, and jostling people about. • Jolly Bad Boys AR in a Row.</p>
        <p>As many as five abreast went down the street together sweeping all before them, and rolling and rollicking about in a very disorderly man- ner. A great number of them also stood in shop doors, and were altogether an intolerable nuis- ance both to the shopkeepers and customers.</p>
        <p>Continued In next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORRIG.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORRIG.</p>
        <p>Economy Will Be The Fashion</p>
        <p>This Year.</p>
        <p>TOURING IN 1916.</p>
        <p>Owing to the abnormal state of affairs exist- ing at the present time it is highly improbable that there will be any regular holiday period during 1916. The shortage of labour is every- where so acute that it will be difficult for most men to get away for any length of time. All the same, if one is going to do one's work suc- cessfully an occasional change is absolutely essential. The Minister of Munitions has already laid down this principle by insisting that all munition factories shall close for one day in seven. Better to have a few days free from the cares and worries of work than a few weeks enforced rest owing to a breakdown in health. If only for economy's sake, therefore, an occasidnal few days' ,vacation is necessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>WEEKLY SKELETON TOUR.</head>
        <p>A WEEKLY SKELETON TOUR.</p>
        <p>Quite apart from the economy of a cycling holiday there,. ids another side to the question. And that is that there is no form of exercise which is so beneficial as cycling. For many years Vast I have spent my holidays on my machine, and I have visited nearly all the districts of interest and beauty in this country of ours—after all is said And donc., one of the most beautiful and interesting in all the world. But in adopting this form of holiday don't make the mistake that ,so many make and attempt too much. it not only spoils the holiday, but it does one harm instead of good. It may be that thirty-five miles is enough for you; others may perhaps do their hundred and feel no ill effects. This is a point which each rider must decide for himself.- In order to assist those-who may- decide to take their holiday awhee/ I propose each week to give a skeleton tour, together with notes of in-, terest on the districts through which one passes, the condition of the roads, and the points of value to the holiday make. At the same time, if any reader wants special information regard- ing a particular district which he proposes to visit I shall be glad ta give him- all the advice I can. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>TIP FOR MUD-PLUGGEFIS.</head>
        <p>A TIP FOR MUD-PLUGGEFIS.</p>
        <p>If you look beneath the mudguards of any machine—except one which is religiously kept stabled when there is a suspicion of wet about— ). guarantee that you will find rust making pre- liminary inroads into the metal. This is bound to be so, for no enamel can long withstand the constant 'Scraping necessary to remove the .con- gealed mud from the underside of the guards. And equally inevitable as a result of the rust, are loosened stray rivets, if nothing worse. come time ago I read of a paint which not only defied rust, but resisted the most energetic craping. Putting it to the test I found it all that was claimed for it. Here is 'the recipe:— Red lead, 2 parts, White lead, 1 part,</p>
        <p>and sufficient boiled oil to bring the lead to the consistency of paint. I now make a practice of daubing with this paint, not only the inside of the mudguards but the brake shoes and other "brake parts liable to rust, expotked baits and nuts, lamp-bracket, and all crevices where damp doth delight to congre- gate. A single th of the spanner has always been sufficient to loosen tuts painted over with this preparation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>PETROL SAVING.</head>
        <p>PETROL SAVING.</p>
        <p>Carburetter tinkering is once again the order I of the day, the latest rise in the price of petrol having tilled us all with .the righteous resolve to screw another mile or two per gallon out of the motor cycle. lam afraid this will spell trouble for -Arno of the modern brigade: In old days we, perforce, learnt so much about the vitals of the carburetter that:it will be second nature to us to tinker about with the adjust- ment until we secure the maximum of :efficiency. The average rider of a. latter-day machine, however, knows as much about car- buretter adjustment as he does about aerostatics. I and unless he gees to work with great care he may do more harm than good with his tinker- ing. The best advice I can give is not to ex- periment alone with different sized jots, leaving all the other parts as they were; but to try a complete new set—jet, choke tube, air cone, Sze., choosing a jet a size or two smaller than you have bolt' accustomed to use, and other things to match. The makers may be retied upon to have fitted originally the combination that gives the best all-round results so far as speed, llexi- hility.and fuel economy are concerned, and most likely any interference with one unit will effect no improvement, bilt will destroy the balance of the whole.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>SOME SMALL ECONOMIES.</head>
        <p>SOME SMALL ECONOMIES.</p>
        <p>One thing, however, may be done wisely on most machines which have seen some wear. The level of the petrol in the jet may be lowered a fraction by tapping the collar of the needle which carries the float levers—a very gentle tapping, mark you—so as to makd the needle settle down on its seating earlier and thereb. lessening the flow of petrol. One or two othe: points are worth giving. A well-tuned engine gives the most economical running; a little mechanical knowledge goes a long way to- wards saving precious pennies otherwise. on the garage man; lubricating .41 ivraght in bulk costs much less than oil bought by the putt or quart. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>ELECTRIC LIGHT WIRING.</head>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHT WIRING.</p>
        <p>7, A' point which .is very often overlooked by motorists is that in order to obtain the best results from a lighting installation there muse be no joints in the wiring. Each length of cable from terminal to terminal should be complete. Should it so happen that for any reason this cannot be done, a proper electrical joint should be made.., It is necessary to solder tlite joint, but instead of using the ordinary flux, namely, killed spirit- or chloride of zinc,..' resin should be employed. The former is not nearly so satis- factory, though it is trne that it is easier td use the work can be done more rapidly. Special insulated tape should be used to cover up the joint for two reasons. One is to prevent moisture from penetrating And the 001E4, ,to hinder alcontact being made. The forme? point is one that is frequently forgotten, but it is very important.</p>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.—Continued.</p>
        <p>, • They rushed into•people and had no regard for quiet _pedestrians.,</p>
        <p>In binding them over, Mr. Jolley said that the magistrates were quite prepared to do every- thing in their power towards keeping order in the streets.</p>
        <p>. The nanies -and 'addresses of the defendants were: John Clarke, 49, Aikin-street; Joseph Cummings, 36, Priestley-street; James Sankey, 117, VireMeld-street; Alfred Mercer, 32, Hood- lane, Sankey; and Harry Hickling, 32, Hood- lane, Sankey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>FISILMONGER'S BANKRUPTCY.</head>
        <p>FISILMONGER'S BANKRUPTCY.</p>
        <p>War A Factor In His Financial Crash.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Bankruptcy Court yester- da?, before, M. Registrar Ridgway, Walter Saunders, residing at the Lodge, Glass Works; Bank Quay, and formerly carrying on business, as a greengrocer and fishmonger at 204, Knuts- ford-road, appeared for his public examination in bankruptcy. His liabilities were expected to. rank at ~V3B .19s. 5d., and assets .28/2s. .10d., leaving a defi- ciency of £B6 16s. Id. Debtor attributed his failure to the increased cost of fish, and the ill- health of his wife and self. He first started' business in the fried fish and chip potato line at 189, Knutsford-road, but continued to follow his employment as a gas-range fitter at Fletcher, Russell's works. About nine years ago he com- menced business on the opposite side of the road as a fish, vegetable and fruit dealer. For some time the business did fairly well, but the increased cost of fish, in consequence of the out- break of war, and the decrease in the quantity sold, caused it to decline. To help matters he took a situation as a tram conductor, and, sub- sequently got employment as a time-keeper at a glass Works. The examination was closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>RUGBY.</head>
        <p>RUGBY.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION</p>
        <p>ONLY FOUR MATCHES.</p>
        <p>Wintry Conditions Play Havoc With League Fixtures.</p>
        <p>Owing to the inclement weather prevailing on Saturday, only four out of the nine Northern Union League matches were decided. At Broughton, a much weakened Huddersfield team were defeated by five points to nil. Wigan were forced to "knuckle under" at Salford by the odd point ta thirteen. Hull, at home, accounted for BatTey by thirteen points to three, and St. Helens Rec. gained an easy victory over Runcorn- by seventeen points to two.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>ONLY ONE MATCH.</p>
        <p>Seeing that wretched conditions prevailed on Saturday, it was, perhaps, a good thing that only one match was played in the local Inter- mediate League. This took place at Wilders- pool, where St. Thomas' and St. Mary's were in opposition. The former team had the better of matters throughout, and the sounding of the final whistle found them winners by fourteen points to nil.</p>
        <p>RESULTS UP TO AND INCLUDING SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 26th. Points P. W. L., D. F. A. P. Heath side Parochial Rylands's St. Thomas' Beamont Bolton . l2 3 7 2 52 66 8 St. Mary's St. James'</p>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS. St. Thomas, 14 points; S. Mary's nil.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>At Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>Parochial v. St. Mary's. Referee, G. Crozier. Kick-off 3 o'clock. Bolton v. St. Thomas'. Referee, J. Flynn. Kick-off 4 o'clock.</p>
        <p>The League meeting will be hold on Thursday, March 9th, at 7.30 prompt.</p>
        <p>JUNIOR RUGBY CHAMPIONS.</p>
        <p>Beamont Old Boys Head The League For 1915-16.</p>
        <p>Officials: A. Hackett, W. Ellis, T. Wycherley and W. Smith, 21, Norris-street, the hon. sec.</p>
