      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</p>
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Amongst the local Junior League clubs there were only two fixtures arranged for last week, and in the fixture arranged- to be played on the Arpley• ground between the Orford Juniors and the Stockton Heath Scouts, the players of the former club were on the ground and dressed ready for the fray, but were disappointed by the non-appearance of the Scouts.</p>
        <p>The Penketh Juniors had the .Bewsey A.F.C. .as visitors to thO Recreation Ground. Both Clubs n7ere well represented, and the home team began in a very aggressive manner, and obtained an early' lead. .They continued to have the better of the exchanges, and added further goals to their credit. When the game had been in pro- gress about seventy minutes the Bewsey players refused to Continue to play because appeals they made to the referee- were ignored. When the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>ECCLES SCORE ONLY GOAL IN,STORMY ENCOUNTER.</p>
        <p>Visit To Atherton To-day.</p>
        <p>Readers will be interested to know that the Committee of Warrington Town are in negotia- tion...with Lightfoot, Southport Central's centre- forward, and I 'should not be surprised if he plays for 'us against Atherton at Hood-lane next week. •</p>
        <p>The previous week's route of the sama team I led me to go to Eccles on Saturday dith the optimism of a Brandenberger, but I had only, been there -a few minutes when I had misgiv- ings as to whether the Town's attack would be Pressed home; and soon after that Ihad a feel- ing that we should do well to draw. But by a surprise raid from the Reserves (for the main culprit was a full-back) we were defeated, just like the Brandenbergers. It was only one goal, but it was enough. Though in the first quar- ter ,of an hour I felt we should not win, I had hoped that we might. escape a reverse, but early in tha kvond half matters became foo:hot; and it .was not in the power of Kite to scuttle the clever tactics which gained the goal.-</p>
        <p>It happened, ,in this way: The Borough had', been. pressing bard. , Time after time Leak, and Cookson • had. held I them hack, and, When the ball flew past,'. Kite,'" the --custodian,: showing- re- markable skill and judgment, had • proved an efiective barrier. All looked to him with con- fidence. Then the Town got hold of the ball and 'rushed ,forward, only to be robbed by Lucas at right-back. Apparently unsatisfied with the -efforts-. of .the-- half-backs line, -.Limas- 'himself dribbled the ball well forward -swiftly, shot it through a ruck of players to the inside ,left, that gentleman whizzed, it into the centre and Kennedy, being Close in; simply slammed it 'into • the .rigkt-hand bottom corner of the net. It was a swift, . short, oblique, and low shot.. Kite was at the other side, and threw his Gft. 4.4ru. across to it head first,: When he arose with mud-stained jersey he saw half a dozen Eccles mervshaking hands With each other. The Town were taken by surprise. , I think they ex- pected•LucaS to 'release the .ball immediAely he obtained it. His, 'forward Play, • took them off their guard. 'l.</p>
        <p>Generally speaking, it• was a curious and</p>
        <p>succesdully, but Kite lifted his hand, and the crisis was suspended for a moment, the ball going over the bar. - This second shot directed the ball behind the net</p>
        <p>In at least two outbursts .of the Town I should liked to have seen.:Owen play hotter. He was 0.. little slow in accepting passes from the halves, if .it meant he had to fight for posses- sion, but it must be admitted that in Smart he had a slick opponent. Once, however, he gained favour by a perfect centre, but some- thing went wrong afterwards. At any rate, the ball did not get into the net. The feature of the second half was the goal-keeping by Kite. Frequently bombarded by fast shots from all quarters, he kept the net intact, excepting once when only a miracle would have saved it.</p>
        <p>The teams were—Warrington Town: Kite; Leah, Cookson; Broster, G. Whitfield, Bullen ; Ditehfield, T. Whitfield, Aspinall, Foster and Owen.</p>
        <p>• Eccles Borough: Foot; Lucas, Smart; Bowen% A. Walker, Wilkinson; Lee, Siddall, Wilde, Kennedy and It. S. Walker.</p>
        <p>The Town goes To Atherton on the 1.17 from Bank Quay, and the team will be selected from: Kite; Leah and Cookson; Broster, G- Whitñeld, Buller' and Osborne; Connor, -T: Whitfield, As- pinall, Foster and Owen. Ditchfield'injured his knee at Eccles last week, and was unable to go to work until-Thursday. He will not be ready ,for active service on thet field for a week or two.</p>
      </div>
