      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 4, 1916.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: DOn't you think you ought to spare one out of the family? The Father (none of the sons was Present): Ti I do that we shall have to reduce Otii. output.</p>
        <p>- - - Tho Mayor: Don't you think you could really spare one? • The Father: I will leave it with you.</p>
        <p>'The Mayor: You will have to do that. The Father: Well,. by leaving it with you as to which one should go, it. will not irritate family matters,‘but.l think you.ought to allow us to get the seed in this spring before he goes. The Tribunal granted three months' pro- visional exemption to one son and disallowed the claim of the other.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>CONSCIENCES INEFFECTIVE.</head>
        <p>CONSCIENCES INEFFECTIVE.</p>
        <p>The " Wee Small voice " Fails To Enchant Rural</p>
        <p>Tribunal.</p>
        <p>Seven conscientious objectors to Military set.- vice presented themsel3.-es to the Warringtcn Rural District Recruiting Tribunal oil Thurs- day, and, with the ,exception of one whose domestic circumstances were regrettable, :Al were told to get about their country's business. Five whe recommended for non-combatant service, while the other's claim was disallowerL Mr. George Mather presided, and the members present were Dr. Murray, the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, Messrs. J. W. Knowles, E. Donnell, W. Woods, P. Longton and James Stevensrn., though the first three gentlemen did not stay to hear all the cases. Mr. W. Furness repre- sented the military authorities, and Mr. E. J. 'Sutton acted as clerk. . . .</p>
        <p>Among the audienee were prominent mem- bers of the No-Compul-ion Committee.</p>
        <p>WHEN HE WOULD SAVE LIFE.</p>
        <p>Jos. Davenport, Park View, Penketh, general clerk in a tannery office, claimed absolute ex- emption from organised warfare on the follow- ing grounds:—" (1) on the definite Christian belief, the commandment Thou shalt not kill ' means to me exactly what it implies; (2) whilst recognising the duties required by the nation from every citizen living therein, I deny any government the right to over-rule my moral and Christian convictions on a matter, the object of whieh is the taking of human life; (3) because it is my duty to serve God before man. Whet• ever the result of my claim for exemption may be, it will not alter lay determination and sted- fastness of purpose in standing for the cause of humanity." - Mr. Furness: You are a member of the Friends f—Yes. . .</p>
        <p>I take it you do, not want to serve in any way ?—Yes. • What woula you do if the Germans came?—l would do what Christ did when they" came for Him.; let them take me. You would allow your relations to be perhaps massacred or dealt with hardly by the invaders and stand by. without lifting a finger?—lf I could not do. anything else, I would • have no option. A Member: You have no objection to saving would save life in the ordinary phase of life.</p>
        <p>' On applicant being recommended( for non- combatant service, Davenport remarked that it was not a bit of use.</p>
        <p>OBJECTORS MUST SERVE THEIR COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>down for non-combatant service he calmly replied, "Well, I beg to state that, under no circumstances can I violate my Conscience. I will make an appeal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>ARMY AS MEDIATOR BETWEEN CONSCIENCE AND GOD.</head>
        <p>ARMY AS MEDIATOR BETWEEN CONSCIENCE AND GOD.</p>
        <p>° Thomas Lowe, 26, West View, Penketh, assistant to works'. manager, claimed absolute exemption from taking part in organised war- fare, combatant or non-combatant, on the I grounds that human life, made in the image of I God, was never intended by the Creator to be I needlessly slaughtered for the settlement or dis- putes between nations, and, therefore, he could I not be a party to any such slaughter, _which would be a violation of the command "Thou shalt do no murder:" To God alone was he responsible in all matters relating to conscience I and to Him only would he be responsible. Be.- tween God and his conscience no man could intervene. He could not, under any circum- stances, take part in this or in any other war. Asked how long he had held these convictions he replied "Since ray schooldays." On the applicant being placed in the non- 'combatant class, he remarked that it was a, decision which he could not accept. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>DOMESTIC REASONS HAVE WEIGHT.</head>
        <p>DOMESTIC REASONS HAVE WEIGHT.</p>
        <p>Besides, having, domestic reasons for exemp- tion, a young fellow, living at Penketh, said he believed all war to ,be contrary to the teaching of Christ, and because he believed in the Fatherhood of God over all men. Human life was sacred: uncle+ all Circunistances; and he denied the right of the State to .corapel him in a matter of moral belief and conscience. The applicant added that he had a.paialysed father, and medical opinion ,was that he would never be able to work again; he had a mother and sister to maintain, and was the only son at home.</p>
        <p>Absolute exemption was granted on domestic grounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>MISSIONARY VOLUNTEERS FOR NON-</head>
        <p>MISSIONARY VOLUNTEERS FOR NON-</p>
        <p>COMBATANT SERVICE.</p>
        <p>Thomas Henry Gregson (25), residing at St. Mary's Tiew,,Penketh-road, Penketh, explained that he was a Methodist Home Missionary, and he had a, conscientious objection to combatant service, either to the taking of life or the handling of weapons. As a teacher of religion he could not reconcile warfare with his spiritual convictions. He was, however, willing to do' non-combatant work. He only arrived home from Australia on February 3rd of this year. There he was in charge of a mission station under the direction of the Methodist Church. ITe resigned owing to a breakdown in health. The Chairman: You are willing to serve your country if we do not ask you to kill anybody ?-- Yes.</p>
        <p>Mr. Furness: How long have you had these convictions?:—Six years. He added that the authorities in Australia had made it a rule not to give anyone his passport unless he promised to join the Army, but they had granted him his passport unconditionally. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: What led up to theso nonvictions?—The acceptance of a broader life in a spiritual sense. After consultation. the Chairman remarked to Greeson: "We have granted your request and will put you down for service you can conscientiously do."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>IF THE GERMANS CAME.</head>
        <p>IF THE GERMANS CAME.</p>
        <p>A bonny young man named William Faulkner, working at the Penketh Tannery, claimed abso- lute exemption as he was opposed to war, be. cause he believed it was wrong to take life. He had held these convictions since the beginning of the war, which, in his .view, involved the surrender of the Christian ideal. The secession of 'destruction for which the world was longing would only be brought about by the abstinence from participation in warfare by people who held these convictions. The Chairman: You are not a member of the Society of Friends ?—No. Have you any objection to taking up non- combatant service P—Yes. Why P—l am not going to assist the war in any shape or form. You would not do anything to save life P—As a civilian I would, but not under military authority. Mr. Furness: You are quite prepared to see England invaded? What would you do if they</p>
        <p>came —I should act according to my conscience. If the Germans came to your house and were going to assault your sister would you allow them ?—I might assist to stop them in some way. I.:lilt I would never take life.</p>
        <p>These convictions never dawned upon you until the war broke out P—Nor necessarily. Application refused. "May I have an appeal form?" the young man asked, and was promptly handed one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>CHEMIST ON ETHICS.</head>
        <p>CHEMIST ON ETHICS.</p>
        <p>On Ralph Herbert Clatworthy, an analytical chemist at Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons' works, residing at 23, Tower House, Park-road, Great Sankey, applying for absolute exemption on conscientious grounds, Mr. Furness asked him how long he had held these convictions, and he replied "For years.- He added that he could not take the military oath, because, virtually, we were told in the Bible not to swear. By joining the Army he would make himself subject to man, when his life should only be subject to God. "God gave me my life for Him," he declared. If he joined a non-com- batant. service he would only relieve another man to go into the fighting I:ne. He believed in carrying out the principles laid dowu by Jesus Christ and, bY doing so, he was helping this country and all mankind by forces much greater than those which were armed. When the Chairman announced that the Tribunal had decided to place the applicant</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>"DO YOUR BIT."</head>
        <p>"DO YOUR BIT."</p>
        <p>George Joseph Baker, a: member of the Society of Friends, and a teacher at Penketh. School, also objected on conscientious grounds, and was recommended - for non-combatant . service. • He said he Would .appeal.. Mr. Dennett (a member of the Tribunal) re- marked that. he thought That these gentlemen should. take some in serving their country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>FRIENDS' SCHOOLTEACHER REFUSED.</head>
        <p>FRIENDS' SCHOOLTEACHER REFUSED.</p>
        <p>The headmaster at Penketh Friends' School applied for the -exemption of his chief assistant master. He said he had proved himself a most valuable master, and his services Were alto- gether indispensable. He had -entire charge ,of the science, department and no other member of the staff could do the work. The Chairman (to the headmaster): Is it pos- sible to get another science teacher?—lt is very doubtful,- and a lady teacher would not help is much, as in a boarding school there was a great deal of -outdoor-. work to be done. At present there were .101 scholars. If he went into th 3 Army- science would- have to be given up for the time being. . The application Was ,refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>CONSIDERATION FOR FARMER.</head>
        <p>CONSIDERATION FOR FARMER.</p>
        <p>A man 23 years of age, in the service of Mr. Joseph Taylor, farmer and hamper manufac- turer, of Rixton, applied for exemption on the grounds that he had a widowed mother, 63 years old, dependent upon him, and an uncle, 72 years' old, who was blind, partially dependent upon him. Labour on the farm was scarce. Case put back three months.</p>
        <p>FARMER REFUSED. A msn who works on a farm with his brother, at Sankey, had his application refused.</p>
        <p>BUT NOT THIS ONE.</p>
        <p>The son of a Croft farmer, who has already •got a brother at the Front, said that there were now only two' working on the farm. Exemption as long as he remained with his father.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRIBUNAL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRIBUNAL</p>
        <p>Many Applications For Relief,</p>
        <p>But Few Exemptions.</p>
        <p>The Warrington.Recruitin Tribunal has met twice at the Town.. Hall this week, Monday and yesterday, when several score cases were heard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>CLERK-CUM-MANAGER,</head>
        <p>CLERK-CUM-MANAGER,</p>
        <p>Messrs. Parry and Rock; of Orford, claimed exemption for their costing clerk, who was said to be indispensable. The manager statek that he was the only clerk they hat: at present. It had taken four years to tiain him in the work, and would take about the same time to train anyone else. When the manager was away he was left in charge. There were about twenty: employes at the Orford briffich of the firm. Captain McLeod: Stippo-Sing this man were taken ill? , • ;</p>
        <p>The Manager: I should bare to do 'the best I could.</p>
        <p>•It would be the same if he lient for military service, you would have to,-ctirry on soMehow p Yes, but it would be eltremilli difficult. Case put back until 2i4ay A USEFJP... SON.</p>
        <p>A. master painter clairaeTbn behalf of his son, stating that the lattee-if all his estimat- ing and plumbing:. He '(the father) was illiterate: all he could write was the signing of his own name.. Without his son he could not carry on his business. The Mayor: Supposing gave you this Coun- cil Chamber to decorate, eciiild you prepare an estimate ?—No.</p>
        <p>• Supposing your son died-F-1-4 should have to close down entirely and do journeyman painting. Case postponed until .May`3lst, an endeavour to be made to get someone elsein the meantime.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>HIS EVENINGS FOR HIS COUNTRY:</head>
        <p>HIS EVENINGS FOR HIS COUNTRY:</p>
        <p>A yoiing man, by no means of a robust consti- tution, declared that he wpf a cabinet maker and upholsterer, trading singly. lie was the sole support of a widowed mother. Ile gave her 30s. a week to keep the home going. None of his two brothers and sister, who were married, subscribed to the home.</p>
        <p>:Postponed until May .315 t when ho must make a further appeal. . - • On hearing the TribunaVs decision,• the applicant remarked that he was not averse to working for hi g country, os he had offered to devote his evenings to munition, work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>94 HOURS A WEEK FOR £2.</head>
        <p>94 HOURS A WEEK FOR £2.</p>
        <p>In the case of a man working for the War- rington Products Co., Ltd., of Longford, it was stated by the manager that he looked after the boiler and machinery. He Was indispensable. Owing to many of the employee having joined the Army it would be impossible to carry on if this man enlisted. All the men there were doing mote than their share of: work. On an average the applicant was working 94 hours a week and had been doing so fOr nearly eighteen months. He earned on an average £2 per week, sometimes it was slightly more. The .Mayor: If this man were to go, should we have more smell in Warrington? The Manager: You don't have any now, but it is possible you would have some. I don't think we have .had a complaint in the last five years. It is up to this man to see that nothing ill-smelling escapes. Adjourned till April 30th, in the meanwhile an effort to be made to replace him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>DELAYED MARRIAGE MEANS SPEEDIER</head>
        <p>DELAYED MARRIAGE MEANS SPEEDIER</p>
        <p>ENLISTMENT.</p>
        <p>A Cheshire Lines Railway clerk asked to be put into married group 33, on the ground that he was married on November 18th. For a long time he had made preparationsfor being married but for various reasons could not do so until that 'date. Also, in the past, railway men had not been allowed to enlist, although he had tried to do so seven times. Seeing that he had not been allowed to enlist and that, in the meantime, he had married, he thought he would be unfairly penalised if he had to be treated as a single man. Captain McLeod remarked that railway clerks generally were not exempt—only those for whom the companies made special application. Application refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>TOO PROUD FOR OLD AGE PENSION.</head>
        <p>TOO PROUD FOR OLD AGE PENSION.</p>
        <p>A barman asked to be put back on the ground that he .was the sole support of his father and mother, aged 75 years and 69 respectively. They would have no other means of support if he were called up. His brother left them a few months ago owing to family differences. He had a sister at home, and she looked after his parents in the house.</p>
        <p>In reply to the Mayor, the applicant said that his father did not receive the old age pehsion. The Mayor: Why ?—lie is a bit too protd to accept it.</p>
        <p>The applicant was allowed until March 31st in order to teach his sister his work, so that she could succeed him.</p>
        <p>PRESSED INTO SERVICE.</p>
        <p>The application of the foreman in the letter- press department of the Co-operative Wholesale Society for postponement for a month or five weeks was refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>ONE SHALL BE TAKEN ----</head>
        <p>ONE SHALL BE TAKEN ----</p>
        <p>A Latchford farmer was told by the Mayor that one of his sons must join the Army. One of his sons_ bad already been exempted, and now the two others asked for exemption. There were forty cows to be looked after, and a lot of Nfork bad to be done.ovithe faym.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>PROTECTOR OF THE FAIR.</head>
        <p>PROTECTOR OF THE FAIR.</p>
        <p>A joiner said that• he lived .with his two sisters, who managed a pawnbroking shop, and the nature of the business was such that it was requisite that a man should be on the premises in the evenings.: The shop was in a very rough neighbourhood, he said, and You have some very peculiar characters to deal with." At those times it was necessary that there should be a man in the,hou,e. Claim disallowed.</p>
        <p>MOTHER, CHILDREN AND BUSINESS TO</p>
        <p>LOOK AFTER.</p>
        <p>watch and clock repairer had his case post- poned for three months, on the ground that he was the sole support of- his mother and two children, aged five and ten years respectively, and that if he -left the business 'would have to be closed dowm..- The applicant succeeded his. father in the business two years ago, when Hitt gentleman died.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>MASTER CARTER'S.GOOD SPI ni Ts.</head>
        <p>MASTER CARTER'S.GOOD SPI ni Ts.</p>
        <p>".I'm not afraid of going; in fact, l'm just the' man for it—and I'll be there," remarked a jovial-faced and burly master carter on hearing that his case could only be postponed until March 31st.. He was a. pleasant, frank in- dividual, who siiid- he waa in "a bit of a diffi- culty," as he was. the • main support of his father and Mother, who were both in ill-health. He owned a horse and cart, and made good money in the carrying trade. "I give my father £3 to £3 Ios. a. week, .and pay for the corn, and all that's over I. keep myself. I used to give him £2 a week, but, since the war, cartin's ris," he said, adding that if he went into the Army he would haue to 1` sell out." .There was also another horse,- and when . his father was well enough he took this out. • The Tribunal asked the man to get some- one else to work the horses, and the applicant replied that he would do his best.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>WIDOWED MOTHER PLEADS IN VAIN.</head>
        <p>WIDOWED MOTHER PLEADS IN VAIN.</p>
        <p>A workman of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd., declared that he was the main support of the home; and his widowed mother appeared with him tq support his claim in the concrete. She said she -had been a widow for 29 , years, and her son now paid her a week.. Lodging with them were her brother, who only paid 3s. a week, an .old man, who sprang a similar .sum, and a sister who gave her nothing, because "her health is bad." •</p>
        <p>The Mayor: How much does it cost to keep your son? The Mother: Over 10s.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: That means that you now make nearly Ds. out of him. Your Army allotment will be 12s. 6d. we expect, and, therefore, yon will be better off when he goes. Claim refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>SOLICITOR APPEARS FOR BREWER.</head>
        <p>SOLICITOR APPEARS FOR BREWER.</p>
        <p>Out of about. fifty appeals so far dealt with in public no solicitor had appeared. Now, how- ever, Mr. T. S. Steel had 4 client in the brewer for the firm of Messrs. 8.... Cunningham. He was not 'allowed to plead, but simply to draw the facts out of the applicant—a task he 'com- pleted Successfully. • It was stated that the applicant was a partner in the firm., His brother Peter, who joined th Army sortie months ago, was a, joint brewer with him. ,If ,he (applicant) went into the Army .it was impossible to get another brewer. 'Many thousands of pounds were involved in the busi- ness. Thirty-seven employes had joined . the Colours and no atterapt'had been made to pre- vent others joining; in fact, they were rather proud . that on Of them had gained a. D.C.M. and another had been mentioned in. dispatches. If applicant left the 'busineSS could not go on. If it had been possible to have found anyone else to take his place as brewer, he would have been quite willing to 'enlist. • The Mayor: Ho* miich holiday have you had since the war broke out?</p>
        <p>The Applicant: Not two consecutive working days.</p>
        <p>The applicant was exempted, on condition that he stayed at the same business and that his circumstances did not change in regard to his position in.the firm.</p>
        <p>TWO BROTHERS SERVING: NOW THREE.</p>
        <p>An employe of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., said he had two brothers serving in the South Lancs. Regiment and another had been rejected, and was never certain of having a post long because he could not lift weights. His father's health also was failing, and he could not earn wages regulaTly. He,(the appli- cant) was afraid that if he himself went into the Army the home would be broken up. He admitted that often his father earned 30s. per week and another brother Hs. • Application refused. "Are we all going to the Front and have no home when we comebaek?" he asked, after.the manner of a preacher, as he went out,4for he did not wait for his question to be answered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>ANOTHER FAMILY OF SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER FAMILY OF SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>"I'm not afraid of going, but I've had one brother killed and have got another one wounded; and I think some other families should go as well." So .summed up a casual labourer who works at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. He added that he never enjoyed good health, and had to be careful in his manner of living. His mother lived at Wigan, although he had tried to per- suade her to come to Warrington, she refusing owing to the fact that her husband had passed away in the mining town. He sent her Bs. to 10s. a week, and did not want her to lose that support. Application refused. He was told that an allotment could easily be made to his mother and, therestore, she would not suffer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>CHIEF CLERK TO GO.</head>
        <p>A CHIEF CLERK TO GO.</p>
        <p>The chief clerk and cashier at an insurance company's office stated that he had a brother now serving at the Front and another was at home wounded. He (the applicant) allowed his mother 10s. a week clear, and he wanted to be placed in the same group as a married man of his age. On the Tribunal being informed that the applicant's salary would be paid by his employers, the claim was disallowed.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETY'S SECRETARY PROVISIONALLY EXEMPTED.</p>
        <p>The general secretary of the Catholic Friendly Societies' Association, whose head office is in Egypt-street, Warrington, was granted an ex- emption for six months, the Mayor stating that the Tribunal hoped an effort would be made to replace him. Mr. w. H. Dixon, of Manchester, solicitor and trustee of the Association, said that the Executive Committee. considered it was abso;</p>
        <p>lutely- essential that the general secretary should be allowed to remain in charge. There were 106 branches of the Association scattered up and down the country, and many thousands of pounds were involved. In order to work the Society, it was essential that a man with a per. feet knowledge of the Workmen's Compen. action Act and the Insurance Act shouldi be engaged. The whole of the work involved was very complex. Some of the points which arose would almost puzzle the Lord Chancellor him- self, and, in addition, the general secretary had to be an accountant, •Advertisemente had been issued in order to get someone to replace him, but tliese had been considered very unsatis_ factory. It was most difficult to get a man qualified for the post to throw up a permanency fore temporary appointment. The Association had a turnover close upon £90,000 per year, and they asked for exemption on the grounds that the general secretary was doing national work, that they had done their best to. replace him, and that, if the exemption were granted, the Association would not ask for exeinption for any of their officials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>COAL DEALER'S LUCK.</head>
        <p>COAL DEALER'S LUCK.</p>
        <p>A yc.nng man, trading on his own account as a coal dealer, declared that if he hid 1,1 his business would be ruined. 'He was ass'..sted by his brother.. The Tribunal grante.l him a FrLro,sonal exemption so long .as hr emaincd in the business, and did not apply for an exemption for his brother.</p>
        <p>=</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
