      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>YESTERDAY'S SITTING. Leading Points From Typical Cases Heard.</head>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S SITTING. Leading Points From Typical Cases Heard.</p>
        <p>About • forty eases were heard yesterday, some of the applicants being, married. An application for exemption for their con- fidential clerk and manager was made by the firm of John Chorley, iron merchants. One of the two parties in the firm is a captain in the 2/4th 'South •Lancashire Regiment. The applicant's work was highly confidential and it had taken years to train him. • Exempted until May 31st, in the meantime an effort, to be made to replace him. The'owner of a commercial motor lurry, who frequently, used it for carrying for works en- gaged on munitions, was told that be would be allowed a month to find someone to drive for him. _ .</p>
        <p>A Warrington man who is. the sple pro- prietor of a restaurant in MancheAer, said that if .he went into the Army his business would have to be closed. He also partiall supported his mother and five children, send- ing them remittanc.i-s to America, where they resided. -</p>
        <p>Appeal referred to Manchester. The sole proprietor of two garages and sev- eral taxicab, and the employer of nine people, was provisionally exempted. He was married, and his brother, who used to help,him in his business, joined the motor transport section in 1914. Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for the appli- cant.</p>
        <p>Business and domestic. reasons formed the claim for exemption of a boot and shoe re- pairer, who had invested his savings in his business. His mother—a widow—and a sister were dependent on his earnings. Another brother also contributed to the home. He had been attested and rejected ,as medically unfit.</p>
        <p>The application was adjourned for a fort- night. A metal worker, who was represented by Mr. T. S. Steel, solicitor, in business on his own, claimed that he was engaged on subsidiary munition work for several local _factories. His father, who was a widower, could not work, as ,he was an invalid. Three of his brothers were in the Army, and another had also been attested and rejected. He had suffered through a shortage of labour. For six years he had been in business. Provisional exemption was granted. _ .</p>
        <p>A time-keeper at a local -wire works ap- pealed, being the partial support of his young brother in business in ;Liverpool, there being no father or mother. His sister, in the Blue- coat School, came out in September. Appli- cant's only other brother was in the Army, but was not allowing anything towards the main- tenance of the children 'at home.—The applica- tion .was refused. A conimercial traveller, with a brother al- ready serving, appealed on the ground that he was the support of his mother, aged 63. He has a brother in the Army, who made an allotment of. 12s. 6d. a week. . She had a ',Carder who Paid 18. s. and he allowed 255. a week. His mother had .{3OO in Corporation stock. He had attested and. was passed for home service . only, 'having varicose veins in both. legs.—Disallowed. • A cooper. whose application was rejected, asked whether 'he could appeal against the decision, and was told that lie could do so within. three days on a form that would be supplied. . In the' case of a painter employed by a local gas stove works, the only support of . his mother, an adjournment for ten days for in- quiries was ordered. A farmer in partnership with his brother- in-law in Folly-lane; said he worked on the land and milked fifteen cows.' They had 40 acres under the plough. They had been on' the :farm for three years and had gradually worked it up. • They could• not get anyone to do their work.—Provisionally •exempted. The claim of Mr. W. T. Williams, draper and niilliner, of Sankey-street, for the exemption 'of' his only male assistant, was allowed. The assistant alsolas two brothers in the Army. Provisional exemption was given to two .em- ployes of dentists who are partly engaged in 'attending to the needs of soldiers. On the application of Mr. T. S. Steel, the head bookkeeper of Messrs. Edwin Richardson and Sons, grocers, Bridge-streets was allowed' a poettnonement fill May 31. It was stated that the firm' had replaced.: eight of their thirty em- ployes by female labour, seven were over mili- tary age, and 14 had attested and one rejected. Peter .McLachlan, builder, Manchester-road, applied on behalf of his joiners' shop foreman. He stated that most of .his building trade was connected with war. work. Considering the 'state of the labour. market it would be very .difficult tO replace tim.—Conditional exemp- tion was granted. Short of Stature, But Fit.</p>
        <p>An only son in business with his mother as a fishmonger based an appeal on domestic grounds. If he were taken his mother, he stated, would be left destitute. Applicant said he was not five feet in height, and the question arose whether he was fit for the Army. He was then taken by one of the military representatives to the Drill Hall, where he was measured and weighed, with the result that Capt. Mathanas was of the opinion that he probably would be fit for ser- vice.</p>
        <p>The Tribunal postponed the ease for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>LYMM TRIBUNAL</head>
        <p>LYMM TRIBUNAL</p>
        <p>A Farmer's Difficulty In Obtaining Labour.</p>
        <p>WOULD RATHER LOSE HIS TEETH.</p>
        <p>The first case heard by the Lymm Tribunal last night was that of a bank clerk, who claimed exemption on domestic grounds. Requesting that he should be heard in private, he said, "I would rather have my front teeth out than come here." His desire for privacy was granted, and afterwards "The Examiner" representative was informed that he had been exempted until April 14th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>SON, INDISPENSABLE TO FARMER.</head>
        <p>SON, INDISPENSABLE TO FARMER.</p>
        <p>A farmer appealed on , behalf of his son, stating that he had 56 statute acres to look after. He had six horses and 11 milking cattle. His son attended to the ,horses and did the plough- ing. He had 2.2 acres under the plough. It was impossible to get anyone else to do the work, and if he went into the Army, he (the father) would have to give Up farming, for he suffered from a disability, which at times forbade physical strain. For fourteen months he had had no one but his son to assist him on the farm as it was impossible to obtain other labour. Exemption until October Ist. Those were the only case,s- heard.</p>
        <p>The Tribunal consisted of Messrs. T. J. Ridg- way; W. G. Groves, R. H. Gotobed, J. IT. Holt S. W. Gould, W., Seymour, F. W. Druce. with Captain Hawkins as military representative, and Mr. W. Mullarcl as clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>INSURANCE OF VOMEN FARM 'WORKERS.</head>
        <p>INSURANCE OF VOMEN FARM 'WORKERS.</p>
        <p>• Addressing the Somerset War Agricultural Committee, Sir Sydney Olivier (Secretary of the Board of Agriculture) said it was perfectly clear that women employed on farms would have to be insured. The committee decided, however, to approach the Government to endeavour to secure exemption , for temporarily employed ; women from the provisions of the National Insurance Act. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>(.1I ‘MBERS OF COMMERCE.</head>
        <p>(.1I ‘MBERS OF COMMERCE.</p>
        <p>The Chambers of Commerce, meeting in Lon- don, considered a number of resolutions on trading after the war. It placed on record its conviction, from war experiencei that the strength and safety of the Empire lie in its ability to produce what it requires as largely as is possible from its own soil and factories. A resolution was carried by a very large majority advocating reciprocal trading relations between all parts of the British Empire, reciprocal trad- ing between the Empire and Allied countries, favourable treatment of neutrals, and restric- tions by tariffs or otherwise of trade with enemy countries. Manchester was not represented at the Conference.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>MATINEE Al ROYAL COURT.</head>
        <p>MATINEE Al ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>Military And Civilians Unite Jo Entertain For Philanthropy.</p>
        <p>The appeal of the afternoon entertainment in the Royal Court Theatre on Thursday, was re- sponded to by an audience occupying every available seat in the building. Organised by a sub-committee comprising Mrs. O. Wheeler, ,Mrs. R. Garnett, and Mr. H. L. Warburton, the enterprise of a matinee fur the raising of funds, to enable the Red Cross Society's working parties to make comforts and provide hospital necessities for our sick and wounded soldiers, was, with the kind 'co-operation of Sir Joseph Beecham, ably and efficiently carried out, the substantial sum Of .270 being realised for this Laudable purpose. Miss Gladys Adams, Mr. J, Babbitt and Mr.. M. Ashton made some interesting contribu- tions to a. miscellaneous programme, in which a number of pieces, played by the Band of the 2nd South .Laneashire Regiment, with the kind permission of Colonel Moggridge, bulked largely. Then, in addition, there. was the appearance on the stage of two capable artistes from the Hip- podrome—Lieut. Gordon ihe_ventriloquist, and Miss Ruth Bucknall; the sopinno vocalist—with with whose names Should be associated those of Mr. F. Afacnaghten and genial manager. Mr. Will A. Jackson. Finally, some members of the Liverpool Repertory Commonwealth Company— including our histrionically-gifted townsman, Mr. Harvey Adams—performed in good style a comedy playlet entitled ".The Rest Cure," greatly to the delight of an audience already surfeited with good things. •</p>
        <p>A .Band: of Parts?</p>
        <p>Of the band pieces, the three "Henry VIII. Dances" were probably the most interesting, as well as• the most appreciated, though all the music was discoursed' with that notable skill end good taste which one has come to associate with the playing of the instrumentalists comprising the Band of' the 2nd South Lancashire. The two songs, "Twilight Love" and "I'm Having a Right Good Time "—both old favourites in the. late George Edwardes's pro- ductions—brought into prominence a degree of versatility and resourcefulness on the part of Miss Adams at once admirable and unsuspected. With a piquant gracefulness and a charming naivete, she sang and acted these vocal moreeaux ins, voice which, if small in volume, is entirely sweet, and with an enunciation singularly clear, the result being that she quite captivated all who saw and heard her. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. H. M. Ashton's drolleries in the guise of "Mrs. Murphy," familiar enough though they are on such occasions, were yet wholly welcome, and that they proved hugely •diverting was testified by the good humour in which they nut all who came under their influence. Of Mr. Babbitt's contributions—" Sound an alarm," "An English Rose," and "Mother- land"—ono-need say no more" than that these songs were rendered by him with that degree of verve and in that thorough-going style which has won for him a position of high and wide- spread esteem, which might well be the envy of all amateur concert singers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>Music Hall to Theatre.</head>
        <p>Music Hall to Theatre.</p>
        <p>The Hippodrome vocalist, Miss Bucknall, sang "I Hear You Calling Me" in a :'high soprano voice of great purity and sweetness and in a manner notably devoid• of what may be called the music-hall bad habits. As a ventri- loquist, Lieutenant Gordon had some points which were both new and attractive, his. Scotch pronunciation and dress combining with much of bis "patter" to bold the audience's atten- tion as well as to amuse it.</p>
        <p>The pianoforte accompaniments to the songs were well played by Miss Clarice Newall and Miss Lily Rabbitt, and an augmented theatre orchestra was capably handled by Mr. IL Halley. and Mr. Millington: in turn. During an interval in the programme Colonel Moggridge briefly thanked all concerned in the carrying through of this matinee enterprise for their most valuable help and co-operation, and the audience for the heart response made,. in point of the numbers present, to its appeal for public support.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>11ILDREN AND SHAKI:‘,PI:</head>
        <p>11ILDREN AND SHAKI:‘,PI:</p>
        <p>It has been decided to make the '3rd of May a special day. for schools and colleges,-. so that. the children of the Empire can feel :that they have some special part- in this universal homage. to Shakespeare.. The children will welcome with. enthusiasm the opportunity of doing_ homage to Shakespeare by wearing, a tercentenary medal- lion, realising that their pennies will go to alleviate the suffering of our gallant sailors and. soldiers; and our brave Allies, the funds raised being devoted to the British Red Cross Society, the National Committee for Relief -in Belgium, and the Leagne of Mercy. In order that children in the very poorest districts inay not be debarred from taking part in this national tribute, donations are asked from those in sympathy with this festival of mercy. Such donations will be earmarked for the special purpose of providing medallion.. for those unable to purchase them. •. . Donation.g. should be sent to .the Lord Mayor. of Lond—on,</p>
        <p>_ Printed and published by John Walker and Co. (Warrington), Limited, at the Expoivnig General Printing Works, Bevrsey-street, War- rington; also published by them Id the EXAMINER Offices, 2, Victoria-road, Widnes; 32, George-street, gt. Helens; 3. Market-street. Earlestown, all in. County •of Lancaster and at Church-street, Runcorn, in the Count, of Cheater.</p>
      </div>
