        <p>40 40 40 40 04 40 4. 40 40 40 40 4441040 40 40 4:4 40 40 40 44 4. •••• t, 04 00 4:4 0:* 4,:* 40 10 J t • 4:9 40 0:0 40 THE FO TBALL FIELD • • RECORDS AND COMMENTS AMONO THE LOCALS; • • ••••••••••••••• • • • • • • • • • • • • • • • ••••••• • • • • • • • almost unsatisfactory game, but always interest- ing. Snow fell frequently, and always a cutting wind pursued its discomfiting course. Feeling between the teams overflowed, and there were countless free kicks, mostly, I am happy to say, for Warrington. The ground was sloppy, and the players had difficulty in keeping their feet. Eccles balanced themselves better than our fel- lows; they were real Blondins. One cannot complain of lack of vigour in the Town, for they immediately attacked through Ditchfield, and Aspinall had a long shot, but Foot, the custodian, easily collared it, and in no time' Eccles were pounding away. Kite was called upon—and he answered the call. Cook- son, at left-back, was playing very_ nicely and strongly, as well as versatilely,. and. he had an excellent supporter irijieorge Whitfield, who frequently proved his resourcefulness. Once, early on, Wilde and A. Walker were. bearing down on him in a conquering style, so, instead of trying to get past them, George back-heeled , the ball to Cookson and away 'it. went, in full. flight to Ditchfield. The little right-winger; however, was robbed of it before he could pro- ceed far, but Leah tackled superbly and re- lieved the situation. This episode was really first-class. The half-backs showed up excellently. Bul- len, who has happily, recovered from his injury, was perhaps not quite up to his usual form, but he watched a rather big area •well. He should be tip-top by now. He lacked speed somewhat, but somehow or other he anticipated the tac- tics of his opponents, and, by short cuts, .stemmed the tide frequently. Broster was not nearly so-busy-in-the first half, though he had a deal to do in the second. He, too, lacks espeed per se, but his weight tells admirably. Once I thought a movement he pivoted would be fruitful. On receiving from Cookson he raced the ball along, and as his opponents ap- proached he placed it beautifully over to Aspinall; who. had to risk; a rather long, shut; which,' unfortunately, went a foot wide of the post. Still, this was encouraging, and the boys put plenty of spirit into the work. • An opportunity was missed through. Ditchteld going too far with the ball, for, when he centred, Aspinall could not reach it in time to prevent Foot pro- curing it. But they would not be denied with- out an effort, and pressure continued for a time, the climax coming when Foster shot high over th'e bar. A rare display on the right wing resulted in Toni Whitfield sending in a fast shot. It curved beautifully, and Foot had to alter his position swiftly, but he was in time. Many a goalkeeper would have erred; In the first half both teams shared the honours in quality of play. Also in goals—but there were none. In the second half, however, Eccles doubled the number of the Town attacks. They seemed to have a better grasp of the game, at times combining really excellently. Early on a corner to them looked ,like turning out</p>
        <p>BEAMONT JUNIORS R.F.C. Intermediate League Champions. Left to right:— Back Row.—R. Bibby, W. Ellis, A. Hackett, C. Baguley, G. Armitage, F. Johnson, J. Ander- son, T. Higgins. Middle Row.—T. Wycherley, G. Taker, W. Smith (captain), J. Higgins, A. Mason. Bottom Row.—D. Sinclair, J. Coleman. A FINE RECORD. Beamont Juniors have played 16 matches, won 13, lost 2, and drawn 1. Points scored For: Tries, 26; goals, 19. Against: Tries, 14; goals, 7. Their biggest score was 29 points tc, none, against Orford Juniors. They have one of the</p>
        <p>finest goal-kickers in Warrington, in D. Armi- tage, who has kicked several goals from the half-way line. This player kicked eleven of the nineteen goals scored. Of the 26 tries, E. Makin scored 8 and W. Smith 5.</p>
