        <p>Bewsey players • left the field the home team were leading by five goalsto,none. There should be an interesting game to-day at Padgate-lane in the return fixture between the Fairfield Reserves and the St. Elphin?s, the latter being -at ther head of the League table. The Reserves will be represented by their strongest eleven, and a strong effort is to be made to reverse the results of the first game between these teams. Woodhouse and Watson are expected to turn out. A very keen game should be witnessed on the Arpley ground between the Orford Juniors :sal Howley United. Since the second half,,of the season was entered upon both teams have been doing well, and for to-day's fixture the strongest teams have been selected., The following will appear for the Orford Juniors: J. McDougall; J. Gibson, W. Doughty; C. Povey, S. Austin, E. Gleave; E. Belshaws J. Clare, J. Hirst, E. Mor- gan and T. Griffiths. The Mersey Juniors are expected to travel to the Penketh Recreation Ground, and-if.the,boine team maintain their recent form they should not have much difficulty in gaining both points. TO-DAY'S MATCHES. Fairfield United Reserves v. St. Elphin's. Howley United v. Orford Juniors. Penketh Juniors v. Mersey Juniors. Goals P. W. L. D. F. A. P. St. Elphin's Howley United Penketh Juniors Orford juniors Fairfield U. Res 9 4 4 1 25 15 9 Stockton 11. Scouts ll 4 G 1 17 30 9 Bewsey A.F.C. Mersey Juniors WARRINGTON TOWN F.C. Mr. Peacock And The Daintith Incident. To the Editor of " The Examiner." Sir,--Mr. Bradshaw's letter in your last week's issue, replying to mine of the 1903 ult., con tamed a distinct note of jealousy towards the Warrington Town Club. 1 have known ever Aimee the formation of the Town Club he has had a longing desire to" strafe" somebody or something connected with the club. Of course, I must congratulate. him in "sticking to his guns," but I cannot regard his reply as the clearest possible "way out." Mr. Bradshaw at the onset states that Daintith was induced to play for the Town by sharp practice. However, Mr. Bradshaw can make such a statement passes comprehension, for the player under notice played for the Town Club of his own free will, expressing a desire to play, feeling the inconvenience of travelling to Runcorn. The Warrington Town Club are, I may say: too honourable to resort to those methods which Mr. Biladshaw implies they are capable of. Daintith was not informed that I had seen Mr. Bradshaw or the Warrington Committee on the matter of playing him, and Daintith has now sent me a letter apologising for the misunder- standing. I said I had seen the Secretary of the Lancashire Combination, not the Secretary of Warrington Rugby Club The' Warrington Town club is a young organisation, and desirous of encouraging young men chiefly engaged on "works of munition" to keep in condition so that when called upon to do more serious duty for the nation, they will not feel the stress of training which wout-.: naturally accrue after a state of lethargy Relative to the granting of permits, it is well known that no club has a hold on any player, and only last meek a perusal of the papers showed where several Northern Union players are engaged in the Rugby matches in the Scrrs of England. In fact; there are no rules binding any player during the present crisis, amateur or professional, and as regards Daintith being hurt, well a true state of affairs from Mr. Bradshaw would have served his ease to a better purpose. Is the success of the Association Club the sore point .with Mr. Bradshaw? We know he was once connected with the soccer code in War- rington, , which I might say was an inglorious failure,' and surely' he ought to be the last per- son in the world to deprive the Town Club of that little support which we claim to be but our just due. Yours faithfully, P. PEACOCK, JIM.</p>
